/* AUTO-GENERATED by tools/build-review.js — do not edit by hand. */
window.GM_REVIEW = [{"id":"g-gpu","front":"GPU","back":"A processor with thousands of simple cores built for throughput — running the same operation across huge amounts of data in parallel — rather than the low-latency single-thread speed a CPU optimizes for.","url":"module-4/lesson-01.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-cuda","front":"CUDA","back":"NVIDIA's programming model and toolkit for general-purpose GPU computing: you write kernels in C++-like code and launch grids of threads. The concepts (not the vendor syntax) transfer to every GPU ecosystem.","url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-kernel","front":"kernel","back":"A function that runs on the GPU, executed in parallel by many threads. You launch it with a grid/block configuration; each thread computes its own piece using its index.","url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-thread","front":"thread","back":"The smallest unit of execution on a GPU. Thousands run concurrently; each computes its global index and processes one element (or a strided set) of the data.","url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-warp","front":"warp","back":"A group of 32 threads (NVIDIA; 64 on AMD, called a wavefront) that execute in lockstep (SIMT) — one instruction across all lanes at once. The true unit of scheduling and memory access.","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-wavefront","front":"wavefront","back":"AMD's name for a warp — but 64 lanes wide, not 32. The single most important difference when porting CUDA to AMD (a hard-coded '32' silently processes half the lanes).","url":"track-d/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-block","front":"block","back":"A group of threads that run on one SM, share fast on-chip shared memory, and can synchronize with a barrier. The unit you tile a problem into (5.5).","url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-grid","front":"grid","back":"The full set of blocks launched for one kernel. Blocks in a grid are independent and may run in any order, on any SM, concurrently — a hardware contract you must not violate.","url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-sm","front":"SM","back":"A Streaming Multiprocessor — the GPU's core execution unit (AMD calls it a Compute Unit). It runs many warps, has a register file, shared memory, and schedulers. A GPU has dozens of SMs.","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-occupancy","front":"occupancy","back":"The ratio of active warps on an SM to the hardware maximum. Set by the tightest of three budgets — registers, shared memory, and block/thread slots. A means to hide latency, not an end in itself.","url":"module-5/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-shared-memory","front":"shared memory","back":"Fast, on-chip, per-block scratchpad memory (AMD: LDS). Software-managed cache used to stage data for reuse (tiling), ~100× faster than global memory but tiny (tens of KB/SM).","url":"module-5/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-register","front":"register","back":"The fastest storage, private to each thread. Plentiful but finite per SM — high register use per thread lowers occupancy, the classic 5.6 trade.","url":"module-5/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-coalescing","front":"coalescing","back":"When the 32 threads of a warp access consecutive memory addresses at one instant, the hardware merges them into a few 32-byte sector transactions with no waste. Scattered access wastes bandwidth up to the sector floor (8× for fp32).","url":"module-5/lesson-07.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-sector","front":"sector","back":"The 32-byte unit the memory system moves. A warp's addresses are served in sectors; scattered access touches many sectors for little useful data — the traffic 'tax' coalescing avoids.","url":"module-5/lesson-07.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-bandwidth","front":"bandwidth","back":"Bytes per second the memory system can deliver (e.g., ~220 GB/s achievable on a T4). The binding limit for most GPU kernels, which move more bytes than they do math.","url":"module-4/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-arithmetic-intensity","front":"arithmetic intensity","back":"FLOPs performed per byte moved from memory. Low intensity = memory-bound; high = compute-bound. The x-axis of the roofline that decides which ceiling limits you.","url":"module-4/lesson-07.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-roofline","front":"roofline","back":"A log-log plot: performance is capped by min(peak FLOP/s, arithmetic-intensity × bandwidth). The ridge point separates memory-bound (left) from compute-bound (right). 'The gap to the roof is your to-do list.'","url":"module-4/lesson-07.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-latency","front":"latency","back":"The time for one operation to complete (e.g., ~hundreds of cycles for a global-memory read). GPUs hide latency with many resident warps rather than reducing it (Little's law).","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-throughput","front":"throughput","back":"Total work completed per unit time. GPUs optimize throughput (lots of work in flight) over the latency of any single operation — the opposite of a CPU's priority.","url":"module-4/lesson-01.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-simt","front":"SIMT","back":"Single-Instruction, Multiple-Thread: a warp's lanes share one instruction stream. When lanes take different branches (divergence), the paths run serially, wasting lanes.","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-divergence","front":"divergence","back":"When threads in a warp take different branches of an if/else, the warp executes both paths serially with lanes masked off — losing parallelism. Structure code so a warp mostly agrees.","url":"module-5/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-reduction","front":"reduction","back":"Combining many values into one (sum, max…) in parallel via a tree: pairs combine each step, halving the count, so N values reduce in log₂N steps. The canonical parallel primitive.","url":"module-5/lesson-08.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-atomic","front":"atomic","back":"A read-modify-write that completes without interruption from other threads. Correct for concurrent updates but serializes contending threads — privatize-then-merge to avoid the bottleneck.","url":"module-5/lesson-08.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-barrier","front":"barrier","back":"A synchronization point where all threads in a block wait until every thread arrives — required before reading data another thread wrote to shared memory. Must be reached by all threads (never inside a divergent branch).","url":"module-5/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-stream","front":"stream","back":"An ordered queue of GPU work. Independent streams overlap (copy while computing), keeping the GPU busy — the async model behind hiding transfer costs (5.9).","url":"module-5/lesson-09.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-tiling","front":"tiling","back":"Loading a chunk (tile) of data into shared memory once and reusing it across many threads, cutting global-memory traffic. The core optimization behind fast matmul and stencils (5.5).","url":"module-5/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-kernel-fusion","front":"kernel fusion","back":"Merging several operations into one kernel so intermediate results stay in registers/shared memory instead of round-tripping through global memory — often the biggest ML speedup (skip the memory trip).","url":"track-a/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-tensor-core","front":"tensor core","back":"Fixed-function hardware that computes small matrix multiply-accumulates in one operation, at many times the FLOP rate of general cores — the engine behind fast fp16/bf16/fp8 training and inference.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-mixed-precision","front":"mixed precision","back":"Using lower-precision formats (fp16/bf16/fp8) for speed and memory, while accumulating in fp32 for accuracy. Requires loss scaling (fp16) to avoid gradient underflow.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-fp32","front":"fp32","back":"32-bit float: 1 sign, 8 exponent, 23 mantissa bits. The default precision — wide range and ~7 decimal digits. Baseline for correctness comparisons.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-bf16","front":"bf16","back":"16-bit float with fp32's 8 exponent bits (same range, no overflow) but only 7 mantissa bits (less precision). The training default because it drops in for fp32 without loss scaling.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-fp16","front":"fp16","back":"16-bit float: 5 exponent, 10 mantissa bits. More precision than bf16 but narrow range (overflows ~65504), so training needs loss scaling.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-fp8","front":"fp8","back":"8-bit float in two flavors: e4m3 (more precision, saturates ~448) and e5m2 (more range, has infinity). Hopper/Blackwell inference and training precision.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-floating-point","front":"floating point","back":"Numbers stored as sign × mantissa × 2^exponent. Addition is non-associative — order changes the result — so parallel reductions differ from serial sums (correct within a budget, not bitwise).","url":"module-1/lesson-08.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-ptx","front":"PTX","back":"NVIDIA's portable intermediate assembly. nvcc compiles CUDA to PTX, which the driver JIT-compiles to the actual machine code (SASS) for the specific GPU — forward compatibility.","url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-sass","front":"SASS","back":"The real, architecture-specific machine instructions a NVIDIA GPU executes, generated from PTX. What a profiler's assembly view shows.","url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-nvcc","front":"nvcc","back":"NVIDIA's CUDA compiler. It splits a .cu file into host (CPU) code for your C++ compiler and device (GPU) code compiled to PTX/SASS, then stitches them into one binary.","url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-hip","front":"HIP","back":"AMD's CUDA-like C++ runtime and kernel language. Porting CUDA to HIP is mostly renaming API calls (cuda→hip); the kernel body is byte-identical — except warp-size-32 assumptions (AMD is 64).","url":"track-d/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-sycl","front":"SYCL","back":"A single-source C++ standard (Intel oneAPI, AdaptiveCpp) that compiles one file to any backend. Accessors declare data intent and the runtime infers the dependency schedule for you.","url":"track-d/lesson-03.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-webgpu","front":"WebGPU","back":"The modern browser GPU API (and its shading language WGSL). A real compute API — the same warps/tiles/barriers as CUDA, in every browser, zero install. The universal GPU lab.","url":"track-d/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-spir-v","front":"SPIR-V","back":"A portable intermediate representation for GPU code. The compile-once-run-anywhere layer under Vulkan shaders, SYCL, and OpenCL — the same idea as PTX but vendor-neutral.","url":"track-c/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-triton","front":"Triton","back":"A Python DSL for writing GPU kernels at the tile level. Its compiler lowers one kernel through MLIR to PTX or AMDGCN and autotunes per architecture — the compiler does the porting and tuning.","url":"track-a/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-kokkos","front":"Kokkos","back":"A C++ performance-portability layer: Views make memory layout a compile-time parameter (coalesced on GPU, cache-friendly on CPU) and one parallel_for compiles to any backend. What national labs bet on.","url":"track-d/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-bvh","front":"BVH","back":"A tree of nested bounding boxes that turns a ray's search from O(N) triangles into ~O(log N) box tests by skipping whole subtrees it misses — what makes real-time ray tracing feasible.","url":"track-c/lesson-09.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-pbr","front":"PBR","back":"Physically Based Rendering: lighting that conserves energy (never reflects more than it receives) via a microfacet BRDF (GGX/Smith/Schlick). Materials authored once look correct under any lighting.","url":"track-c/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-rasterization","front":"rasterization","back":"Converting triangles into fragments (candidate pixels). Fragments shade in 2×2 quads (for derivatives), which is why sub-pixel triangles waste most of their fragment work.","url":"track-c/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-barycentric","front":"barycentric","back":"Weights (α, β, γ summing to 1) expressing a point inside a triangle as a blend of its three vertices. How the rasterizer interpolates attributes — and what a ray-triangle test also returns.","url":"track-c/lesson-02.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-mipmap","front":"mipmap","back":"A chain of prefiltered half-resolution copies of a texture. The hardware picks the level (via the quad's derivatives) where ~1 texel maps to 1 pixel, killing minification aliasing for +1/3 memory.","url":"track-c/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-shader","front":"shader","back":"A program that runs on the GPU within a graphics/compute pipeline. A compute shader is a general GPU kernel — workgroup = block, shared = __shared__, barrier = __syncthreads: the core model, renamed.","url":"track-c/lesson-07.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-kv-cache","front":"KV-cache","back":"Cached key/value tensors from previous tokens so an LLM doesn't recompute them each step. Its size (layers × heads × dim × tokens × 2 × bytes) dominates inference memory; decode is memory-bound reading it.","url":"track-a/lesson-08.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-flashattention","front":"FlashAttention","back":"An attention algorithm that tiles the computation so the N×N scores never touch HBM (O(N²)→O(N) memory), using the online-softmax trick — tiling (5.5) applied to attention.","url":"track-a/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-hbm","front":"HBM","back":"High-Bandwidth Memory — the stacked DRAM that gives data-center GPUs their enormous bandwidth (TB/s). The 'global memory' kernels read from; the resource the roofline's diagonal measures.","url":"module-4/lesson-05.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-pcie","front":"PCIe","back":"The bus connecting CPU and GPU (~11–12 GB/s pinned on gen3, ~64 GB/s gen5) — far slower than on-GPU bandwidth, so minimizing host↔device transfers (and overlapping them) is critical (4.6).","url":"module-4/lesson-06.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"g-little-s-law","front":"Little's law","back":"Concurrency = latency × throughput. To saturate memory bandwidth you need enough in-flight requests (warps × bytes-per-thread) to cover the latency — why occupancy and vectorized loads matter.","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"Glossary","tag":"term"},{"id":"q-1","front":"What does the program counter (PC) hold?","back":"The memory address of the next instruction to fetch — The PC is the processor's bookmark — the address of the next instruction. Loops and branches work by writing a new address into it, which is why \"jump\" is all you need to build if and for .","url":"module-0/lesson-01.html","title":"0.1 What a Computer Actually Does — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-2","front":"On a modern machine, roughly how do the costs of \"add two numbers already in the core\" and \"fetch a number from main memory\" compare?","back":"The fetch is on the order of ~100× slower than the add — Arithmetic is well under a nanosecond; a main-memory fetch is on the order of ~100 ns. This imbalance — cheap math, expensive data movement — is the reason both caches (CPU) and massive multithreading (GPU) exist.","url":"module-0/lesson-01.html","title":"0.1 What a Computer Actually Does — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-3","front":"What does it mean that a CPU is optimized for \"latency\"?","back":"It's built to finish a single task in as little time as possible — Latency is time-to-finish-one-task. The CPU's caches, branch predictors, and out-of-order machinery all exist to race a single instruction stream forward without stalling. \"Most tasks per second regardless of individual speed\" describes throughput — the GPU's goal, next lesson.","url":"module-0/lesson-02.html","title":"0.2 The CPU — Built for Latency — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-4","front":"Most of a CPU core's silicon area is spent on…","back":"control logic and caches — the machinery that avoids waiting — A CPU spends the bulk of its transistors and energy on caches, branch prediction, and scheduling — everything needed to keep one stream from stalling. Only a small fraction is arithmetic. The GPU flips this budget, spending most of its area on arithmetic units.","url":"module-0/lesson-02.html","title":"0.2 The CPU — Built for Latency — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-5","front":"A single GPU core is generally slower than a single CPU core. How does a GPU still win overall on the right problems?","back":"It runs thousands of cores at once and hides memory stalls behind other work — huge throughput despite slow individual cores — A GPU trades single-thread speed for massive parallelism. Thousands of simple cores plus latency hiding (switch to another warp when one stalls) give enormous throughput even though each core is slower and each task has higher latency than on a CPU.","url":"module-0/lesson-03.html","title":"0.3 The GPU — Built for Throughput — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-6","front":"What is a \"warp\" (NVIDIA terminology)?","back":"A group of 32 threads that execute the same instruction in lockstep, each on its own data — A warp is 32 threads driven by one instruction stream in lockstep (SIMT). One fetch/decode feeds 32 arithmetic units. (AMD's equivalent, the wavefront, was historically 64 threads.) This sharing is how the GPU affords so many cores.","url":"module-0/lesson-03.html","title":"0.3 The GPU — Built for Throughput — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-7","front":"A program is 80% parallelizable. What is the maximum possible speedup from moving the parallel part to an infinitely fast GPU?","back":"5× — the serial 20% caps it at 1 ÷ 0.20 — Amdahl's law: with serial fraction s = 0.20, the ceiling is 1/s = 1/0.20 = 5×, even if the parallel 80% takes zero time. The serial part you can't parallelize is what limits you — which is why shrinking it matters as much as speeding up the rest.","url":"module-0/lesson-04.html","title":"0.4 CPU vs GPU — The Trade-off — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-8","front":"In the host–device model, which statement is correct?","back":"The CPU runs the program and dispatches parallel work and data to the GPU across a relatively slow bridge — The CPU (host) is the manager: it runs the program, handles serial parts, and copies data/work to the GPU (device) over PCIe or NVLink. Their memories are separate, and that bridge is much slower than either chip's own memory — so minimizing crossings is a core skill.","url":"module-0/lesson-04.html","title":"0.4 CPU vs GPU — The Trade-off — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-9","front":"What was \"GPGPU,\" and why did it matter historically?","back":"Doing general-purpose math on a GPU by disguising it as graphics — it proved GPUs could vastly outperform CPUs on the right problems, before CUDA made it easy — Before CUDA (2006), researchers coaxed GPUs into general computation by encoding data as textures and computation as pixel shaders — General-Purpose GPU computing. It was awkward but demonstrated the GPU's power for non-graphics work, which motivated CUDA and everything after.","url":"module-0/lesson-05.html","title":"0.5 How the GPU Evolved — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-10","front":"Why were GPUs, rather than CPUs, the hardware that powered the deep-learning revolution?","back":"Neural networks are mostly huge matrix multiplications — millions of independent, identical multiply-adds — which is exactly the throughput workload GPUs excel at — A neural network is, underneath, enormous matrix multiplications: millions to billions of independent, identical multiply-add operations. That's the ideal GPU workload (independent + uniform + arithmetic-heavy). AlexNet (2012) made this undeniable, and Tensor Cores (2017) then hardwired the matrix operation into the silicon.","url":"module-0/lesson-05.html","title":"0.5 How the GPU Evolved — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-11","front":"Why does NVIDIA lead the GPU-computing market so decisively?","back":"As much for CUDA's mature software ecosystem (libraries, tools, adoption) as for the hardware itself — NVIDIA makes excellent hardware, but its durable advantage is CUDA's ecosystem — a decade-plus of libraries, tools, and near-universal adoption that competitors must overcome even with strong chips. CUDA is NVIDIA-only, which is exactly why portable frameworks (ROCm/HIP, oneAPI/SYCL, WebGPU) and Track D exist.","url":"module-0/lesson-06.html","title":"0.6 The GPU Landscape & Your Roadmap — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-12","front":"When choosing a GPU for a large AI model, what should you check first ?","back":"Memory capacity — whether the model even fits in the GPU's memory — If the model doesn't fit in GPU memory, nothing else matters (short of splitting it across multiple GPUs). After capacity, bandwidth and compute-at-your-precision decide speed, and cost/power decide practicality — but capacity is the first gate. Clock speed, from Lesson 0.1, is nearly irrelevant for choosing between GPUs.","url":"module-0/lesson-06.html","title":"0.6 The GPU Landscape & Your Roadmap — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-13","front":"What is 0x2F in decimal?","back":"47 — 2×16 + 15 — Hex is base 16: 0x2F = 2×16¹ + F(=15)×16⁰ = 32 + 15 = 47. Reading hex digits as if they were decimal (29) is the classic first-week mistake.","url":"module-1/lesson-01.html","title":"1.1 Numbers, Bases & Binary — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-14","front":"An int32_t holds 2 147 483 647 (its maximum) and your CUDA kernel adds 1 to it. What does the C/CUDA standard say happens?","back":"Undefined behavior — the compiler may assume it never happens and optimize accordingly — Signed overflow is UB. On most hardware you'll *observe* the wrap to −2 147 483 648, but the compiler is allowed to assume it can't occur — which can delete your bounds checks. Unsigned types wrap by definition; signed types do not promise anything.","url":"module-1/lesson-01.html","title":"1.1 Numbers, Bases & Binary — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-15","front":"In C/CUDA, with int i = 11551, W = 1920; what is i % 3 and what does it recover in the RGB layout i = (row·W+col)·3 + c ?","back":"1 — the channel c (green) — % undoes the last operation applied (·3 + c): 11551 = 3850·3 + 1, so i % 3 = 1 = c. Inversion always unwraps in reverse order of construction.","url":"module-1/lesson-02.html","title":"1.2 Algebra as Computation — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-16","front":"You need enough blocks of 256 threads to cover N = 1000 elements. Which expression is correct and why?","back":"(N + 255) / 256 — rounds up exactly, including when 256 divides N — 1000/256 = 3 (truncated) misses 232 elements; +1 gives a spurious extra block when N is an exact multiple; ceil(N/256) on ints truncates BEFORE ceil sees it — the classic trap. (N+B−1)/B is exact in all cases.","url":"module-1/lesson-02.html","title":"1.2 Algebra as Computation — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-17","front":"Without a calculator: 2²⁰ × 2¹² / 2² = ?","back":"2³⁰ ≈ 10⁹ — add 20+12, subtract 2 — Multiplying same-base powers adds exponents; dividing subtracts: 20+12−2 = 30, and 2³⁰ ≈ 10⁹ via the 2¹⁰≈10³ bridge. Multiplying exponents is the rule for (aᵐ)ⁿ — a different situation.","url":"module-1/lesson-03.html","title":"1.3 Exponents, Logs & Orders of Magnitude — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-18","front":"A parallel reduction halves the number of active values each round. For 16 million (≈2²⁴) values, how many rounds until one value remains — and what growth class is that?","back":"24 rounds — O(log n) — Each round is one halving; halvings to 1 = log₂(2²⁴) = 24. This is why GPUs sum a billion numbers in ~30 rounds of parallel work — the log₂ staircase is the deepest idea in parallel algorithms.","url":"module-1/lesson-03.html","title":"1.3 Exponents, Logs & Orders of Magnitude — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-19","front":"A shader calls sinf(45.0f) intending a 45° result. What does it actually compute?","back":"sin of 45 radians ≈ 0.851 — about 7.16 full turns around the circle — 45 radians = 45/(2π) ≈ 7.16 turns; the fractional 0.16 turn lands near 0.851. No language type-checks angle units (a few libraries offer wrappers). The bug produces plausible-looking garbage — the worst kind.","url":"module-1/lesson-04.html","title":"1.4 Geometry, Coordinates & Trigonometry — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-20","front":"Two embedding vectors have squared distances d₁² = 0.84 and d₂² = 0.36 from a query. A teammate insists you must take square roots before ranking. Are they right?","back":"No — x→√x preserves order for non-negatives, so ranking by d² is identical and cheaper — √ is monotonic on [0, ∞): a² < b² ⇔ a < b. Ranking, nearest-neighbor, thresholding (compare to t²) all work in squared space. (The overflow concern is real in fp16 but is solved by scaling, not by sqrt.)","url":"module-1/lesson-04.html","title":"1.4 Geometry, Coordinates & Trigonometry — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-21","front":"a = (2, −1, 3), b = (1, 4, 0). What is a·b, and what does its sign tell you?","back":"−2 — the angle between them exceeds 90° — 2·1 + (−1)·4 + 3·0 = 2 − 4 + 0 = −2. Negative means angle > 90° (\"pointing away\"), NOT exactly opposite (that needs a·b = −‖a‖‖b‖). And the dot product is always a single number — mixing it up with elementwise multiply is the classic first NumPy bug (a*b vs a@b).","url":"module-1/lesson-05.html","title":"1.5 Vectors & the Dot Product — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-22","front":"Two embedding vectors have cosine similarity 0.98, but a·b = 47.3. What must be true?","back":"The vectors aren't normalized: a·b = ‖a‖‖b‖·0.98, so ‖a‖‖b‖ ≈ 48.3 — cos θ = a·b/(‖a‖‖b‖). A raw dot of 47.3 with cosine 0.98 just means the magnitudes multiply to ~48.3. Ranking by raw dot when magnitudes vary conflates \"similar direction\" with \"long vector\" — a real retrieval bug: long documents win regardless of relevance.","url":"module-1/lesson-05.html","title":"1.5 Vectors & the Dot Product — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-23","front":"A is 3×5, B is 5×2. Which products exist, and what shape results?","back":"Only AB, shape 3×2 — BA would need B's 2 columns to match A's 3 rows — AB: (3×5)(5×2) — inner 5s match → 3×2. BA: (5×2)(3×5) — inner 2 vs 3 fails. Shape-checking before writing any matmul line is a two-second habit that catches half of all framework bugs (\"mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied\").","url":"module-1/lesson-06.html","title":"1.6 Matrices & Matrix Multiplication — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-24","front":"Your C code fills a row-major array with matrix M and calls a column-major BLAS gemm on it, no flags. What does the library actually compute with?","back":"Mᵀ — it reads your rows as its columns; no error, wrong numbers — Both sides see the same 1D bytes but decode with different formulas (r·W+c vs c·H+r) — which is exactly the definition of transposition. Nothing crashes; results are silently wrong (or, worse, right for symmetric test matrices and wrong in prod). Fixes: use the 'T' transpose flags, swap operand order using (AB)ᵀ=BᵀAᵀ, or use cuBLAS's row-major-aware wrappers.","url":"module-1/lesson-06.html","title":"1.6 Matrices & Matrix Multiplication — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-25","front":"Two GPUs each fail on a given day with P = 0.001, independently. What's the probability your 2-GPU training job survives 30 days with neither failing?","back":"(0.999)⁶⁰ ≈ 0.942 — sixty independent survivals multiply — Survival = 0.999 per GPU-day; 2 GPUs × 30 days = 60 independent survivals: 0.999⁶⁰ = e^(60·ln0.999) ≈ e^−0.06 ≈ 0.9418. (Option A is the linear approximation — close here because P is small, but it's an approximation that fails as probabilities grow, and knowing WHICH is exact matters.) Checkpointing exists because this number decays with every added GPU and day.","url":"module-1/lesson-07.html","title":"1.7 Probability & Statistics Essentials — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-26","front":"Your benchmark of a kernel returns (ms): 2.1, 2.0, 2.1, 2.2, 14.9. The right way to report performance?","back":"Median 2.1 ms, note the outlier and check whether it repeats (likely warmup/JIT) — Median 2.1 resists the outlier and represents typical latency; the 14.9 is data ABOUT your system (first-call JIT? clock ramp? contention?) — investigate, don't hide. Min is defensible for \"best case\" microbenchmarks but overpromises for production; silent deletion is how benchmark fraud starts.","url":"module-1/lesson-07.html","title":"1.7 Probability & Statistics Essentials — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-27","front":"In fp32, what does 16777216.0f + 1.0f evaluate to, and why?","back":"16777216.0 — the gap between floats there is 2, so +1 rounds back (absorption) — 2²⁴ is where fp32's integer-exactness ends: 24 significant bits are spent, the ulp becomes 2, and 16777217 does not exist in fp32. The add rounds to the nearest representable — the original value. This is absorption, and it silently breaks long float accumulators (frame counters, running sums) in real systems.","url":"module-1/lesson-08.html","title":"1.8 Floating Point — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-28","front":"A parallel GPU sum of the same fp32 array gives 1024.0007 while the sequential CPU sum gives 1024.0011. Who's wrong?","back":"Neither — addition order differs, rounding differs; the tree sum is typically CLOSER to the true value — (a+b)+c ≠ a+(b+c) in floats. The GPU's pairwise/tree order keeps partial sums similar in magnitude → less absorption → error O(log n) vs the sequential running sum's O(n). Different ≠ wrong; tests must use tolerances, and \"GPU matches CPU bitwise\" is the wrong acceptance criterion.","url":"module-1/lesson-08.html","title":"1.8 Floating Point — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-29","front":"After x = 7; y = x; x = x + 1 , what are x and y — and why?","back":"x = 8, y = 7 — y's tag still points at the int 7; only x was re-pointed — y = x pointed y's tag at the SAME int 7 object x pointed at. x = x + 1 then re-pointed x at a new object (8); y's tag never moved. Names point at objects; they do not point at other names. (This exact picture becomes critical when the object is a mutable list — 2.4.)","url":"module-2/lesson-01.html","title":"2.1 Your First Program — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-30","front":"What does print(int(\"7\") + int(2.9) + 7 // 2) output?","back":"12 — 7 + 2 (truncated) + 3 (floor division) — int(\"7\")=7; int(2.9)=2 — int() truncates toward zero, it does NOT round; 7//2=3 — floor division stays int. Sum: 12. The int()-truncates fact is a recurring interview micro-trap and a real bug source in index math.","url":"module-2/lesson-01.html","title":"2.1 Your First Program — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-31","front":"What does this print? for i in range(2, 11, 3): print(i, end=\" \")","back":"2 5 8 — 11 would equal stop, and stop is never produced — Values are start, start+step, … strictly below stop: 2, 5, 8 (next would be 11 = stop, excluded — half-open). Count check: (11−2+3−1)//3 = 3 ✓ — M1's ceiling division predicts loop lengths.","url":"module-2/lesson-02.html","title":"2.2 Control Flow — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-32","front":"This loop is meant to find the first power of two ≥ N: p = 1 then while p < N: p *= 2 . For which N does it misbehave, and how?","back":"No N misbehaves in Python — but N ≤ 0 returns p = 1, which may be a WRONG answer rather than a crash — For N ≤ 0 the condition is False immediately and p = 1 comes back — silently claiming 1 is a power of two ≥ N (true mathematically for N ≤ 1, but is it what the caller meant for N = -5?). Python ints don't overflow (M1 L1), and N = 1 correctly returns 1. The lesson: loops rarely crash on edge cases — they return plausible wrong answers. Decide and document behavior for the edges (and in C, the overflow answer WOULD be a live bug — Module 3).","url":"module-2/lesson-02.html","title":"2.2 Control Flow — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-33","front":"What does this print? def f(x): x = x + 1 then y = f(10); print(y)","back":"None — f has no return, so the call evaluates to None — Python functions return None unless a return says otherwise — there is no implicit \"last expression\" return (that's other languages). The x=x+1 happened in f's frame and evaporated with it. When something is mysteriously None, look for the missing return first.","url":"module-2/lesson-03.html","title":"2.3 Functions & Scope — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-34","front":"count = 0 at module level; inside def tick(): count += 1 . Calling tick() raises UnboundLocalError. Why, when reading count works fine elsewhere?","back":"The assignment makes count local to tick for the whole function body — so += reads a local that doesn't exist yet — count += 1 is count = count + 1: the assignment marks count LOCAL everywhere in tick, so the read side finds an unassigned local. Fixes, best first: pass it in and return it (pure!), use a class (later), or declare global count (design smell). The rule is compile-time, not line-by-line — that's the surprising part worth remembering.","url":"module-2/lesson-03.html","title":"2.3 Functions & Scope — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-35","front":"runs = [3, 1, 2] ; result = runs.sort() ; print(result, runs) outputs?","back":"None [1, 2, 3] — sort mutates in place and returns None — In-place mutators return None by convention — Python's way of saying \"the effect WAS the result.\" Want an expression? sorted(runs). The assign-the-None bug then propagates: result[0] crashes with 'NoneType is not subscriptable' — now you know what that message means before you've ever seen it.","url":"module-2/lesson-04.html","title":"2.4 Lists, Tuples, Dicts & Sets — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-36","front":"You need \"does this dtype string belong to the supported set\" checked a million times against 5 supported dtypes. Which is right, and why does it barely matter HERE but matter enormously at 10⁶ supported items?","back":"Set + in — hashed O(1)-ish vs linear scan; at 5 items both are instant, at 10⁶ the list scan is ~10⁶× more work per query — Membership: set hashes to the answer; list scans element by element. Five items: nanoseconds either way (constants dominate — M1 L3 §3 honesty). A million items × a million queries: 10¹² comparisons vs 10⁶ hashes — the growth-class table decides. Binary search (log n) is a fine middle path but you'd be hand-building what set already is. Default: set for membership, and know WHY.","url":"module-2/lesson-04.html","title":"2.4 Lists, Tuples, Dicts & Sets — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-37","front":"x = np.zeros((3, 4)); y = x[1]; y += 5 . What is x[1, 2] , and what's the contrast with 2.4's lists?","back":"5.0 — x[1] is a VIEW of row 1; += wrote through it into x's buffer — Array indexing/slicing yields views onto the same buffer; += mutates in place through the view. A Python LIST slice would have copied (2.4 §1) — same syntax, opposite memory semantics, and knowing which you're holding is the sharp edge of this lesson. y.base is x confirms it.","url":"module-2/lesson-05.html","title":"2.5 NumPy & Vectorized Thinking — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-38","front":"u = np.arange(3); v = u.reshape(3, 1); u + v — what comes out, and why is this the \"accidental broadcast\" to fear?","back":"shape (3,3) — (3,) right-aligns as (1,3), meets (3,1), both stretch: an outer sum you didn't ask for — Right-align: (3,1) vs (1,3) → both 1-axes stretch → (3,3). Legal, silent, and at (100000,) vs (100000,1) it's a 10¹⁰-element, 80 GB \"oops\" (or, subtler, a wrong loss that still trains — the worst outcome). Defense: shape asserts at function boundaries (2.6) and .reshape(-1) / keepdims discipline. This single gotcha causes real production incidents in ML codebases.","url":"module-2/lesson-05.html","title":"2.5 NumPy & Vectorized Thinking — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-39","front":"A 40-line script wraps everything in try: main() except Exception: print(\"error, retrying\") and loops. Production reports it \"hangs at 100% CPU, no logs.\" What happened?","back":"A real bug (say, KeyError) is caught, its traceback destroyed, and the doomed call retried forever — the handler turned a crash into an infinite silent loop — Catch-all + retry is the classic bug laundering machine: deterministic bugs retry identically forever, and the evidence (traceback) is discarded each time. Fixes: catch only the retryable types (network timeouts), log the FULL traceback (logging.exception), cap retries with backoff, and let everything else crash loudly. \"Catch late, catch narrow\" is this quiz in four words.","url":"module-2/lesson-06.html","title":"2.6 Errors, Debugging & Testing — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-40","front":"Your fp32 kernel test assert np.allclose(fast, oracle, rtol=1e-7) passes on 100-element inputs but fails on 1M-element inputs. The most likely verdict?","back":"The tolerance is wrong: accumulated rounding grows with n (order differs between implementations), and 1e-7 is below fp32's error floor for million-term sums — budget it, don't loosen blindly — rtol=1e-7 is BELOW fp32's single-op ε (1.19e-7) — no two differently-ordered million-term fp32 reductions can meet it (M1 L8: non-associativity, error ~ log n · ε at best). The fix is a computed budget (e.g., rtol=1e-5 for pairwise sums), documented in the test. A scale-dependent bug IS possible — which is why you budget first, then investigate residuals above budget. The test was wrong before the kernel was.","url":"module-2/lesson-06.html","title":"2.6 Errors, Debugging & Testing — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-41","front":"You create test.py to try things, later random.py for dice utilities. Suddenly import numpy fails deep inside with AttributeError: module 'random' has no attribute 'gauss' . What happened?","back":"Your random.py shadows the standard library's random module — NumPy imported YOUR file (script's folder wins the search) and found no gauss in it — Import search checks the script's directory before the standard library — your random.py wins, and every consumer of random (NumPy included) gets your dice file. Diagnose with print(random.__file__); fix by renaming (and deleting the stale random.pyc/__pycache__). The error appearing INSIDE numpy is the cruel part: the traceback points at innocent code. Never name files after anything importable — test.py included (pytest tries to collect it).","url":"module-2/lesson-07.html","title":"2.7 Files, Modules & Environments — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-42","front":"A script works launched as python scripts/run.py from the repo root but crashes with FileNotFoundError from anywhere else. The code says open(\"data/config.json\") . Diagnosis and durable fix?","back":"The relative path resolves against the CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY, not the script's location — anchor it: Path(__file__).parent.parent / \"data\" / \"config.json\" — \"data/config.json\" means \"data under wherever the USER happens to be standing\" — launch-location roulette. __file__ anchoring makes the path a property of the code's location, invariant to cwd. (For installed packages the modern tool is importlib.resources, same principle.) This single fix ends a whole genus of \"works for me\" tickets.","url":"module-2/lesson-07.html","title":"2.7 Files, Modules & Environments — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-43","front":"Your edges output is random-looking static instead of ring outlines. The most likely one-line cause?","back":"You differenced in uint8: 5 − 10 wrapped to 251 (M1 L1), so every gentle slope became a violent edge — cast to float32 first — Unsigned subtraction wraps: anywhere the right neighbor is darker, dx explodes to ~250 instead of a small negative. The magnitude map then looks like noise. The float32 cast (or int16) before differencing is the Stage-2 dance — this quiz is the course's single most-committed image bug, pre-committed for you.","url":"module-2/lesson-08.html","title":"2.8 Capstone: Image Processing with NumPy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-44","front":"blur3 shrinks (256,384) to (254,382), and a teammate \"fixes\" it by padding the INPUT with zeros first so shapes match. What did the fix trade, and what's the honest menu?","back":"It invented black pixels: the border now blurs toward darkness (visible dark halo). The menu — shrink (honest, smaller), zero-pad (halo), edge-replicate pad (smeared border), reflect pad (usually best-looking) — is a POLICY choice to document and test, not a shape bug to hide — Every convolution library (scipy, PyTorch conv2d) exposes exactly this choice as mode=/padding= — because there is no free option, only documented trade-offs. Your test should pin BOTH the shape contract AND the border behavior on a known fixture. \"Make the shapes match\" without choosing a policy is how dark halos ship.","url":"module-2/lesson-08.html","title":"2.8 Capstone: Image Processing with NumPy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-45","front":"gcc main.c -o prog fails with undefined reference to 'sqrt' — yet #include <math.h> is right there at the top. What's actually wrong?","back":"math.h only DECLARES sqrt (satisfies the compiler); the linker still needs the BODY — add -lm to link libm — Headers are promises, libraries are deliveries (the m3l1-symbols picture). The compiler was satisfied by the declaration; the matchmaker found no body. On Linux, libm is separate: gcc main.c -o prog -lm. Distinguishing compile-stage from link-stage errors by MESSAGE alone is a skill interviewers actively probe.","url":"module-3/lesson-01.html","title":"3.1 Why C — Source to Binary — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-46","front":"printf(\"%d\\n\", 2.75); compiles (with a warning) and prints garbage like 536870912 . Why garbage and not 2 or an error?","back":"%d makes printf reinterpret the double's raw IEEE-754 bytes (M1 L8!) as an int — undefined behavior, garbage by luck — No conversion happens: printf reads the argument bytes AS an int because you promised int. A double's bit pattern (sign/exponent/mantissa — M1 L8) read as int32 is meaningless. It \"works\" differently across machines and optimization levels — the definition of UB. The warning you scrolled past named this exactly.","url":"module-3/lesson-01.html","title":"3.1 Why C — Source to Binary — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-47","front":"uint8_t a = 5, b = 10; int d = a - b; — what is d, and why isn't it 251?","back":"-5 — a and b promote to int BEFORE the subtraction; the math never happened in uint8 — Promotion first (diagram 2): int(5) − int(10) = −5, stored into an int unchanged. You'd get 251 only by storing into uint8_t (narrowing mod 256) — or in NumPy, which wraps in-dtype (M2 L5)! Same expression, three systems, three answers: C-int −5, C-uint8-store 251, NumPy-uint8 251. Knowing WHOSE rules apply is the entire game.","url":"module-3/lesson-02.html","title":"3.2 Types, Sizes & Undefined Behavior — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-48","front":"This loop hangs only in release builds: for (int i = 1; i > 0; i *= 2) count++; . The developer says \"-O2 has a bug.\" Your verdict?","back":"i *= 2 eventually signed-overflows (UB); -O2 legally reasons 'i starts positive, doubling stays positive' → i > 0 becomes 'true' → infinite loop. The BUG is the overflow; -O0 just hid it — At -O0, i wraps to negative on most hardware and the loop exits after 31 iterations — UB \"behaving\". At -O2 the optimizer uses the no-overflow guarantee YOU provided by using signed int. Fixes: unsigned i (defined wrap → loop exits), or bound the loop explicitly. UBSan flags line and value on the first run. \"Works at -O0, breaks at -O2\" ≈ UB, almost always — it's row 4 of 3.8's tool table.","url":"module-3/lesson-02.html","title":"3.2 Types, Sizes & Undefined Behavior — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-49","front":"int a = 1, b = 9; int *p = &a; *p = 5; p = &b; *p = 7; — final values of a and b?","back":"a=5, b=7 — *p=5 wrote through to a; then p MOVED to b; *p=7 wrote b — Two different operations: *p = 5 writes THROUGH the current arrow (a); p = &b re-aims the arrow itself (no data touched); *p = 7 writes through the new aim (b). Following-vs-moving is the pointer analog of M2 L1's \"= moves tags\" — and confusing them is the #1 first-week pointer error.","url":"module-3/lesson-03.html","title":"3.3 Pointers — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-50","front":"Why does cudaMalloc take void** instead of just returning the new pointer like malloc does?","back":"Its return value is already spent on the error code (cudaError_t) — so the address must travel out through a pointer-to-your-pointer — malloc signals failure in-band (returns NULL). CUDA's convention: EVERY call returns cudaError_t, so results leave via out-params. To fill in your float* it needs the float*'s address: a float** (cast to void** for genericity). You'll type this weekly for years — and now it's a design decision you can explain, not an incantation.","url":"module-3/lesson-03.html","title":"3.3 Pointers — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-51","front":"double d[10]; f(d); where void f(double *x) . Inside f, what does sizeof(x) / sizeof(x[0]) evaluate to on a 64-bit machine, and what did the author probably want?","back":"1 — sizeof(pointer)/sizeof(double) = 8/8; the length was lost to decay and must be passed as a parameter — x is a pointer (8 bytes) no matter what was passed; 8/8 = 1. The ARRAY_LEN idiom only works in the scope where the true array is visible. The loop for(i=0;i<sizeof(x)/sizeof(x[0]);i++) silently processing ONE element is a real, recurring production bug — quiet data loss, no crash. Counts travel as parameters, forever.","url":"module-3/lesson-04.html","title":"3.4 Arrays, Strings & Pointer Arithmetic — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-52","front":"char buf[8]; snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, \"%s\", \"benchmark\"); — what's in buf, and what did snprintf just save you from?","back":"\"benchma\" + \\0 — truncated at 7 chars + terminator; strcpy would have written 10 bytes into an 8-byte box (overflow into the neighbors) — \"benchmark\" is 9 chars + \\0 = 10 bytes. snprintf writes at most size−1 chars and ALWAYS terminates: \"benchma\\0\". Its return value (9 = what it WANTED to write) lets you detect truncation: if (ret >= (int)sizeof buf) → truncated (M2 L7's filename discipline had this same check). strcpy would have overflowed — the single most exploited bug class in computing history, retired from your code today.","url":"module-3/lesson-04.html","title":"3.4 Arrays, Strings & Pointer Arithmetic — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-53","front":"double *make(void) { double d[1000]; return d; } vs double *make2(void) { return malloc(1000 * sizeof(double)); } — why does gcc warn on the first and stay silent on the second, and what NEW obligation does the second create?","back":"d lives in make's frame and dies at return (dangling — the stack neighborhood); malloc's block survives returns (heap) but now SOMEONE must free it — the caller inherits ownership, which the API must document — Same signature, opposite lifetimes — the memory-map diagram in one quiz. The second is correct AND dangerous differently: an undocumented transfer of ownership is a leak factory (\"who frees make2's result?\"). Real APIs write it down: 'caller must free' in the docs, or a paired destroy function (make_buffer/free_buffer — the shape of cudaMalloc/cudaFree).","url":"module-3/lesson-05.html","title":"3.5 The Stack, the Heap & malloc — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-54","front":"A service leaks ~40 MB/hour. The code contains buf = realloc(buf, cap * 2 * sizeof *buf); inside a retry loop where realloc occasionally fails (huge caps). Where's the leak?","back":"On failure realloc returns NULL but the OLD block still exists — and buf just got overwritten with NULL: the old block's last tag is gone. Leak, then likely NULL-deref later — The x = realloc(x, ...) anti-pattern: failure orphans the old allocation (no pointer left to free it — M2 L1's 'no tags left' but with no collector coming). The temporary-variable idiom from the worked example is the entire fix. This exact bug ships constantly because the happy path works perfectly. (cap*2 overflow is ALSO worth guarding at huge sizes — calloc checks its multiply; your realloc wrapper should too.)","url":"module-3/lesson-05.html","title":"3.5 The Stack, the Heap & malloc — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-55","front":"sizeof(struct { int32_t a; double b; int32_t c; }) on a normal 64-bit system?","back":"24 — a(4)+pad(4)+b(8)+c(4)+pad(4): b needs offset 8; the struct rounds to 8 for arrays — b's alignment (8) forces 4 pad bytes after a; trailing pad brings 20→24 so runs[1].b stays aligned in an array (the diagram's rule). Reorder as {double b; int32_t a, c;} → 16 bytes, zero waste. Padding IS deterministic given the ABI — 'could be anything' undersells how predictable (and exploitable for savings) it is.","url":"module-3/lesson-06.html","title":"3.6 Structs, Headers & the Build — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-56","front":"You edit a constant in buffer.h. make rebuilds main.o, buffer.o, AND program — but you \"only touched a header.\" Correct behavior or broken Makefile?","back":"Correct — every unit that PASTED buffer.h compiled that constant into its .o; all of them are now stale. Headers are inputs to every unit that includes them — #include is paste (3.1): the header's text is INSIDE each unit's compilation. The Makefile's %.o: %.c buffer.h line declares that truth. The REAL bug is the opposite: a Makefile that omits header deps quietly keeps stale .o files — mixed-version binaries with impossible bugs. (Real projects auto-generate header deps with -MMD; deep dive §4.)","url":"module-3/lesson-06.html","title":"3.6 Structs, Headers & the Build — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-57","front":"void process(std::vector<float> v) is called in a loop 1000 times with a 40 MB vector. The profiler shows 60% of runtime in memcpy. Diagnosis and the one-character-ish fix?","back":"Pass-by-value deep-copies 40 MB per call (value semantics!); make it const std::vector<float>& — borrow instead of copy — 40 GB of copying hiding behind a clean-looking signature — the valuesem diagram's cost row, live. const& borrows (Python's default, made explicit and safe). The deeper habit: in C++ reviews, every by-value container parameter gets a \"did you mean this?\" — it's correct occasionally (when you NEED a private copy), and that intent should be visible in a comment.","url":"module-3/lesson-07.html","title":"3.7 C++ Essentials for CUDA — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-58","front":"auto &x = v[3]; v.push_back(1.0f); x = 9.0f; — the write to x sometimes updates v[3], sometimes corrupts, sometimes crashes. Which rule from THIS MODULE did the code break, and in which lesson did you first meet it?","back":"Growth may realloc-move the buffer, invalidating every prior pointer/reference/iterator — 3.5's realloc rule (Ex.2), inherited by vector unchanged — The reference is an address into the OLD buffer; push_back at capacity moves the block and frees it (use-after-free wearing modern clothes). \"Sometimes fine\" = capacity had room (no move) — the same nondeterminism that made 3.5's version a heisenbug. Fixes: reserve() first, take the reference AFTER growth, or store the INDEX (indices survive moves — 3.5 Ex.2's closing rule). You built this bug's engine by hand two lessons ago; that's why you can now diagnose it from the symptom line alone.","url":"module-3/lesson-07.html","title":"3.7 C++ Essentials for CUDA — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-59","front":"A program crashes 1-in-1000 runs in production (no sanitizers there). You have one core dump. What's the highest-value FIRST move, and why not \"add printfs and redeploy\"?","back":"gdb ./prog core → bt → inspect the dying frame's variables: the autopsy is already in hand — no waiting, no heisenbug risk from changed timing/layout — The core IS the crash, frozen — bt + print interrogate it immediately. printf-redeploy waits ~1000 runs AND can shift memory layout/timing enough to hide the bug (the heisenbug effect — UB symptoms move when the binary changes, gotcha #3's physics). Then: reproduce locally under ASan with the clues from the core. Core-dump literacy is a production-engineering marker skill.","url":"module-3/lesson-08.html","title":"3.8 Segfaults, gdb & Sanitizers — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-60","front":"ASan says: READ of size 4 ... 16 bytes to the LEFT of a 4000-byte region allocated at matrix_flat matmul.c:12 . Translate to a hypothesis before opening the editor.","back":"Some index computed −4 elements before the block: reading M[r*W+c] with a NEGATIVE or wrapped index — likely r or c went negative / the IDX formula underflowed (16 bytes left = index −4 in fp32) — LEFT of the region = index below 0: 16/4 = element −4 (M1 L1 division turning bytes into elements — the ⑤ annotation doing real work). Prime suspects from this course: size_t subtraction wrap (3.2's countdown trap) making a huge index that ALIASES negative, or r−1 at the boundary of a stencil. The report handed you the index; the hypothesis names the line class before you scroll. That's instrument literacy.","url":"module-3/lesson-08.html","title":"3.8 Segfaults, gdb & Sanitizers — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-61","front":"You test three builds against plain-naive: (a) ikj at -O2, (b) naive at -O3 -ffast-math -march=native, (c) a pairwise tree-sum version (5.8's shape). Which may legally differ from the reference, and which must match bitwise?","back":"(a) matches BITWISE: ikj reorders the loops but each C cell still accumulates its K products in ascending-k order — same sums, same rounding. (b) and (c) may differ within budget: fast-math licenses SIMD reassociation, and the tree changes the addition ORDER structurally (M1 L8 non-associativity) — The subtle one — verified by measurement in this course's own QA: naive/ikj/blocked all sum each cell in ascending k, so they agree to the BIT under default flags (loop order ≠ addition order!). What changes addition order: -ffast-math's vectorized partial sums, explicit tree reductions, and atomics-based parallel sums — those need the K·ε budget (and trees are usually MORE accurate: seq_sum of {1e8, 1.0×8} loses all eight 1.0s to absorption; tree_sum returns 100000008 — M1 L8 §3, demonstrable in 10 lines of C). Knowing WHICH transformations preserve order is what lets you demand bitwise tests where they're valid and budgets where they aren't.","url":"module-3/lesson-09.html","title":"3.9 Capstone: CPU Matmul & the Honest Ladder — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-62","front":"Adding #pragma omp parallel for to the naive ijk's OUTER loop works fine, but a teammate put it on the INNER k-loop and gets wrong results AND 10× slowdown. Both effects, explained?","back":"Correctness: all k-iterations write the SAME s/C cell — a data race (M2 L3's hidden-state disaster, live). Speed: a fork/join barrier per (i,j) pair = millions of synchronizations for microseconds of work each — Parallelize where iterations are INDEPENDENT (i or j: different C cells — purity!) and where the work-per-fork amortizes the ~microsecond fork/join cost (M1 L3 estimation: 1M forks × 1µs = seconds of pure overhead). The k-loop is a REDUCTION — parallelizing it correctly needs reduction machinery (omp reduction clause, or 5.8's tree), not a bare pragma. 'Which loop gets the pragma' is the CPU rehearsal of 'which loop becomes the grid' (3.4 Ex.1) — the same design instinct, third appearance.","url":"module-3/lesson-09.html","title":"3.9 Capstone: CPU Matmul & the Honest Ladder — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-63","front":"A 3.5 GHz core runs your loop at IPC 0.4. Your colleague proposes overclocking to 4.0 GHz (+14%). Roughly what speedup should you promise, and what's the better first move?","back":"Likely much less than 14% if the stalls are MEMORY-bound (DRAM doesn't speed up with the core clock); the better first move is finding WHY IPC is 0.4 — perf counters, then fix stalls or chains — IPC 0.4 says the core is idle 90% of issue slots. If waits are memory-latency (measured in NANOSECONDS, not cycles), a faster clock just counts more idle cycles per wait. If the chain is arithmetic-latency, clock helps proportionally. Diagnose before buying hertz — the exact discipline 3.9's perf preview taught, now with the mechanism attached.","url":"module-4/lesson-01.html","title":"4.1 Inside a CPU Core — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-64","front":"Why does s += x[i] in a float loop cap IPC near 0.25 on a 4-wide core with 4-cycle FP add latency — and why can't -O2 fix it while -O2 CAN fix the same loop over ints?","back":"Each += depends on the previous (a serial chain: one result per 4 cycles = 0.25/cycle); splitting into partial sums changes float ROUNDING ORDER (M1 L8: non-associative), so -O2 may not do it — int addition IS associative, so -O2 unrolls ints freely — The chain diagram, quantified: latency 4, one chain → 1 add per 4 cycles regardless of width. Integer sums reassociate legally (exact math — M1 L1); float sums don't (M1 L8) — so the identical loop shape gets vectorized for int and left serial for float unless YOU write partial sums or grant -ffast-math. This asymmetry, which your 3.9 QA measured as the -O3-helps-ikj-more mystery, is a genuinely elite interview answer.","url":"module-4/lesson-01.html","title":"4.1 Inside a CPU Core — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-65","front":"Your stride-sweep shows the plateau starting at stride 8 (32 B) on a friend's Apple-silicon laptop, not stride 16. Broken experiment?","back":"No — the experiment WORKS: it just measured a different machine's line/sector behavior (Apple M-series uses 128-B lines but the plateau shape depends on prefetch + hierarchy details; the METHOD found the truth for that machine) — 64 B is the x86 convention, not physics — M-series lines are 128 B, GPUs serve 32-B sectors, and the whole point of measuring is that YOUR machine answers for itself. The experiment's shape (climb then plateau) is universal; the knee's position is the hardware's signature. Same epistemology as 3.9: models predict, measurements decide, discrepancies teach.","url":"module-4/lesson-02.html","title":"4.2 The Memory Hierarchy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-66","front":"An 8-thread histogram over bytes (256 int bins, shared array, one atomic-free bin[v]++ per element after per-thread privatization was SKIPPED) runs SLOWER than 1 thread. The counters show massive coherence traffic. Diagnosis and the two-word fix family?","back":"False sharing (plus true sharing): 256 ints = 16 lines ping-ponging between 8 cores on every increment — private per-thread histograms merged at the end remove ALL sharing (\"privatize, reduce\") — Yes there's also a correctness race (M2 L3's warning) — but the PERFORMANCE crater is line ping-pong: every write invalidates the line in 7 other L1s. \"Privatize, then reduce\" (per-thread bins → tree-merge, M1 L3's staircase) fixes correctness AND performance in one move — and it is EXACTLY the shared-memory histogram pattern of CUDA kernels (5.8): same disease, same cure, 100× more threads.","url":"module-4/lesson-02.html","title":"4.2 The Memory Hierarchy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-67","front":"A pipeline is 95% parallelizable. Marketing wants \"50× faster with our 64-core box!\" on the slide. What's the honest ceiling, and what SINGLE change to the workload could make the slide almost true?","back":"Amdahl caps it at 1/(0.05 + 0.95/64) ≈ 15.4× (∞ cores: 20×). The rescue is Gustafson's: grow the parallel work per run (bigger batches/resolution) so the serial 5% shrinks as a FRACTION — then 40–50× effective throughput at fixed time is honest — 1/(0.05+0.95/64) = 15.4 — the law is merciless at fixed work. But scale the problem (more items per batch) and the serial setup amortizes: throughput-per-hour scales near-linearly. Same hardware, both claims 'true' — knowing WHICH question each law answers is the difference between engineering and marketing. (Superlinear speedups DO occur when per-core working sets start fitting cache — real, occasional, and worth recognizing rather than worshipping.)","url":"module-4/lesson-03.html","title":"4.3 The Parallelism Taxonomy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-68","front":"Inside one warp, if (x[i] > 0) f(); else g(); where the condition is a coin flip per thread. SIMD, SIMT, and MIMD each handle this differently — rank the cost and name the mechanism per shape.","back":"MIMD: free-ish (each core takes its own path). SIMT: runs BOTH f and g serially with masks — ~2× the work every time. SIMD: no branching exists — you compute both and blend (also ~2×) but with zero flush risk. The predictor-flush cost of 4.1 applies per-CORE (MIMD/scalar), not per-lane — The taxonomy's cash value: divergence is a SHAPE property. MIMD cores go their own ways (4.1's predictor handles each). A warp has ONE program counter — split opinions mean executing f (half masked off) THEN g (other half masked): the 4.4 divergence bill. SIMD never branches — 4.1 Ex.2's select/blend IS the only option. Corollary you'll use weekly: sort/partition your data so warps agree (M1 L7's sorted-data trick becomes a GPU performance pattern).","url":"module-4/lesson-03.html","title":"4.3 The Parallelism Taxonomy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-69","front":"A kernel uses 128 registers per thread (a fat physics kernel). Blocks of 256 threads. How many blocks fit per SM (65,536 registers), what's the occupancy, and is that automatically bad?","back":"65,536/(256×128) = 2 blocks = 16 warps = 25% occupancy — bad ONLY if stalls outnumber the coverage: a compute-dense kernel with high ILP per warp can run near-peak at 25% (occupancy is a means, not a score) — The floor division: 65,536/32,768 = 2 (M2 L3 P3's formula, on real silicon). But the second half is the wisdom: warps-needed ≈ latency/issue-gap — if this kernel's warps each carry long independent FMA chains (high ILP, 4.1), 16 warps may cover the stalls fine. Chasing 100% occupancy by spilling registers to memory (the compiler WILL if you cap regs) often makes kernels SLOWER. 5.6 measures this trade; today you can already reason it.","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"4.4 Inside the GPU — SMs, Warps & Latency Hiding — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-70","front":"Within one warp: if (threadIdx.x < 16) f(); else g(); versus if (blockIdx.x % 2) f(); else g(); — same branch shape, wildly different cost. Explain.","back":"The first splits INSIDE every warp (lanes 0–15 vs 16–31): true divergence, f then g serially, ~2×. The second is uniform PER BLOCK — every warp in a block agrees, takes ONE path: free (a predictable branch — 4.1's happy case) — Divergence is measured at WARP granularity: uniform-within-warp branches cost nothing (all masks on). Corollary that designs real kernels: make branch conditions functions of warp/block ID or of SORTED data (4.3's corollary), not of per-lane noise. The same physics gave you 4.1's sorted-vs-shuffled ×3; here the multiplier applies to 32 lanes at once. Branch SHAPE, not branch COUNT, is what you audit.","url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","title":"4.4 Inside the GPU — SMs, Warps & Latency Hiding — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-71","front":"A kernel reads float4 s (16 B per thread, consecutive). How many sectors per warp, and why do vectorized loads help at ALL if the total bytes are identical to four float loads?","back":"32×16 B = 512 B = 16 sectors either way — the TRAFFIC is identical; the win is fewer INSTRUCTIONS (1 load vs 4: issue slots, address math — 4.4 §1's instruction-mix budget) and a 16-B alignment guarantee that keeps sectors tight — Bytes are bytes: bandwidth-bound kernels don't speed up from vectorization alone. What improves: 4× fewer load instructions (scheduler slots for latency hiding — 4.4), less index arithmetic, and alignment-by-construction (M1 L1's masks, guaranteed by the type — the 3.4 §4 float3-breaks-alignment warning, inverted into a tool). This distinction — traffic vs instruction economy — is a favorite senior-interview probe.","url":"module-4/lesson-05.html","title":"4.5 The GPU Memory System — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-72","front":"Two proposals to fix a hot-bin histogram (80% of values hit bin 42): (a) shared-memory per-block bins then merge; (b) keep global atomics but have each thread accumulate a private count for bin 42 in a REGISTER and flush once. Evaluate both with this lesson's model.","back":"Both attack serialization at different levels and COMPOSE: (a) moves contention from one global address to per-SM copies (~132× relief) but the hot bin still serializes within each block's shared atomic; (b) collapses each thread's 80% of hits into ONE atomic (⚡ the big win for skew); (a)+(b) together is the production pattern — The register is level 0 of the privatization staircase (register → shared → global — the memory board AS a contention strategy). Skewed data makes (b) enormous: 80% of atomics vanish into a local counter (M2 L2's count pattern, promoted to a concurrency tool). The compositional habit — privatize at EVERY level the hierarchy offers — is exactly how fast histogram/reduction libraries are actually written (5.8).","url":"module-4/lesson-05.html","title":"4.5 The GPU Memory System — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-73","front":"A data-science teammate benchmarks \"GPU vs CPU\" for standardizing a 2 GB dataframe (subtract mean, divide std — 2 passes, ~4 FLOPs/byte) INCLUDING transfer both ways, gets \"GPU is 1.4× slower,\" and files a ticket titled 'GPU overrated'. Adjudicate with numbers.","back":"4 FLOP/B ≪ the ~1,600 PCIe balance: this single-use trip could never pay (transfers ≈ 63 ms round trip vs ~30 ms of CPU compute). The honest benchmark asks the RESIDENT question: if the dataframe lives on-GPU across the whole pipeline (load once → standardize → train), the standardize step costs ~0.5 ms. Both measurements are true; only one matches production — Transfer: 4 GB / 64 GB/s ≈ 63 ms. Kernel at HBM floor: 4 GB/8 TB/s ≈ 0.5 ms. CPU: ~2 GB×2 passes/65 GB/s ≈ 60 ms compute-ish... the bridge trip alone matches the CPU's whole job. Pinning (~2×) can't fix a 400× intensity shortfall — only residency can (§2). The ticket's real title is 'we benchmarked the bridge': the single most common GPU-evaluation error in industry, and you can now dissolve it with three divisions.","url":"module-4/lesson-06.html","title":"4.6 The Host–Device Bridge — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-74","front":"Your inference server uploads each request's 4 KB prompt with a blocking cudaMemcpy, then launches, then downloads 4 KB. Throughput plateaus at ~30k req/s regardless of batch size, GPUs at 20% util. The bridge is at 0.4% width. What's saturated?","back":"The TOLL BOOTH, not the road: 3 blocking bridge operations × ~10 µs ≈ 30 µs of serialized fixed cost per request ⇒ ~33k req/s ceiling from latency alone. Fixes: batch requests into one copy/launch/copy (amortize the toll), async streams (hide it), or persistent kernels (abolish it) — 1/(30 µs) ≈ 33k/s — the measured plateau derived from the toll arithmetic (§3), with width irrelevant at these sizes. The three fixes are the standard escalation ladder, and 'batch the bridge' is why every serving framework's first knob is batch size. Diagnosing latency-vs-bandwidth saturation from a throughput plateau + a utilization number is a genuine SRE-of-ML interview question — answered here with M1 L3's ladder and one division.","url":"module-4/lesson-06.html","title":"4.6 The Host–Device Bridge — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-75","front":"A vendor slide claims \"our kernel achieves 1,650 TFLOPS on RTX 5090!\" Your one-glance audit?","back":"1650 > the 838 TF DENSE fp16 peak: the claim only parses against the sparsity-doubled brochure number — so it's either 2:4-sparse workload FLOPs (fine, must be labeled), counting skipped zeros as work (marketing), or fiction. Ask: dense or sparse, and what precision? — The asterisk audit, reflexive: dense fp16 tops at ~838; 1650 ≈ the sparse brochure line. (fp8 would double dense again — which is why \"what precision, dense or sparse\" is ONE question, not pedantry.) Sparsity claims count the zeros you didn't multiply — legitimate for genuinely 2:4-sparse models, deceptive otherwise. This 10-second audit has saved real procurement meetings; the ladder diagram is its cheat sheet.","url":"module-4/lesson-07.html","title":"4.7 Spec Sheets & the Roofline — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-76","front":"Your fused elementwise kernel measures 1.72 TB/s effective on the 5090 (96% of the memory roof) at AI = 0.4. The team wants it \"optimized to use the tensor cores.\" Response?","back":"It's DONE: at AI 0.4 the roofline caps it at 0.4×1792 ≈ 717 GFLOP/s regardless of compute units, and it's already at 96% of the binding roof. Tensor cores move the FLAT roof, which this kernel never touches. The only real lever is raising AI — fuse MORE into the pass or restructure the algorithm — The chart's commandments, applied: left-of-ridge kernels are immune to compute-side upgrades (fp8 halves BYTES — that one actually helps by raising effective AI per element! — but 'use tensor cores' on a streaming kernel is category error). Declaring a kernel FINISHED — at 96% of its physical roof — is a skill exactly as valuable as optimization, and rarer. The roofline is how you defend 'done' with physics instead of fatigue.","url":"module-4/lesson-07.html","title":"4.7 Spec Sheets & the Roofline — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-77","front":"Your binary, built with -arch=sm_90 on the cluster, prints \"no kernel image is available\" on your laptop's RTX 5090 (sm_120). But a colleague's old sm_75 binary RUNS on the 5090. Explain both facts.","back":"Your fatbin has sm_90 SASS only (newer GPUs don't run older-arch SASS directly, and you built without PTX or it was excluded); the colleague's binary carries PTX which the driver JIT-compiles forward to sm_120. Fix: -arch=native locally, or -gencode multiple targets + PTX for shipping — SASS is architecture-specific machine code; PTX is the portable IR the driver can JIT for FUTURE architectures (the toolchain diagram's whole point). Default nvcc embeds PTX for the target's virtual arch — but explicit -arch=sm_90 as code=sm_90 only, or stripped PTX, breaks forward compat. Shipping binaries = fatbin with several SASS + top PTX. This exact confusion fills forums; you now debug it from the diagram.","url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","title":"5.1 Lab Setup & First Compile — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-78","front":"hello >>() prints 8 lines. What does hello >>() do differently under the hood, and what would hello >>() vs hello >>() do?","back":"33 threads = 2 warps (one full, one with 1 live lane — 4.4's fractional-warp tax on display); 2048 = the per-block maximum (valid but heavy... actually 1024 is the block cap!); 4096 fails at LAUNCH with invalid configuration — checked by cudaGetLastError (5.4) — Warps are ceil(33/32)=2 (M1 L2's division allocating hardware); non-multiples are LEGAL but waste masked lanes. The block cap is 1024 threads (2048 is per-SM RESIDENCY, not per-block — a classic mix-up worth making once here, cheaply); exceeding it → cudaErrorInvalidConfiguration at launch, silent unless you CHECK (5.4's entire pitch). Nothing truncates silently — but nothing reports loudly either, unless you ask.","url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","title":"5.1 Lab Setup & First Compile — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-79","front":"A teammate writes int i = threadIdx.x + blockIdx.x; (plus instead of times-plus). For >>, describe exactly what the kernel computes and what the output looks like.","back":"Threads collide: block 0 covers i=0..255, block 1 covers i=1..256, etc. — elements 0..258 get processed (many multiple times, racing), elements 259..n-1 NEVER touched: mostly-stale output with a plausible-looking head — the classic silent indexing bug — i ranges only 0..(255+3): four overlapping windows. The races on shared elements are UB-ish (last-writer wins per element, fine for idempotent saxpy... but wrong coverage regardless); the tail is untouched memory (3.2's ghosts). Defense: the fixture test on RAGGED sizes (3.9's discipline) catches coverage bugs instantly — the head-looks-right trap is exactly why tests check the TAIL. This typo ships weekly somewhere; your test suite is why not from you.","url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","title":"5.2 Your First Real Kernel — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-80","front":"For a 1920×1080 image with block(16,16), compute the grid, the total threads, the wasted threads, and identify which threads waste — then say why block(32,8) might beat block(16,16) for a row-major image op even at identical occupancy.","back":"grid(120, 68): 1920/16=120 exact, but 1080/16=67.5→68 rows of blocks; threads = 120·68·256 = 2,088,960 vs 2,073,600 pixels → 15,360 idle, all in the bottom block-row's y≥1080 lanes (the 2D guard's job). block(32,8): a warp = one 32-wide row segment → fully coalesced rows AND fewer partially-idle warps at the bottom edge (8-row tail vs 16) — One dimension divides, the other doesn't — per-axis ceil + per-axis guards (M1 L2 twice over). The block(32,8) insight: warp shape follows threadIdx.x-major order, so a 32-wide block makes each warp exactly one contiguous 128-B row run (4.5's perfect sector story) — shape the BLOCK so warps align with memory, not just the occupancy math. Rounding down (option 3) silently crops 8 pixel rows: M2 L8's edge-policy sin, GPU edition.","url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","title":"5.2 Your First Real Kernel — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-81","front":"cudaMemcpy(h_y, d_y, n*sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost) after a kernel launch returns correct data WITHOUT any explicit synchronize. Why — and which two changes would break that safety?","back":"Plain cudaMemcpy is stream-ordered AND blocking: it waits for prior work in the (default) stream, then copies — an implicit sync. Broken by: (a) cudaMemcpyAsync (returns immediately — you read h_y before data lands) or (b) launching the kernel in a NON-default stream while copying in default (no ordering between them — 5.9's rules) — Stream order is the guarantee (the async diagram's timeline): same-stream operations serialize. Async variants and cross-stream launches remove exactly that guarantee — which is why 5.9 pairs them with events. The pointer-dependency option is seductive and false: CUDA tracks STREAMS, not data flow — you are the dependency tracker (M2 L3's explicit-state philosophy, at driver level).","url":"module-5/lesson-03.html","title":"5.3 Memory Management — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-82","front":"A 2 GB managed-memory training prototype runs fine; adding a per-step printf(\"%f\", loss_tensor[0]) on the HOST makes each step 400 ms slower. Mechanism, and the two fixes in cost order?","back":"The host touch migrates the page GPU→host; the next kernel touch migrates it back: a per-step round-trip page storm at bridge+fault latency (4.5 P6, live). Fixes: (1) copy just the 4 bytes with an explicit tiny cudaMemcpy (µs); (2) accumulate loss on-device, read every N steps (4.6 Ex.1a's pattern) — One innocent host read = two page migrations per step (the ledger 4.5 §4 wrote). The tiny-memcpy fix respects the topology (8 bytes cross, not pages); the batch-N fix respects the toll count (4.6 §3). Managed memory didn't lie — it silently obeyed a topology you stopped watching. This exact bug, in PyTorch clothing (.item() per step), is 4.6 P5's grep list — now you've seen its CUDA skeleton.","url":"module-5/lesson-03.html","title":"5.3 Memory Management — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-83","front":"Mid-service, every CUDA call in your inference server starts returning cudaErrorIllegalAddress — including plain cudaMalloc calls that \"can't\" be wrong. The on-call engineer proposes adding retry loops around the mallocs. Verdict and correct playbook?","back":"Useless: the error is STICKY — some earlier kernel corrupted the context, and every subsequent call echoes it. Retrying re-asks a corpse. Playbook: log + restart the process (the only cure), then reproduce offline under compute-sanitizer to find the original illegal access; in production, a watchdog that restarts on sticky errors is standard — The \"everything fails identically\" signature IS the diagnosis (sticky diagram). GetLastError clears the RECORD, not the corruption — the context stays dead (a classic interview trap). Real inference servers (Triton et al.) implement exactly this watchdog-restart pattern because one bad request's OOB must not brick the server for the next thousand requests — 5.3's scenario logic at service scale.","url":"module-5/lesson-04.html","title":"5.4 Errors & compute-sanitizer — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-84","front":"Your debug build (sync after every launch) shows the error at kernel K3's launch line. Your release build blames a cudaMemcpy 200 lines later. A teammate concludes \"the release compiler miscompiles K3\". Better hypothesis, and the 30-second test that settles it?","back":"Nothing miscompiled: both builds crash in K3; they differ only in WHERE the async error SURFACES (debug syncs at the launch = local report; release's next sync is that memcpy = distant report). Test: run the RELEASE binary with CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 — if the error moves to K3's launch line, it's pure surfacing, case closed — The reported line is where the error was NOTICED, not where it happened (channel-2 diagram). Moving error lines between build modes is the asynchrony signature, not a compiler one. LAUNCH_BLOCKING changes surfacing without changing compilation — a controlled experiment isolating one variable (M1 L7's method, debugging edition). Miscompilation exists but is the LAST hypothesis, after your own async model — 3.2's humility rule.","url":"module-5/lesson-04.html","title":"5.4 Errors & compute-sanitizer — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-85","front":"A tiled kernel is correct at TILE=16 but intermittently wrong at TILE=32 — ONLY on large inputs, maybe 1 run in 20. Racecheck flags a write-after-read hazard on As. The second __syncthreads() (after the inner k-loop) was omitted. Why did TILE=16 appear correct?","back":"Luck with scheduling: the race exists at BOTH sizes, but at 16 the warps' timing happened to order writes after reads on this hardware. Bigger tiles → more warps per block in flight between barriers → more interleavings → the loss ticket finally gets drawn. Correctness by schedule is 4.3's race lesson: absence of symptom ≠ absence of race — racecheck's verdict outranks 19 green runs — Bank conflicts (below) cost TIME, never correctness — eliminating option 4 is itself a tested skill. 32×32×2 tiles×4 B = 8 KB, nowhere near the budget. The race was always there; the schedule was kind at 16. This exact \"worked for months, broke on the new GPU\" story is a CUDA rite of passage — skip it by trusting the tool, not the streak (5.4's sanitizer-clean doctrine, mirrored).","url":"module-5/lesson-05.html","title":"5.5 Shared Memory & Tiling — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-86","front":"Halving HBM traffic 16× with TILE=16 took ~10 lines. A teammate proposes TILE=64 for \"64× reuse\". Three separate walls stand in the way — name them with their lesson citations.","back":"(1) Shared budget: two 64×64 float tiles = 32 KB — squeezes to ≤1 block on 48 KB default parts, gutting occupancy (5.6's dial). (2) Threads/block ceiling: 64×64 = 4096 threads > the 1024 hard limit (4.4) — each thread must now compute MULTIPLE outputs (register tiling, 5.11's rung). (3) Diminishing returns: at AI ≈ 16, matmul nears the T4 ridge — the bottleneck moves to compute/registers, and more reuse buys ~nothing (4.7's law: optimize the BINDING constraint) — The complete answer braids three modules — resource budget (this lesson), hardware ceiling (4.4), economics (4.7) — which is why it's a real interview question at ML-infra shops. The escape hatch (one thread : many outputs) is precisely how production kernels (and 5.11's ladder) break the coupling between tile size and thread count. \"Strictly better\" answers to tuning questions are almost always missing a constraint.","url":"module-5/lesson-05.html","title":"5.5 Shared Memory & Tiling — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-87","front":"A kernel applies slow_f to positive elements, cheap_g to negatives. On RANDOM data it runs 3.1× slower than on the SAME values SORTED (positives together). A teammate says \"sorting improved cache locality\". You saw this exact input-trick in 4.1 — with a different mechanism. Untangle both.","back":"GPU mechanism: divergence, not caches. Random signs split nearly every warp → every warp pays slow_f PLUS cheap_g. Sorted signs make warps uniform (only the boundary warp splits) → each warp pays one path. Same elements, same total work per thread, ~sum-vs-max pricing per warp. 4.1's CPU version of this trick was the BRANCH PREDICTOR (mispredict flushes); the GPU has no predictor to appease — it has masks. Same experiment, two architectures, two mechanisms: know which story you're in — The element count per path is IDENTICAL either way — only the warp-level mixing changed (option 3 dies by arithmetic: count the calls). This pairing is a spectacular interview discriminator because the famous StackOverflow sorted-array question (4.1) primes everyone to say \"branch prediction\" — on a GPU that answer is wrong. Masks, not predictions. Cache effects exist but are second-order here; the 3.1× tracks the slow/cheap path ratio.","url":"module-5/lesson-06.html","title":"5.6 Warps, Divergence & Occupancy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-88","front":"Kernel at 100% theoretical occupancy is HBM-bandwidth-bound at 85% of peak. An engineer spends a week of __launch_bounds__/tile-size tuning \"to keep occupancy at 100%\" while adding features, rejecting any variant that drops below. Judge the policy with 4.4 + 4.7.","back":"Wrong optimization target. The kernel is at 85% of the MEMORY roofline (4.7): its ceiling is bandwidth, and it's near it. Occupancy above \"enough to keep the bus saturated\" is pure slack — dropping to 75% or 50% costs nothing if the bus stays fed (Little's-law arithmetic, 4.6, decides how many warps 'enough' is). The week should have gone to TRAFFIC reduction (5.5's tiling, fusion, dtype width — the only levers that move a bandwidth ceiling). Policing a non-binding constraint is optimizing the speedometer — The complete reasoning chain: identify the binding ceiling (roofline, 4.7) → ask what occupancy must deliver (enough in-flight requests to saturate it — 4.6's bytes-in-flight) → treat everything past that as trade-able slack (the worked example traded it for registers and won). \"Occupancy = quality\" is the most common intermediate-level CUDA superstition; this quiz is its vaccination.","url":"module-5/lesson-06.html","title":"5.6 Warps, Divergence & Occupancy — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-89","front":"An image kernel assigns thread i to process ROW i of a row-major float image (each thread loops x across its row). Bandwidth is terrible. The loop inside each thread walks CONSECUTIVE addresses — a teammate insists \"each thread reads sequentially, it must be coalesced.\" Locate the error and give the fix.","back":"The instant rule is being violated by the ANALYSIS: coalescing is judged across LANES at one instruction. At any instant, lane 0 reads row 0 col k, lane 1 reads row 1 col k — addresses N·4 bytes apart: stride-N, sector floor, 8× tax at EVERY step of those \"sequential\" loops. Fix: invert the mapping — threads span x (columns) within a row-block, loop over y; adjacent lanes then read adjacent pixels. Per-thread sequentiality is irrelevant; per-WARP adjacency is everything — The seductive wrongness of \"each thread reads sequentially\" makes this THE canonical coalescing interview question. float4 on the broken mapping just fetches 16-B islands N apart — still scattered (option 4 optimizes within a broken pattern). The one-line index swap is the scenario section's 11× — cheapest big win in GPU practice. CPU intuition (\"sequential = fast\", 4.5) actively sabotages here: on CPU, per-THREAD sequential was exactly right; on GPU the warp is the actor, not the thread.","url":"module-5/lesson-07.html","title":"5.7 Coalescing in Code — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-90","front":"Profiling shows kernel A at 31.8 sectors/request (floats), kernel B at 4.2. Yet A and B run the SAME wall time, and both are \"memory-bound\" per the roofline. A teammate concludes sectors/request is a useless metric. Rescue the metric and explain the tie.","back":"The metric is telling you A moves ~8× the BYTES for the same useful data (scatter floor). Equal wall time with 8× traffic means A is being SAVED by something — likely L2: its scattered reads have reuse that hits cache (sectors/request counts L1→L2 requests, not HBM), or A simply has 8× less useful data to move. The metric isn't a verdict; it's a traffic multiplier that must be joined to TOTAL bytes and the roofline (4.7) before sentencing. Diagnosis: read A's L2 hit rate and HBM-level bytes next (5.10's two-table habit) — Metrics are witnesses, not judges (M1 L7's rule, profiler edition). The classic real-world instance: gather kernels with power-law reuse (embeddings!) show terrible sectors/request yet fine throughput because L2 absorbs the repeats — Track A's embedding-table lesson in embryo. A metric that \"contradicts\" wall time is never useless; it's naming a compensating mechanism you haven't found yet — the gap IS the to-do list (4.7's law).","url":"module-5/lesson-07.html","title":"5.7 Coalescing in Code — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-91","front":"A histogram kernel (256 bins, 100M uint8 pixels) using one global atomicAdd per pixel runs 40× slower than the same kernel on UNIFORM-random pixels when fed a real X-ray (mostly black — 80% of pixels in bins 0–3). Same instruction count. Explain, and give the two-rung fix.","back":"Contention is per-ADDRESS: uniform pixels spread atomics across 256 bin addresses (256 short queues, mostly parallel); the X-ray funnels 80% of 100M atomics into 4 addresses — four enormous serialized queues (the cash-register economics, data-dependent!). Fix: rung 1 — per-BLOCK bins in shared memory (atomics on shared: SM-local, ~10× cheaper, and 4 hot bins × 100s of blocks = parallel queues); rung 2 — one merge pass adding each block's 256 bins globally (256 × gridDim atomics total, contention-free by construction) — Atomic cost scales with QUEUE LENGTH AT EACH ADDRESS, so performance became a property of the DATA's histogram itself — beautifully circular and a real production trap (medical, astronomy, any skewed distribution). Privatization converts data-dependent serialization into fixed-cost merging. This exact question — \"your histogram is slow only on real data, why?\" — is a staple at imaging and ML-infra shops because it tests whether \"atomics serialize\" is a slogan or a model.","url":"module-5/lesson-08.html","title":"5.8 Reductions & Atomics — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-92","front":"Your v3 reduction returns 16777216.0, 16777215.8, 16777216.2 across three identical runs. An auditor demands bit-identical results run-to-run (\"regulatory reproducibility\"). Diagnose the wobble and give the two-level response: what you change, and what you tell the auditor about the CPU baseline they trust.","back":"No race: every add is atomic; what varies is ORDER (blocks finish in scheduler-dependent sequence, and float addition isn't associative — M1 L8). Fix: replace the final atomics with a deterministic architecture — blocks write partials to an ARRAY (slot = blockIdx), then a single-block second kernel reduces that array in FIXED order: bit-identical forever, ~zero cost at this size. Tell the auditor: their single-threaded CPU sum is just ONE arbitrary order among the N! possible — also not \"the true value\"; the honest standard is a documented, fixed order plus an error bound (pairwise trees have BETTER bounds than the CPU's left-to-right — M1 L8's absorption lesson) — Distinguishing NONDETERMINISM (order varies, each result individually valid) from RACES (updates lost, results invalid) is the difference between a redesign and a bugfix. Doubles shrink the wobble but don't zero it — order still varies (option 4 treats the symptom's size, not its existence). The blocks-to-array + fixed-order-merge pattern is exactly how frameworks implement deterministic mode, and knowing that the CPU's answer is not ground truth — just a privileged order — is the M1 L8 literacy that ends audit arguments.","url":"module-5/lesson-08.html","title":"5.8 Reductions & Atomics — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-93","front":"Your pipeline shows ZERO overlap in the profiler: chunks run perfectly serially despite 3 streams and correct Async calls everywhere. The buffers came from a teammate's \"fast allocator\" that wraps malloc with alignment. Diagnose, and name the 30-second experiment that convicts it without reading any allocator code.","back":"Pageable memory: DMA can't target movable pages, so each cudaMemcpyAsync silently staged/serialized (the trap in §pinned — no error, no warning, just no overlap). Experiment: swap ONE buffer to cudaMallocHost and rerun the profile — if that lane alone starts overlapping, verdict delivered. Fix: route the transfer pool through cudaMallocHost (or cudaHostRegister the allocator's pages), keeping the pool pattern of 5.3 P6 — Silent degradation is what makes this THE classic streams bug: every call succeeds, cudaSuccess everywhere, timeline flat (contrast option 4: MISSING dependencies cause wrong ANSWERS or races, not serialization). The one-buffer A/B swap is the controlled experiment habit (M1 L7) — five minutes, no code archaeology. T4 has 2 copy engines; option 3 is checkable in one line: deviceProp.asyncEngineCount (5.1's generator already printed it).","url":"module-5/lesson-09.html","title":"5.9 Streams, Events & Async — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-94","front":"To time a kernel in stream s2, an engineer records start/stop events on the DEFAULT stream around the launch, gets 0.003 ms for a kernel that nsys shows takes 4.1 ms, and concludes \"events are unreliable on this driver.\" What actually got measured, and what are the two correct placements?","back":"The events entered the DEFAULT stream's queue, not s2's: they measured the gap between two markers in a lane the kernel never ran in — i.e., ~nothing (the launch returned in µs, 5.3's enqueue trap in event clothing). Correct: (a) record BOTH events on s2 (markers bracket the kernel in ITS lane's order), or (b) for multi-stream totals, record after a join point (event after StreamWaitEvent convergence, or bracket a full drain). Events are exact; they answer exactly the question their PLACEMENT asks — Same instrument, wrong probe point — the oscilloscope was on the wrong wire. Event semantics are strictly \"a marker in ONE stream's FIFO\"; cross-stream timing without a declared join measures scheduler coincidence. The habit that prevents it: before reading any timing, say aloud WHICH lane's order both markers live in (the 5.3 async model, now your reflex). \"Instrument unreliable\" conclusions get expensive (M1 L7's scenario); \"probe misplaced\" is almost always the truth.","url":"module-5/lesson-09.html","title":"5.9 Streams, Events & Async — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-95","front":"nsys shows your training step: 41% kernels (dense), 38% H2D copies (serial, between kernel bursts), 21% gaps aligned with CPU spikes labeled \"dataloader\". Two engineers propose: (A) \"ncu the top kernel — it's 18% of kernel time\"; (B) \"fix the copy/loader pipeline first.\" Arbitrate with arithmetic.","back":"B, and it isn't close: copies + loader gaps = 59% of wall time that kernel tuning cannot touch. Perfect-overlap pipelining (5.9's ring for copies, prefetching loader threads for the CPU gaps) targets up to ~1.7× on wall time (1/(1−0.42)... compute: hiding most of 59% behind the 41% kernels bounds near 2.4×, realistically 1.5–2×). Best case for A: the 18% kernel at even 2× faster = 41%×18%×½ ≈ 3.7% of wall. Amdahl (4.4's law) arbitrates: B's denominator is 16× bigger. ncu's turn comes AFTER the timeline is dense — The numbers are the answer: Amdahl assigns each proposal its ceiling before any work starts (4.4's habit, applied to engineering time — the scarcest resource). \"Both in parallel\" sounds mature but splits attention across a 3.7%-ceiling task; and a faster GPU makes the kernel slice SMALLER, worsening the copy/loader share (the classic upgrade-disappointment, 4.6's economics). The senior tell in this answer: converting profiler percentages into bounded speedups before choosing — thirty seconds of arithmetic that redirects a quarter's roadmap.","url":"module-5/lesson-10.html","title":"5.10 Nsight Profiling — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-96","front":"ncu on your \"optimized\" kernel: SOL Memory 34%, SOL Compute 29%, achieved occupancy 24% (theoretical 75%), stall reasons dominated by 'long scoreboard'. Diagnose the kernel's actual disease, explain the theoretical-vs-achieved gap NOT being the min() table's fault, and prescribe in order.","back":"Both SOLs LOW = the starved regime: the GPU is waiting, not working (5.6's knee, from the wrong side). Achieved 24% vs theoretical 75% says the min() table PERMITS 3× more warps than show up — the gap is dynamic: grid too small (few blocks < SMs×capacity — 5.2's saturation rule), severe load imbalance (some blocks 10× longer: tail effect), or launch confetti (kernel too brief to fill). Prescribe: (1) check grid size vs SM count ×blocks/SM — the 30-second one; (2) nsys for confetti/short-kernel evidence; (3) if grid is big and balanced, THEN feed latency: more ILP per thread (5.6 P6) or merge work per block. Coalescing is innocent: sectors weren't cited, and Memory 34% means the bus is IDLE, not abused — Both-low is the third SOL regime and the least taught: not memory-bound, not compute-bound — STARVED (latency-bound). The tell-pair is (low SOLs, big achieved-occupancy gap) + long-scoreboard: warps exist on paper but not in flight, or too few exist at all. Option 1 is the reflex this module must extinguish: coalescing fixes traffic EFFICIENCY, not traffic EXISTENCE. Spills would show as local-memory traffic in the Memory tables (5.6 P4's signature) — checkable, absent here. Regime first, lever second: every prescription in this course hangs off that order.","url":"module-5/lesson-10.html","title":"5.10 Nsight Profiling — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-97","front":"Interviewer: \"Your v5 hits 3.2 TF; cuBLAS hits 4.4 on the same card. Why would anyone ever run your kernel — and when would hand-rolling actually be the right call in production?\"","back":"In production, for plain sgemm: nobody should — you ship the library (vendor-tuned per arch; my ladder exists so the library is a CHOICE I can audit, not a mystery I depend on). Hand-rolling earns its keep exactly where libraries can't go: FUSION (epilogues — bias+GELU into the matmul's registers, skipping an HBM round trip that no separate library call can skip — 5.10 P7's lever), special shapes/sparsity the library serves badly (tall-skinny, K tiny — regimes where cuBLAS's tiles misfit, verified by benchmark not vibes), and determinism or numerical-order contracts (5.8's territory). The 1.4× gap I don't chase is swizzles + cp.async + scheduling — months of vendor work; the gap I CAN beat is the memory traffic BETWEEN library calls — The answer interviewers want has three moves: concede the library's dominance on its home turf (maturity signal), name the exact niches where custom wins WITH mechanisms (fusion = traffic, not FLOPs — the course's deepest refrain), and show the ladder's real purpose (auditable understanding, not competition). Options 3/4 signal a junior who'll burn quarters rediscovering this. \"I know when not to compete\" is a hire signal at every ML-systems shop.","url":"module-5/lesson-11.html","title":"5.11 Capstone — The Matmul Ladder — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-98","front":"Interviewer: \"You claim v3 tiling made matmul 6× faster but the results are bitwise identical to v1. A colleague insists tiling reorders the summation, so identical bits prove your test is broken. Adjudicate.\"","back":"The test is fine; the colleague conflates reordering LOADS with reordering ADDS. v3's tile loop changes WHEN operands arrive from memory, but each output's accumulator still sweeps k = 0..K−1 in identical order with identical FMA contraction — float addition order per output is UNCHANGED, so bits match (Module 3 proved this exact distinction on the CPU: ikj vs naive, bitwise identical). What WOULD change bits: split-K (two partial sums merged — a genuinely different addition tree), atomics (5.8's scheduler-ordered adds), or unrolling that interleaves multiple accumulators per output. My fixtures encode this: rung-vs-rung asserted BITWISE (a free, powerful invariant — any drift flags an accidental algorithm change), rung-vs-oracle asserted with K-scaled budget (M1 L8) — This is the single most discriminating numerics question in GPU interviews because BOTH naive intuitions are wrong: \"tiling must change bits\" (no — trace the accumulator's order) and \"floats can't be compared bitwise\" (no — determinism is about ORDER, and when order is fixed, bits are law: M1 L8). Being able to name the three real bit-breakers, unprompted, is the difference between having read about floating point and having instrumented it. Option 4 flunks 3.6: different PTX, verifiably.","url":"module-5/lesson-11.html","title":"5.11 Capstone — The Matmul Ladder — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-99","front":"A colleague proposes skipping backprop entirely: \"just compute each ∂L/∂wᵢ numerically — loss(w+h) minus loss(w−h), done. Simpler code, no calculus.\" For a 7B-parameter model where one forward pass takes 0.5 s on your cluster, price their idea against backprop — and name the structural reason backprop is so much cheaper.","back":"Numeric: 2 forward passes PER parameter → 14e9 × 0.5 s ≈ 222 YEARS per training step. Backprop: one forward + one backward ≈ 1.5 s for ALL 7e9 partials. The structural reason: every parameter's gradient shares the same downstream factors (dL/da of later layers) — backprop computes each shared factor ONCE and reuses it (memoization on the graph); finite differences recompute the entire pipeline per knob, learning nothing from one probe that helps the next — The arithmetic is the argument: 222 years vs 1.5 seconds — a ×4.7e9 gap, which is why autodiff isn't an optimization, it's the enabling technology. Note that FD remains the perfect TEST (worked example) at a handful of parameters: probe a few knobs, compare, ship. Tool for training, tool for testing — different tools (M2 L6's oracle doctrine: the slow obvious method referees the fast clever one).","url":"track-a/lesson-01.html","title":"A1 Calculus for Backprop — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-100","front":"Your central-difference check of sin'(1), measured in fp64: error 9.0e-04 at h=1e-1, 9.0e-06 at 1e-2, 9.0e-08 at 1e-3 … then 2.6e-09 at 1e-8, and RISING to 1.8e-04 by h=1e-13. Explain both regimes of this V-shape, and why a teammate running gradient checks at h=1e-12 \"to be extra accurate\" keeps flagging correct gradients as broken.","back":"Left slope: truncation error — central difference is exact only in the h→0 limit; its leading error term is O(h²), hence the measured ×100 drop per decade. Right slope: cancellation — sin(x+h) and sin(x−h) agree in their leading ~15 digits, so subtracting them annihilates significance (M1 L8's catastrophic cancellation), and dividing by tiny 2h amplifies the surviving noise. The dip (~1e-5..1e-6 in fp64) is the only trustworthy zone; at h=1e-12 the teammate is measuring float noise, not calculus — their \"extra accuracy\" walked off the right side of the V — The V-shape is two error sources trading dominance — a pattern you'll re-meet everywhere (B1 measures the same V for simulation steps). The ×100-per-decade left slope is the O(h²) claim MEASURED, not cited (M1 L7). Practical constants worth memorizing: fp64 central diff → h ≈ 1e-5·max(1,|x|); fp32 is hopeless for gradient checks (the V's floor sits near 1e-3) — which is exactly why A3's gradcheck runs in double precision.","url":"track-a/lesson-01.html","title":"A1 Calculus for Backprop — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-101","front":"A teammate benchmarks their new model: \"forward pass takes 40 µs — 300× faster than the old one!\" Their code: t0=time.time(); y=model(x); print(time.time()-t0) on a CUDA tensor. The old model was benchmarked with the same snippet plus a print(y.mean().item()) on the next line. Explain BOTH measurements and produce the honest comparison protocol.","back":"Neither number is a forward-pass time, but they lie DIFFERENTLY: the new model's 40 µs is pure enqueue (kernels queued, clock read before any ran — 5.3's async model); the old model's number accidentally INCLUDED the full execution, because .item() on the next line forced a device sync inside the timed region... actually before t1? — the sync happened after t1 was read, so the old number is ALSO enqueue-only unless the .item() sat between the calls; either way the 300× compares two differently-contaminated clocks. Protocol: warmup iterations, then torch.cuda.synchronize() before t0 AND before t1 (or torch.cuda.Event pairs — 5.9's stopwatch), median of K (M1 L7), same batch/dtype/device clocks for both models — The correct option's mid-sentence self-check is deliberate — it models the reasoning: WHERE the hidden sync sits relative to the clock reads decides what got measured, and you must trace it, not vibe it (5.3's async model demands line-level reading of timing code). The tell in the wild: any GPU \"speedup\" over ~50× with time.time() and no visible synchronize is an enqueue measurement until proven otherwise. Events + warmup + medians (5.9 Ex.2's GpuTimer discipline, which torch.utils.benchmark implements for you) is the only protocol that survives review.","url":"track-a/lesson-02.html","title":"A2 PyTorch Internals — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-102","front":"Mid-refactor, someone changes h = torch.relu(z) to h = z.relu_() (\"in-place saves memory\") inside a training step. Two possible outcomes, depending on the surrounding code: (a) training continues, slightly less memory; (b) backward() raises a RuntimeError about a variable \"modified by an inplace operation.\" Explain the mechanism deciding which — and why outcome (a) is NOT automatically safe to ship.","back":"The version counter: every tensor carries a mutation count; ops that SAVE a tensor for backward record its version at save time and re-check at backward. If some earlier op saved z (needs z's original values for ITS gradient), relu_'s mutation bumps the version → backward detects the stale save → error (b): autograd refusing to compute silently-wrong gradients. If nothing saved z (e.g., z came from an add whose backward needs no inputs), the in-place is legal → (a). Not automatically safe: legality depends on the WHOLE graph's save-set, which the next refactor can change — a distant edit can turn today's (a) into tomorrow's (b), so in-place in autograd code needs a comment justifying it and a gradcheck fixture guarding it (A1's scenario doctrine) — The version counter is autograd's ASan (3.8's philosophy: make the invisible corruption LOUD): mutated-after-save would otherwise mean wrong gradients with zero symptoms — A1's scenario as a one-liner. The deep lesson is the coupling: in-place legality is a property of the graph, not the line, so it degrades under maintenance exactly like 5.5's barrier-dependent code did. Frameworks' memory planners (A9's Inductor) do provably-safe in-placing automatically — which is the mature answer to \"I want the memory back.\"","url":"track-a/lesson-02.html","title":"A2 PyTorch Internals — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-103","front":"Your op benchmarks 4.1× faster than the torch composition it replaces — standalone. Integrated into the team's inference server (a 5.9-style pipelined service), end-to-end p50 gets WORSE by 18%. nsys shows the op's kernels on the default stream while the service's copies and other kernels ride named streams, with full-width gaps around every op call. Diagnose and fix.","back":"The binding launched on the DEFAULT stream (hardcoded 0 or forgot getCurrentCUDAStream): the legacy default stream synchronizes with ALL other streams (5.9 §1), so every op call is a full pipeline barrier — the picket fence 5.9's scenario taught you to read. The op is fast; its LANE DISCIPLINE poisons the overlap around it. Fix: one line — launch on at::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream() (and pass it to any cuBLAS/cub calls inside too); rerun nsys, fences gone, then re-measure end-to-end — The single most common production custom-op bug, and it's invisible in every standalone benchmark (nothing else was in flight to serialize against — 5.9 P6's regime lesson: benchmarks lie by omission of context). The nsys signature (default-stream kernels + picket fence) convicts in one capture. Rule worth tattooing: an op that doesn't take/respect the current stream is broken even when it's fast — stream hygiene is correctness for pipelines, not a perf nicety.","url":"track-a/lesson-03.html","title":"A3 Custom CUDA Ops for PyTorch — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-104","front":"Your fused layernorm op passes gradcheck (fp64) perfectly, matches a reference in fp32 unit tests at rtol 1e-5, then destabilizes training in bf16 while the unfused torch version trains fine at bf16. Rank the suspects and name the missing fixture.","back":"Prime suspect: accumulation dtype inside the kernel — the unfused torch path reduces mean/variance in fp32 even for bf16 inputs (AccumulateType policy); if your fused kernel accumulates in bf16, the variance of a 4096-wide row loses enough digits (M1 L8's absorption at ~3-digit bf16 precision) to wobble normalization and destabilize training. gradcheck is BLIND to this — it certified fp64 calculus, not low-precision policy. Missing fixture: forward/backward vs fp32 reference AT bf16, with a per-dtype budget (and a variance-of-constant-row edge case) — test at deployment dtype (M2 L6's regime labels). Fix: accumulate in float regardless of storage dtype (5.11's law, now an API: at::acc_type) — gradcheck-in-fp64 CAN'T fail on an accumulation-dtype bug — fp64 accumulation is what it's testing in. That blind spot is the quiz's teaching: each fixture certifies one layer (a3-gradcheck's pyramid), and dtype policy is its own layer. The unfused-works/fused-fails asymmetry is the tell — the library encoded the fp32-accumulate wisdom (option 3 is refuted by the unfused path working). This exact bug class (bf16 accumulation in hand-fused norms/softmaxes) is a recurring postmortem across the industry; A5 builds the kernels with the policy baked in.","url":"track-a/lesson-03.html","title":"A3 Custom CUDA Ops for PyTorch — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-105","front":"A teammate's Triton softmax passes its fixture on random test data (rtol 1e-5, 10k random rows — green). In production, a classifier's \"no detection\" rows (all logits strongly negative) return garbage probabilities. Their load: tl.load(..., mask=mask, other=0.0) . Explain both facts — why 10k random rows never caught it, and what the production rows hit — then state the fixture doctrine this violates.","back":"With other=0.0, padded lanes enter BOTH reductions. On typical random rows (max > 0), the damage is only exp(0−max) extra in the denominator — rel-error ~1e-2 at worst, often UNDER a loose rtol with wide rows... and their rows were 781 wide with 243 pad lanes: 243·exp(−max) can still pass rtol on high-max rows — the green suite measured the bug as noise. All-negative rows are the cliff: the padded 0 BECOMES the max, the shift normalizes against a phantom, outputs are ~100× off (measured: [.007,.002,.001] vs [.665,.245,.090]). Doctrine violated: M2 L6's boundary fixtures — edge regimes (all-negative, single-element, all-equal, ±inf logits) are chosen, not sampled; random data systematically avoids exactly the corners where identity-element bugs live — The two-regime damage profile (quiet 4%-class error on normal rows, catastrophe on edge rows) is what makes identity-element bugs the WORST class: they pass casual tests and detonate on the inputs that matter (a \"no detection\" row is precisely when a downstream threshold reads the probabilities!). The executed numbers are in the worked example. Fixture rule to keep: every masked reduction gets an adversarial-row suite — all-negative, all-equal (softmax must return uniform), one-hot-large (must saturate), and n_cols == BLOCK (zero dead lanes — the OTHER edge).","url":"track-a/lesson-04.html","title":"A4 Triton Kernels in Python — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-106","front":"Your autotuned matmul kernel benchmarks beautifully in the sweep notebook. In the training job, the profiler shows periodic multi-hundred-ms stalls on the training loop's critical path, correlated with batch-size changes from the ragged final batch of each epoch. Mechanism, and the two-level fix?","back":"The autotune cache is keyed on shape (key=['M','N','K']): every NEW shape triggers a full config benchmark ON the critical path — the tuner runs in your training step (a4-autotune's \"first call per key is the tuner\"). Ragged final batches mint a new key every epoch → periodic tuning stalls. Fixes: (1) shape-level — pad/bucket batch sizes so the key set is small and stable (5.7 P2's padding economics: a few wasted rows buy away the stalls; also what serving systems do); (2) tuner-level — warm up all expected keys at startup (run each shape once before training), and/or coarsen the key (bucket M to multiples of 64) so raggedness maps to existing entries. The general law: any lazily-specializing system (autotune, torch.compile's guards — a9-when's recompile row, JITs everywhere) turns shape churn into critical-path compilation; stabilize shapes or pre-pay — This is the production face of \"the first call is the benchmark\" — harmless in notebooks, a tail-latency machine in jobs with shape diversity. The signature (stalls correlated with novel shapes) plus the fix vocabulary (bucketing, warmup, key coarsening) transfers verbatim to torch.compile (A9) and inference servers (A8's shape buckets). M1 L7's warmup ritual, it turns out, was also a PRODUCTION architecture principle all along.","url":"track-a/lesson-04.html","title":"A4 Triton Kernels in Python — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-107","front":"A profiler shows a transformer block spending 41% of time in matmuls (already cuBLAS/TC) and 38% across ~9 small ops (bias, GELU, dropout, residual adds, layernorms). Two engineers propose: (A) hand-optimize the matmuls (\"they're the biggest single item\"); (B) fuse the 9 small ops into 2–3 kernels. Arbitrate with the two ledgers, and predict B's ceiling.","back":"B, decisively. The matmuls are compute-bound at their roofline (5.11 quiz 1: you don't out-tune cuBLAS); their 41% is near-irreducible. The 9 small ops are ~zero-FLOP, traffic-priced (the §1 ledger): each is a full activation round-trip, so 9 ops ≈ 9 trips where 2–3 fused kernels ≈ 2–3 trips → that 38% shrinks toward ~12% — a ~1.3–1.4× step-time win (Amdahl on the 38%: 1/(1−0.38+0.38·3/9) ≈ 1.34), for a few days of Triton (A4's velocity). B's ceiling: once glue is fused, the block is matmul-dominated and the next lever changes LEVEL — precision (A6) or architecture, not kernels — Option 4 is the half-right trap: launch overhead (5.2's floor) is real but SECONDARY — the dominant cost is each op's full read+write of the activation tensor (the §1 ledger; a 64 MB tensor's trip is ~0.3 ms on T4 while a launch is ~5 µs). Getting the MECHANISM right matters because it predicts the fix's shape: fusion helps even for LARGE tensors where launch overhead is negligible. This quiz is A9's a9-when table derived by hand — and the exact reasoning torch.compile's authors baked into Inductor.","url":"track-a/lesson-05.html","title":"A5 ML Kernel Patterns — Fusion, Softmax, FlashAttention — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-108","front":"Your from-scratch flash kernel matches naive attention at rtol 1e-5 on random Q,K,V — but a colleague claims flash attention is \"approximate attention\" and demands you switch to the \"exact\" naive version for a compliance-sensitive model. Adjudicate with mathematical precision, including what IS different between the two outputs and the one scenario where naive is legitimately preferable.","back":"Flash is EXACT attention: the online rescale is an algebraic identity (e^{x−m} = e^{x−m'}·e^{m'−m}) — the worked example measured max|diff| = 3.3e-16 in fp64, i.e., machine epsilon, not an approximation error. What differs: floating-point ORDER (per-tile partial sums vs full-row sums), so outputs differ in last bits at working precision — 5.8 quiz 2's nondeterminism vocabulary: both orderings are individually valid evaluations of the same exact formula; neither is \"the true one\" (the naive order is not privileged — M1 L8). Legitimate naive preference: when you need the full P matrix ITSELF as an artifact (attention-map audits/interpretability exports) — then materializing N² is the requirement, not a cost — \"Tiled = approximate\" is a surprisingly common senior-level misconception, and the refutation must be surgical: identity-based reordering (exact, flash) vs genuine approximation (sparse/linear attention variants, which DO change the math). The compliance answer mirrors 5.11 quiz 2's bitwise doctrine: fix the ORDER if you need bit-reproducibility (flash is deterministic per config!), state budgets otherwise. Knowing the one honest naive use-case (wanting P itself) turns the answer from defensive to authoritative.","url":"track-a/lesson-05.html","title":"A5 ML Kernel Patterns — Fusion, Softmax, FlashAttention — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-109","front":"A team moves training from fp32 to fp16+amp. Loss curves match for 3k steps, then their run plateaus visibly above the fp32 baseline. GradScaler logs show a healthy scale (~2¹⁵, occasional backoffs). A teammate says \"loss scaling is failing — raise the scale cap.\" Diagnose with the two-failure-mode framework and name the two most likely true culprits.","back":"The scaler is HEALTHY (stable scale, occasional backoff = working as designed) — scaling fixes RANGE, and range isn't the symptom's shape. A plateau-above-baseline is the ABSORPTION signature: something is accumulating in half precision. Culprits, in base-rate order: (1) a custom op/kernel accumulating in fp16 (A3 quiz 2's bug — the fused thing someone wrote last month; audit acc dtypes), (2) optimizer state or master weights accidentally cast to half (a sweeping .half() on the whole module, or a checkpoint reloaded through a half-casting path) — updates below w·eps/2 apply as zero (worked example's law), so training crawls exactly on the smallest-gradient weights. Raising the scale does nothing for either: they're precision failures, and scale is a range knob — Diagnosis-by-failure-mode is the lesson's whole point: flush symptoms (early divergence, zero grads, inf storms) vs absorption symptoms (quiet plateaus, stalled small-weight learning) are as different as 5.4's sticky vs non-sticky errors — and each has its own instrument (grad-zero-fraction histograms for flush; an fp32-shadow comparison run for absorption). Option 3 is refuted by a decade of fp16-trained models; option 4 is the eternal scapegoat (A2 Ex.2's oscillation lesson: exhaust the mechanism hypotheses before touching hyperparameters).","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"A6 Mixed Precision & Tensor Cores — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-110","front":"Same model, two runs on an A100: hidden size 4096 → 51 ms/step; a researcher's \"tiny tweak\" to hidden 4100 (\"only 0.1% more parameters!\") → 68 ms/step. Explain the 33% cliff, the general law, and the fix that costs nothing.","back":"4100 = 4·1025 breaks tensor-core tiling: TC paths want dims in multiples of 8/16 (a6-tc — the MMA unit consumes fixed tiles); 4096 feeds clean 16-tiles, 4100 leaves ragged edges that fall back to slower paths and misaligned accesses (5.7 P2's alignment tax joins in — 4100·2B rows aren't 32B-aligned either). The law: matmul time is a STAIRCASE in dimensions, not a line — cost jumps at tile boundaries, so \"0.1% more math\" can buy 30% more time. Free fix: pad to 4104 (×8) or 4112 (×16) — or just use 4096: dimensions are KERNEL-ECONOMICS choices (A5 P4's closing lesson), and every production transformer's suspiciously-round sizes are this law, obeyed — The staircase model replaces the linear-cost intuition everyone imports from complexity class: hardware charges per TILE, and a tile is charged whether full or ragged (5.2's tail threads, at matmul scale). torch exposes the check (dims % 8) and profilers show the kernel-name change (a2-dispatch: the slow row got dispatched). The \"suspiciously round numbers\" observation turns this from trivia into reading skill: model configs are full of frozen kernel-economics decisions — now you can see them.","url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","title":"A6 Mixed Precision & Tensor Cores — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-111","front":"Moving from 8 to 32 GPUs (same per-GPU batch), tokens/s/GPU drops 34%. nsys on rank 0 shows ncclAllReduce kernels running AFTER backward completes, serially, not overlapped — on 8 GPUs the same trace shows them interleaved with backward. The model/code are unchanged. What plausibly broke, in what order do you check, and why did it only appear at 32?","back":"The overlap collapsed, not the bandwidth: with 4× ranks, per-bucket allreduce LATENCY grew (more hops per ring step — 2(N−1) latencies) while bucket compute-time between gradient-ready events stayed fixed → buckets now finish arriving after backward ends (the a7-overlap timeline's network row slid right past the compute row). Check order: (1) bucket_cap_mb — fewer, larger buckets amortize hops (tolls, 4.6) but verify the tail; (2) confirm no stray synchronize/.item() between backward and step that serializes (A2's drains break overlap too and can COINCIDE with scale-up commits); (3) NCCL topology dump — did 32 GPUs span a slower inter-node link the 8-GPU job never touched? (the worked example's cliff hiding inside \"same cluster\"). It appeared at 32 because latency terms scale with N while overlap headroom didn't — a regime crossing, not a mystery (5.9 P6) — The trace's serial-vs-interleaved comparison is the whole diagnosis — 5.10's timeline literacy pointed at a new row (network). Note the checking order: config (free) → code drains (cheap) → topology (the expensive truth). Option 4 is checkable in the same trace (gaps BEFORE forward, not after backward) — one capture arbitrates all hypotheses, which is why the capture comes before any opinion (5.10's law, fleet edition).","url":"track-a/lesson-07.html","title":"A7 Multi-GPU — NCCL, DDP, FSDP — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-112","front":"A team enables FSDP on their 13B model across 8×80GB GPUs expecting ~\"13B×16 bytes ÷ 8 ≈ 26 GB/GPU — plenty of headroom\" — and OOMs at sequence length 8192, batch 4. Their arithmetic isn't wrong about what it counts. What did it not count, what's the actual ledger, and what are the two fixes (one from A1, one from A5)?","back":"FSDP shards params/grads/optimizer STATE — it does not shard ACTIVATIONS: the tape (A1 §3) is per-GPU, proportional to LOCAL batch × seq × hidden × layers, untouched by sharding. Ledger: sharded state ≈ 26 GB ✓, plus the momentarily-gathered layer (~small), plus activations at seq 8192 batch 4 for a 13B (≈5120 hidden, 40 layers): tens of GB — attention's naive tape alone would be catastrophic; even flash-based, MLP/norm activations land ~30–45 GB → OOM. Fixes: activation checkpointing (A1 §3's recompute trade — store layer boundaries only, ~√-ish memory for ~30% recompute), and FlashAttention if not already on (A5: the N² tape term deleted). Sharding answers a STATE problem; tapes are a RECOMPUTE problem — different axes, different levers — The memory ledger has THREE independent columns — state (sharding's domain), tape (checkpointing/flash's domain), workspace/fragmentation (the small print) — and every OOM war-room that conflates them burns a day. The trap sentence \"their arithmetic isn't wrong about what it counts\" is the lesson: OOM math fails by omission, not calculation. TP (option 4) does shard activations along hidden — a real but heavyweight answer; the checkpoint+flash pair usually buys the same fit for one config line (cost ladders before architecture changes — 5.10 quiz 1's Amdahl discipline).","url":"track-a/lesson-07.html","title":"A7 Multi-GPU — NCCL, DDP, FSDP — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-113","front":"A dashboard shows your inference fleet's GPUs at 31% \"SM efficiency\" during pure-decode periods, and a cost-cutting initiative concludes \"we can triple load before buying hardware.\" Using a8-phases and the decode roofline, explain why that 31% may already be FULL utilization, what number to check instead, and the one scenario where the initiative is actually right.","back":"Decode is memory-bound mat·vec: the SMs WAIT on weight/KV reads by physics (AI ≈ 1 FLOP/B vs a ~300 FLOP/B ridge — compute units are idle at FULL memory throughput). Check SOL-Memory / achieved bandwidth instead (a10-metrics' honest row): if HBM is at 80–90% of peak during decode, the GPU is done — tripling load buys queueing, not throughput. The initiative is right only if BATCH can still grow: headroom = KV memory left (the envelope's 62 GB ledger) AND the bandwidth denominator not yet KV-dominated — i.e., the fix is bigger B (more streams amortizing the same reads), which raises tokens/s WITHOUT raising SM% much. Same dashboards, opposite conclusions, depending on which ceiling you believe you're under (4.7's only question) — The fleet-scale version of 5.10 quiz 2's regime reading: LOW compute utilization is the EXPECTED signature of a memory-bound phase, not waste — and \"efficiency\" initiatives that read SM% without the roofline regularly size fleets wrong in both directions. The correct instrument stack: bandwidth SOL for \"is the GPU done,\" KV-memory headroom for \"can B grow,\" queue depth for \"is load even arriving.\" Option 3 gestures at truth but forfeits the DIAGNOSTIC power — tok/s tells you what; the roofline tells you what to do.","url":"track-a/lesson-08.html","title":"A8 Inference — Quantization, KV-Cache, Batching, vLLM — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-114","front":"Two configs of the same 7B on the same H100: (A) batch 8, avg context 32k; (B) batch 64, avg context 2k. Same total live tokens (256k), same KV memory. A capacity planner treats them as equivalent. Compute enough of each's step economics to show they are NOT — which serves more tokens/s, which has better per-stream latency, and what third factor (attention kernel cost) seals it?","back":"Per decode step both read 14 GB of weights + ~256k·131KB ≈ 34 GB of KV → similar step TIME (~14–15 ms at 3.35 TB/s) — but (B) emits 64 tokens/step vs (A)'s 8: ~8× the tokens/s from the SAME bytes (the weight+KV read amortizes over B, and only B). Per-stream latency: both ~1/step-time ≈ 65–70 tok/s… (A)'s streams each get one token per step too — per-stream speeds are comparable; (B) simply serves 8× more users. Sealing factor: attention FLOPs/step scale with B·ctx — equal here — but (A)'s 32k contexts stress flash-kernel long-seq efficiency and (B)'s 64-way batch gives attention better parallelism (more query rows per kernel — 5.2's saturation); plus (A) suffers 32k-prefill head-of-line convoys (a8-batch). Verdict: (B) dominates throughput at equal memory; long-context (A) is a PRODUCT choice you pay ~8× per token for — and pricing that honestly is the planner's actual job — \"Same memory ≠ same economics\" is THE capacity-planning trap: memory is a capacity constraint, but throughput is a BANDWIDTH-AMORTIZATION function of B specifically. The tokens-per-byte-read framing (B tokens out per fixed read) collapses the whole analysis into one fraction — carry it into every pricing/SKU conversation. Padding (option 4) is irrelevant under paged KV + continuous batching — which is precisely what those two inventions were FOR.","url":"track-a/lesson-08.html","title":"A8 Inference — Quantization, KV-Cache, Batching, vLLM — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-115","front":"After enabling torch.compile in a serving worker, p50 improves 25% — but every ~40 minutes a request stalls 6–9 seconds, and the stalls correlate with unusual request sizes. Meanwhile a counter you wisely exported shows compiled-graph count growing without bound. Diagnose the mechanism, the eventual silent failure it's marching toward, and the two-layer fix.","back":"Guard-miss recompilation on the request path: each novel shape (odd batch size, unusual sequence length) misses every cached graph's guards → Dynamo recompiles (seconds) while the request waits — A4 quiz 2's tuner-stall, one level up the stack. The march: when the per-frame cache_size_limit is exceeded, dynamo stops compiling and SILENTLY runs eager — the 25% win evaporates fleet-wide with no error, only a slow drift in p50 (the counter you exported is the only witness). Fix, two layers: (1) shape discipline at the API boundary — bucket/pad request shapes to a small fixed set (A8's serving stacks do this natively) and mark_dynamic the batch dim so one symbolic graph absorbs it; (2) operational — warm all buckets at startup (readiness probe runs one request per key), alarm on recompile rate and on eager-fallback, exactly like a 5.4 sticky-error watchdog — The graph-count counter is the hero: recompile pathologies are invisible in averages and fatal in tails, and the SILENT eager-fallback ending is the part almost nobody knows until it bites (no exception is thrown — by design). The bucketing fix is the same shape-stabilization you met in A4 quiz 2 and A8's serving schedulers — three systems, one law: lazily-specializing machinery converts shape entropy into critical-path latency, so you spend entropy down BEFORE the hot path.","url":"track-a/lesson-09.html","title":"A9 torch.compile & TorchInductor — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-116","front":"A colleague benchmarks torch.compile on their research notebook: \"2.4 s eager vs 31 s compiled — compile is garbage.\" Their loop: fresh model per cell, 5 iterations, different batch size each iteration, one .item() print per step. List everything wrong with the measurement AND the honest prediction for their model if measured correctly (it's a small GNN: mostly scatter/gather ops with tiny dense layers).","back":"Measurement sins, in order: (1) compile cost (seconds) paid inside the timed region with only 5 iterations — amortization sabotaged (M1 L7's warmup law; the 31 s is mostly the compiler running); (2) a different shape per iteration → recompile EVERY step (quiz 1's pathology, maximized); (3) the .item() print is a graph break in the loop body — the captured region is shredded into islands (a9-breaks) so even the compiled steps barely fuse. Honest protocol: fixed/bucketed shapes, 50+ warmup steps, THEN time (A2 quiz 1's discipline). Honest prediction even then: MODEST gains — scatter/gather-dominated models are 5.7-gather-bound (memory-latency physics the compiler cannot re-index away) with little elementwise glue to fuse (a9-when's ledger): maybe 1.1–1.3× plus launch-overhead relief if kernels are tiny. \"Compile is garbage\" and \"compile is magic\" are the same error: skipping the ledger — The three sins map to this lesson's three mechanisms (amortization, guards, breaks) — a tidy diagnostic checklist for every \"compile made it slower\" report you'll ever receive (you will receive many). The GNN prediction is the deeper skill: the compiler automates A5's ledger but cannot repeal 5.7's physics — fusion helps glue, not gathers. max-autotune (option 3) would spend MORE compile time tuning kernels that were never the problem: a knob turned without a diagnosis (5.10's cardinal sin).","url":"track-a/lesson-09.html","title":"A9 torch.compile & TorchInductor — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-117","front":"Friday, 2 a.m.: after a rolling HOST driver \"upgrade\" (ops replaced several nodes with a different image), 30% of pods crashloop with CUDA init errors while 70% run fine; the canary — which ran on the newest nodes — was green. Reconstruct the failure, explain why the canary lied, and name the two controls that make this class of incident structurally impossible.","back":"Version-skew inversion: the replaced nodes came up with an OLDER (or differently-capped) driver than the fleet's floor, and containers pinned to a CUDA runtime NEWER than those drivers support fail at init — the a10-container invariant violated on a SUBSET. The canary lied by sampling bias: it scheduled onto the newest nodes (the compatible ones) — a canary validates the CARTESIAN PRODUCT only if it runs on every (image × node-class) combination, not a lucky corner. Controls: (1) preflight assert at pod start — container CUDA vs THIS host's driver, fail fast with a named error (the worked example's box, doing its job at t=0 instead of 2 a.m.); (2) fleet invariant as CI/admission policy — driver floor recorded, image builds gated on it, node pools labeled and scheduling constrained until upgraded (make the invariant a scheduler constraint, not a wiki page) — The deep content is the canary-coverage lesson: canaries sample a matrix (image × node × traffic-shape), and every green canary that \"lied\" was sampling one cell (A8's scenario had the traffic-shape version of this). Retries against a deterministic init failure (option 3) are the ops equivalent of 5.4 quiz 1's retry-the-corpse. The mechanism-first reconstruction — WHICH invariant, violated WHERE, hidden by WHAT sampling — is the incident-review skill this track has been building since \"read the report like a machine log\" (5.4).","url":"track-a/lesson-10.html","title":"A10 Deployment — Containers, Serving, Monitoring — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-118","front":"An autoscaler configured on \"GPU utilization > 80% for 3 min → add node\" produces a Monday-morning oscillation: nodes added, p99 WORSENS for ~10 minutes, utilization stays high, more nodes added, then mass scale-down at noon, repeat. Decompose the feedback loop's three broken links and design the replacement policy.","back":"Link 1 — the signal lies: decode is memory-bound; util% reads high regardless of headroom (a10-metrics; A8 quiz 1) — the scaler can't see real capacity. Link 2 — new nodes HURT before helping: cold pods pay compile+autotune+capture warmup on live traffic (A9 quiz 1's stalls) and steal batch density from warm pods (continuous batching thrives on FAT batches — splitting load across more half-empty workers RAISES per-token cost, A8 quiz 2's amortization law reversed), so p99 worsens → util stays high → scale again: positive feedback. Link 3 — scale-down triggers on the same blind signal, dumping capacity right before the next wave. Replacement: scale UP on queue depth + tokens-in-flight vs envelope capacity (arithmetic headroom, not busyness), gate pod readiness on the warmup contract (never route to cold), scale DOWN on sustained queue emptiness with drain-then-remove, and keep a warm-spare floor sized to traffic's known daily shape — A control loop is only as good as its sensor and its actuator model: here the sensor (util%) measures the wrong thing and the actuator (cold pod) has a NEGATIVE transient — textbook conditions for oscillation, no exotic bug required. The fix pattern (measure headroom arithmetically, make the actuator's transient non-negative via warmup gating) generalizes to every lazily-initialized system this track met: autotune, compile, CUDA graphs, and now fleets. Option 3 surrenders real elasticity; option 1 tunes a broken sensor.","url":"track-a/lesson-10.html","title":"A10 Deployment — Containers, Serving, Monitoring — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-119","front":"Interviewer: \"Your table shows +3% at K=4096. Why ship a custom kernel for 3%?\" — what's the complete answer, including the case where you DON'T ship?","back":"The 3% row isn't the product — the REGIME MAP is: at the epilogue-heavy shapes my serving workload actually runs (K≤1024 projections, the +22–52% rows), the win is large, and the dispatcher engages the kernel ONLY there, falling back to compile elsewhere — so the fleet pockets the big rows and never pays the wash. I don't ship if: the workload's shape histogram lives in the no-win regime (then the README's honest rows just saved us a maintenance liability), or the win doesn't survive the TOTAL cost — an owner, fixtures in CI, re-validation per torch/Triton upgrade (A3's scenario priced this). A kernel is an ASSET with a carrying cost; the table plus the shape histogram is the underwriting, and sometimes underwriting says no — which is a successful analysis, not a failed project — The trap is defending 3% (option 1's eagerness or option 3's escalation); the senior answer reframes: per-regime engagement + total-cost underwriting. Option 3 specifically: the 3% gap at K=4096 is Triton-GEMM-vs-cuBLAS efficiency (5.11's vendor-tuning moat) — CUDA won't cheaply close it either; the fix is not fighting cuBLAS where cuBLAS wins (5.11 quiz 1, third appearance, because interviewers love it). \"Sometimes the deliverable is the decision not to ship\" is a sentence that reads as maturity in exactly the rooms that decide levels.","url":"track-a/lesson-11.html","title":"A11 Capstone — The Fused Epilogue, Shipped — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-120","front":"Interviewer: \"Your backward saves the pre-activation z. A reviewer says 'recompute z in backward instead — you already have x, W, b; save nothing and win memory.' Adjudicate with numbers for M=N=4096, K=4096 fp16.\"","back":"Recomputing z means re-running the GEMM in backward: 137 GFLOP ≈ 4 ms extra per backward (T4) to avoid storing 33.5 MB — a terrible exchange HERE: activations-to-FLOPs ratios matter, and a matmul is the most expensive way known to reproduce a tensor (contrast A5's flash, where recompute won because the saved tensor was O(N²) and the recompute was cheap relative to the memory catastrophe). The ledger: save-z costs 33.5 MB of tape (identical to what eager's composition saved — no regression); recompute costs ~2× backward matmul time forever. Checkpointing logic (A1 §3) always asks: bytes saved vs FLOPs to regenerate — flash sat on one extreme, this op sits on the other, and the reviewer applied a slogan where a division was required. Counter-proposal that IS worth benching: save gelu'(z) directly as int8/bf16 (the derivative's range is [~-0.09, ~1.1] — quantization-friendly) if tape memory ever becomes the binding resource (A7 quiz 2's ledger decides, not fashion) — Option 4 is the subtle trap: unlike relu (A3 Ex.2b's legitimate output-mask trick), GELU's derivative is NOT recoverable from its output alone (gelu is not invertible cheaply near the origin's curvature; you'd need x anyway) — knowing WHICH activations admit the save-output trick is real currency. The division framework (bytes vs regeneration FLOPs) is A1 §3 + A3 P3 unified, and delivering it with the flash contrast shows you own the principle, not the instances. Option 3 doubles tape for zero benefit — \"to be safe\" is not a mechanism.","url":"track-a/lesson-11.html","title":"A11 Capstone — The Fused Epilogue, Shipped — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-121","front":"A colleague's reaction-diffusion sim \"randomly explodes — must be a race condition in the CUDA port.\" Same code, same inputs: some runs finish, some blow up to 1e30 around different steps. The chemistry has a fast decay term (rate λ ≈ 800/s) they consider \"boring background.\" Timestep: dt = 0.0024, chosen \"for accuracy of the slow dynamics.\" Diagnose, explain the run-to-run randomness WITHOUT a race, and give the two-branch fix.","back":"dt vs the FASTEST mode: the wall for the λ=800 term sits at h = 2/800 = 0.0025, and their 0.0024 is 4% under it — every step's amplification factor |1−λh| = 0.92 barely damps that mode, so any perturbation feeding it (varying initial noise, last-bit nondeterminism from 5.8-style atomics in the port!) can push the EFFECTIVE local rate over the edge in some runs and not others: stochastic-LOOKING explosion from a deterministic stability cliff grazed too closely. Fix, two branches: (a) explicit + smaller dt (safe margin below the wall: dt ≤ 1/λ_max is a healthy rule) — costs steps; (b) treat the stiff decay IMPLICITLY (it's \"boring\" precisely because it's fast — backward-Euler that one term: unconditionally stable, one extra divide) and keep dt sized for the slow dynamics you actually care about — the standard IMEX pattern. Sanitizer's fine to run (option 1 isn't wrong to CHECK), but the λ-audit takes 60 seconds and names the wall exactly — The tell is \"boring background fast term + dt chosen for the slow physics\" — stiffness in one sentence. Grazing a stability boundary produces exactly the intermittent, input-sensitive explosions people blame on races and hardware (and the CUDA port's benign last-bit wobble becomes the trigger, which is why it \"started when we moved to GPU\"!). The 60-second audit — list every rate/eigenvalue in the system, compute 2/λ_max, compare dt — belongs at the top of every \"sim explodes\" runbook, before any debugger opens.","url":"track-b/lesson-01.html","title":"B1 Numerical Calculus from Scratch — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-122","front":"To \"future-proof accuracy,\" a teammate switches the gradient checks and Jacobian builds in your optimizer from central differences at h=1e-6 to h=1e-12 (\"a million times more accurate!\"), in fp64. Checks start failing on known-good code, and the optimizer's convergence degrades. Explain both symptoms quantitatively, and state the actual rule for choosing h.","back":"They walked off the V's right side: central-difference error = truncation O(h²) + cancellation O(eps/h). At h=1e-6, terms ≈ 1e-12 + 1e-10 — fine; at h=1e-12: truncation ~1e-24 but cancellation ≈ eps/h = 2.2e-16/1e-12 ≈ 2e-4 — the \"million times more accurate\" h delivers FOUR significant digits, worse than fp32! Gradient checks on good code now fail their 1e-7 tolerances (measuring noise, A1 quiz 2's teammate reborn), and the optimizer's Jacobians carry 1e-4-level noise → search directions wobble → degraded convergence. The rule: minimize the sum — h* ≈ (3·eps)^(1/3)·scale ≈ 1e-5·max(1,|x|) for fp64 central (the measured dip's location, derived); one-sided differences want h* ≈ √eps ≈ 1e-8. Print the V for YOUR f once; trust the dip, not intuition about smallness — The arithmetic IS the answer: eps/h at the proposed h gives 2e-4 in one division — no simulation needed to reject the change (though the V-plot settles arguments with people who don't divide). Option 4 treats the symptom by destroying the test's power (a 1e-3-tolerance gradcheck catches almost nothing — A1's fp32 lesson). This bug ships constantly because \"smaller h = more accurate\" feels like calculus; the V is the correction calculus never taught.","url":"track-b/lesson-01.html","title":"B1 Numerical Calculus from Scratch — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-123","front":"A group ports their solver and reports: \"cuBLAS is broken — our GEMM-based routine returns transposed garbage, so we added explicit transpose kernels before and after every call; now it's correct but 40% slower than expected, and memory usage spiked.\" Write the code review that fixes correctness, speed, and memory in one change, and name the fixture that would have prevented the whole arc.","back":"The transposed \"garbage\" was the column-major view (b2-colmajor): their row-major buffers were REINTERPRETED, not corrupted. The fix deletes all transpose kernels and swaps operand order per call — gemm(B, A) yields (A·B) in row-major bytes via Cᵀ=BᵀAᵀ: zero copies, zero extra memory, full library speed. Their patch was correct-by-brute-force: two O(N²) transpose passes per GEMM (the 40% at their sizes — 5.7's transpose cost, self-inflicted twice) plus transpose buffers (the memory spike). The fixture: the 3×2·2×4 hand-checked product on day one — non-square ON PURPOSE, because square test matrices make transposition bugs shape-legal and often value-plausible (5.11's gotcha; A1 P6's square-matrix trap, third sighting) — The identity-vs-copy distinction is THE cuBLAS onboarding moment, and teams re-discover it expensively every year (option 4 re-invents what the identity gets free; a \"row-major flag\" (option 1) exists in some wrappers precisely by applying this identity underneath — knowing the mechanism means you're never hostage to a wrapper). Review-comment form matters too: fix + mechanism + the missing fixture, three sentences (A5 P5's format — teach, don't gatekeep).","url":"track-b/lesson-02.html","title":"B2 cuBLAS, cuSPARSE & cuSOLVER — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-124","front":"Two matrices, both N=100k, both from real problems: (a) a 3D Poisson operator, ~7 nnz/row; (b) a covariance matrix from finance, dense but \"we could threshold small entries to 30% density and use cuSPARSE — sparse is faster, right?\" For each: the storage and SpMV/GEMV cost arithmetic, the verdict, and — for (b) — the numerics objection that outranks the performance one.","back":"(a) 7 nnz/row = 0.007% density: CSR ≈ 100k×7×8 B ≈ 5.6 MB vs dense 40 GB — sparse by four orders in storage, and SpMV ≈ bandwidth over 5.6 MB: the only sane option (this is the b2-sparse sweet spot: PDE matrices). (b) 30% density: the measured crossover says sparse LOSES at 50% and barely wins at 10% on CPU — at 30% on GPU (gather tax) expect ≈ parity-to-loss, while CSR storage (30%×(4+4)B vs dense 4B) is only 40% smaller: performance verdict — keep it dense, use GEMV/GEMM at full library speed. The outranking objection: THRESHOLDING CHANGED THE MATRIX — a covariance matrix's small entries carry correlation structure; zeroing them can break positive-definiteness (Cholesky fails or, worse, \"succeeds\" on a matrix that no longer models the portfolio — silently wrong risk numbers, M1 L8's budget question asked about the MODEL, not the floats). Approximation decisions belong to the science, not the storage layer — Two matrices, opposite verdicts, one method: density arithmetic + crossover data + a numerics veto. The (b) trap is endemic — \"sparsify for speed\" pitched as a free lunch when it's (i) usually not faster above ~10–20% and (ii) never free mathematically: any thresholding is a MODEL change requiring domain sign-off (the B-track's version of A8's quantization-needs-evals doctrine). Format choice (option 4) is a second-order question that only opens AFTER density says sparse at all.","url":"track-b/lesson-02.html","title":"B2 cuBLAS, cuSPARSE & cuSOLVER — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-125","front":"A group's production climate-adjacent run \"went unstable after 11 days of wall-clock\" — millions of healthy steps, then NaN in hours. The team suspects hardware (ECC logs clean) or a rare data race. The config shows dt was set to 0.995× the CFL limit \"to maximize throughput.\" Explain the 11-day fuse quantitatively, exonerate the hardware, and give the two-line fix with its honest cost.","back":"At 0.995×CFL the worst mode's amplification is |1−λdt| ≈ just barely... UNDER 1 in exact arithmetic — but the CFL limit is derived for the IDEALIZED operator: variable coefficients, boundary rows (B1 P1's one-sided stencils have different stability radii!), or a slightly deeper local α push the EFFECTIVE local limit below the global setting in a few cells → those cells' checkerboard mode grows at ×(1+ε) per step with ε ~ 1e-6-class → e^(εN) needs millions of steps to surface: the 11-day fuse is exp incubation from round-off seeds, exactly the measured 200-vs-2000-step demo stretched by a tiny ε. Hardware exonerated by mechanism: the growth is deterministic and reproducible at the same step count (rerun and see). Fix: dt at 0.8–0.9× the AUDITED limit (min over local coefficients AND boundary stencils) — costs ~10–20% more steps, deletes the failure class; or go implicit for the diffusion term (B1 §2) and retire the wall entirely — The teaching is the FUSE ARITHMETIC: margin ε to the wall sets the incubation time as ~1/ε steps — 4% over blows in 2000 steps (measured), 0.1%-class effective violations take millions: \"stable for days then explodes\" is a signature, not a mystery. The 0.995 config line is the smoking gun reviewers should challenge on sight: stability margins exist because the textbook wall is for the textbook operator. Clamps (option 4) would hide the symptom and corrupt the physics silently — strictly worse than the crash.","url":"track-b/lesson-03.html","title":"B3 Stencils & Grid Codes — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-126","front":"Your halo-exchanged multi-GPU stencil passes conservation and spread-law fixtures at 1 GPU, but at 4 GPUs conservation drifts by 1e-9/step and a symmetric initial condition develops a faint seam along subdomain boundaries. Rank the three classic culprits, name the fixture that localizes each, and explain why the drift is 1e-9 rather than obviously wrong.","back":"Culprits, base-rate order: (1) halo off-by-one — an edge column updated from a stale or shifted neighbor value (the seam!): localized by the SYMMETRY fixture per-subdomain (asymmetry appears exactly at the guilty edge) and by a 2-GPU minimal repro with a ramp initial condition (values encode positions — 3.4 Ex.2's recognizable-pattern doctrine); (2) double-counted or skipped flux at the boundary between owned and halo cells (conservation's 1e-9: ONE row of cells mis-accounted among millions ≈ row/total magnitude — the drift SIZE is the cell-count arithmetic, which is why it's subtle: 4096 cells wrong of 16.7M ≈ 2.4e-4 of the field's smallest scales × step size); (3) exchange/compute ordering race — computing edges before the halo lands (5.9's dependency, B5's tag/order rules): localized by forcing synchronous exchange (overlap OFF) — drift vanishes → the async path's ordering is guilty. All three are YOUR bugs; the fabric (option 1) doesn't round — The drift-magnitude arithmetic is the senior move: 1e-9/step isn't \"small noise,\" it's ~one-row-of-cells-per-step mis-accounted — a COUNTABLE bug, not a numerical mood (option 3 confuses 5.8's last-bit reduction wobble, ~1e-16-class, with a 7-orders-larger bookkeeping error: budgets exist so you can tell mechanism from mood). The localization ladder — symmetry per subdomain, ramp patterns, sync-vs-async A/B — converts a \"spooky parallel bug\" into an afternoon. Every multi-GPU stencil team meets this exact bug; now you'll meet it armed.","url":"track-b/lesson-03.html","title":"B3 Stencils & Grid Codes — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-127","front":"A risk team scales their GPU Monte Carlo from 8 to 512 workers for a regulatory deadline. Throughput scales beautifully; the final VaR number's error bar, computed as always from sample variance, SHRINKS on schedule — but backtesting later shows the estimate was far outside its own confidence interval, badly. The RNG line: curand_init(42 + worker_id % 16, 0, 0, &state) . Reconstruct the failure, explain why the error bar LIED, and give the two-line fix plus the fixture that catches this class forever.","back":"The seed arithmetic wraps at 16: workers 0–15 get distinct seeds, but worker 16 repeats worker 0's ENTIRE sequence, 17 repeats 1's… — 512 workers = 16 independent streams × 32 exact copies. Effective N is 1/32 of nominal, but the sample-variance calculation can't see it: duplicated samples don't inflate the spread (they're legitimate draws — just SHARED), so s/√N_nominal reports a bar √32 ≈ 5.7× too tight: the estimate is honest-ish, the CONFIDENCE is fiction — the worst failure mode in regulated statistics. Fix: curand_init(seed, GLOBAL_worker_id, 0, &state) — Philox subsequences are disjoint by construction for every distinct id, no modulo, no arithmetic cleverness (b4-streams' theorem doing the work). Fixture: the cross-stream correlation audit (pairwise corr on a sample from every worker — measured 1.0 catches copies instantly) PLUS the calibration test from the worked example: across 100 seeds, the truth must land in the 95% bar ~95 times — 60/100 flags this bug without ever reading the RNG code — The % 16 is a real-world bug shape (a modulo meant for something else, a copy-paste from a 16-GPU config) — and its cruelty is that MORE hardware made the statistics WORSE while every dashboard improved. The mechanism to internalize: duplicated streams shrink effective N invisibly to variance-based bars, because the bar formula ASSUMES independence — the one assumption the bug broke. Calibration fixtures test the assumption itself, which is why B8 makes them mandatory for anything reported with a ±.","url":"track-b/lesson-04.html","title":"B4 Monte Carlo & cuRAND — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-128","front":"Fresh from this lesson, a colleague applies antithetic pairing to their photon-transport code (payoff: 1 if the photon escapes a slab, 0 if absorbed — a jagged, non-monotone function of the driving uniforms) and reports \"variance reduced 1.7×… sometimes, and 0.95× on other configs.\" Then they propose Sobol sequences \"for the 1/N rate\" on the same 60-dimensional discontinuous problem. Set expectations with mechanisms, and redirect to the two techniques that DO fit this problem.","back":"Both proposals ignore their WHY. Antithetic's 62× came from MONOTONE f (f(U) and f(1−U) anti-correlate — b4-variance's fine print); an escape/absorb indicator is a step-function tangle of the uniforms: pairing buys corr ≈ 0 (their 1.7×/0.95× wobble is noise around 'nothing'). Sobol's near-1/N needs SMOOTH integrands and effective low dimension; on a 60-d discontinuous indicator it degrades toward plain MC (and its error estimation gets harder — you pay complexity for nothing). What fits transport: (1) importance sampling — sample the paths that MATTER (bias toward escape-likely trajectories, reweight by the likelihood ratio): the transport field's workhorse, variance reductions of 10–1000× on rare-event tallies; (2) stratification on the FIRST decisive dimensions (entry angle/depth) where the payoff's structure lives, plus Russian roulette/splitting for path population control — the neutron-transport classics. Rule: variance reduction is a THEOREM with hypotheses; check the hypotheses or measure noise — The 62× was real AND conditional — this quiz is the price of teaching by measurement: learners over-generalize the number and skip the hypothesis (monotonicity). The redirect matters as much as the rejection: importance sampling is where transport codes live (and its likelihood-ratio machinery is exactly what B8 will audit for weight-degeneracy). \"Technique X reduced variance 62×\" is always shorthand for \"…on integrands satisfying its hypotheses\" — reading the fine print is the difference between a method and a slogan.","url":"track-b/lesson-04.html","title":"B4 Monte Carlo & cuRAND — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-129","front":"A student's 2D halo code works on 4 ranks, hangs on 16 — sometimes. Pattern: every rank does MPI_Send(north) then MPI_Send(south)… then the Recvs. On 4 ranks (2×2, periodic) it \"always worked.\" Explain the deadlock mechanism, why 4 ranks got lucky, why 16 is INTERMITTENT, and the three standard fixes ranked by robustness.","back":"All ranks Send before any Recv: if Sends block (no buffer space / rendezvous protocol for larger messages), everyone waits for a receiver who's also sending — a dependency cycle (5.5's barrier law as a graph: A7 gotcha 3's shape). 4 ranks got lucky because small halos rode the EAGER protocol (library buffers small messages and returns — a favor, not a contract); at 16 ranks the 2D domain's halos may cross the eager/rendezvous threshold OR buffer pools fill under more simultaneous messages — and \"sometimes\" is exactly what protocol-threshold luck looks like (message size, timing, and pool state vary run to run). Fixes, by robustness: (1) Isend/Irecv all, then Waitall — no ordering assumptions at all, and it's the overlap idiom you want anyway; (2) MPI_Sendrecv — the library pairs the exchange deadlock-free; (3) odd-even ordering (evens send first) — correct but fragile under refactor (a discipline, not a structure). Raising buffers (option 1) converts a deterministic design bug into a rarer one — the worst trade in debugging — \"Works small, hangs big, intermittently\" is THE eager-threshold signature — one of a handful of distributed fingerprints worth memorizing (with A7 P4's stuck-collective and B3 quiz 2's seam). The deeper law: correctness that depends on library BUFFERING is luck wearing a green test suite; the Isend/Irecv+Waitall idiom removes the assumption class entirely, which is why the worked example's r2 rung standardizes it. Fingerprint + mechanism + idiom — that's the whole distributed-debugging trade in one quiz.","url":"track-b/lesson-05.html","title":"B5 MPI + CUDA, NCCL & GPUDirect — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-130","front":"Your CUDA-aware halo exchange benchmarks at 2.8 GB/s on nodes with 25 GB/s InfiniBand and NVLink'd GPUs. MPI_Send with device pointers \"works fine\" (correct results). Rank the four hypotheses, give each one's 60-second test, and state which single command settles most of them.","back":"Ranked: (1) the MPI module isn't CUDA-aware/UCX-CUDA built → silent host staging (correct-but-4-hop — b5-aware's fallback): test with ompi_info / ucx_info -d for CUDA transports, or the killer: the SAME benchmark with HOST buffers — if host hits 22 GB/s and device hits 2.8, staging is convicted; (2) GPUDirect RDMA unavailable (driver/nv_peer_mem/fabric config) → pipelined staging: better than naive but far from wire — ucx_info shows gdr_copy/cuda_ipc presence; (3) affinity: rank's GPU and NIC on different NUMA/PCIe domains → every byte crosses the socket (4.6's topology tax): test with binding report (--report-bindings) + nvidia-smi topo -m; (4) actual fabric issue — LAST (fabrics fail loudly; misconfigs fail quietly at 2.8 GB/s). The settling command: the device-vs-host osu_bw pair — two numbers that partition the whole hypothesis space in one minute — \"Works fine (correct) but slow\" is the CUDA-aware trap's signature: the API contract is honored either way, the PATH is the variable — exactly like A3 quiz 1's default-stream op (fast standalone, poison in context): correctness tests can't see path bugs; bandwidth tests are the fixture. Option 4 is checkable in the same sweep (bandwidth-vs-size curve: small messages are latency-bound — 4.6's tolls — but 2.8 GB/s FLAT across sizes is a path ceiling, not a toll). The topology check (option 3's nvidia-smi topo -m) becomes routine on every new cluster — 10 seconds that explain years of mysteries.","url":"track-b/lesson-05.html","title":"B5 MPI + CUDA, NCCL & GPUDirect — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-131","front":"A Fortran climate module's first OpenACC port runs 74× SLOWER than the CPU original. The team concludes \"our code isn't GPU-friendly\" and shelves the effort. The profile (which nobody opened) would have shown 96% of time in cuMemcpyHtoD/DtoH, kernels totaling 0.9%. Write the rescue: mechanism, the one-line-class fix, the expected outcome arithmetic, and the organizational lesson about WHERE that profile should have been in the decision.","back":"The signature is b6-map's disaster verbatim: no enclosing data region, so every parallel loop's operands round-trip the bridge per invocation — 96% memcpy time IS the diagnosis (5.10's WHERE, unread). Fix: one data region (or unstructured enter/exit data for messy lifetimes) hoisting residency outside the time loop, plus create() on intermediates; expected outcome by arithmetic: kernels were 0.9% of 74×-slower ≈ comparable-to-CPU compute, and stencil-class loops then land near the bandwidth ceiling → tens-of-× FASTER than CPU, not 74× slower — the staged demo measured 89× from this one line. Organizational lesson: the profile comes BEFORE the verdict, always — \"not GPU-friendly\" is a claim about regimes (4.7) that a timeline falsifies in five minutes, and shelving a port on an unprofiled 74× is deciding a hardware strategy on a typo — This exact arc — catastrophic first port, verdict pronounced, profile unopened — is the most common way institutions wrongly conclude \"GPUs don't work for us\" (the B6 twin of 5.7's \"not GPU-shaped\" scenario). The memcpy-dominated profile is the single most recognizable signature in all of directive computing; pattern-matching it from the symptom description alone (74×, no profile) is the fluency bar. Option 4 has a real cousin (tiny arrays ARE launch/toll-bound) but 96% memcpy convicts the data region, not the size.","url":"track-b/lesson-06.html","title":"B6 OpenMP & OpenACC Offload — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-132","front":"After the fix, the same module runs 31× over CPU — but a hand-CUDA rewrite of its dominant kernel benchmarks 1.18× faster than the directive version. A new team member proposes rewriting all 14 offloaded kernels in CUDA \"since CUDA is clearly faster.\" Arbitrate with the 90/10 curve, Amdahl, and the maintenance ledger — and name the ONE kernel class where you'd approve the rewrite.","back":"Amdahl first: if the dominant kernel is 40% of runtime, 1.18× on it buys 1.06× end-to-end; across all 14 (assuming similar margins) maybe 1.15× total — for 14 kernels' worth of CUDA to own forever (A3's maintenance scenario: every arch bump, every new hire, every review now bilingual). The 90/10 curve says WHY the margin is small: these are bandwidth-bound physics loops — both authors sit at the same 220 GB/s ceiling, and 18% is tiling/launch-shape crumbs, not a regime change [worked ex]. The ledger: 1.15× vs permanent bilingual maintenance of the code that was ported with directives PRECISELY to avoid that. Verdict: keep directives; revisit per-kernel WHEN the profile shows a kernel whose ceiling responds to craft. The approvable class: a kernel with real REUSE structure the compiler can't exploit — a 5.5-style tiling opportunity (small matrix products per cell, flux kernels with shared operands) or an A5-style fusion with algebraic restructuring — where hand CUDA buys 2–5×, not 1.18×: THERE the interop seam earns its keep, one kernel at a time (b6-ninety's dashed line, crossed with evidence) — The three-instrument arbitration (Amdahl for the ceiling, the regime curve for the mechanism, the maintenance ledger for the cost) is this course's standard governance move — 5.10 quiz 1 and A11 quiz 1 ran the same play. \"Purity\" (option 3) is as unserious as \"rewrite everything\": the interop seam exists so decisions can be per-kernel and evidence-priced. The 2–5×-not-1.18× threshold for approving hand kernels is a good default number to carry into meetings.","url":"track-b/lesson-06.html","title":"B6 OpenMP & OpenACC Offload — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-133","front":"Two users submit equivalent 16-GPU studies. User A: one job, --time=7-00:00:00 (\"covers everything\"), all 25 configurations sequentially inside. User B: a 25-element array, --time=03:30:00 each, from a pilot. A waits 6 days in queue and loses everything at a node failure on day 5; B finishes in 31 hours wall-clock including queue. Decompose B's advantage into the three scheduler/fault mechanisms at work.","back":"(1) Backfill geometry: 3.5 h × 16-GPU rectangles slot into the schedule's gaps around big reservations; a 7-DAY rectangle must wait for a contiguous week-long hole — the queue penalizes the SHAPE of the ask, not just its size. (2) Fault blast radius: A's monolith couples 25 configs to one node's MTBF — day-5 failure kills 100+ GPU-days; B's failure domain is ONE 3.5 h element, auto-requeued (--requeue or resubmit): the array is checkpointing at the JOB level. (3) Parallelism the monolith forfeited: B's elements run CONCURRENTLY as slots open (25 × 3.5 h of work in ~31 h wall) while A serialized by construction. Same science, same total GPU-hours asked — the difference is entirely in how schedulers price shape, couple failures, and exploit independence (B4's embarrassing parallelism, applied to the QUEUE itself) — The three mechanisms are independent and compounding — and none is luck. \"Structure work as small, restartable, independent units and the queue becomes your ally\" is the entire operational philosophy of shared clusters; it's also, not coincidentally, the checkpoint philosophy (bounded loss units) and the B4 philosophy (independent samples) — the same design instinct at three scales. Option 3 anthropomorphizes a bin-packing algorithm; the algorithm just packs rectangles.","url":"track-b/lesson-07.html","title":"B7 Clusters — SLURM, Modules, Apptainer — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-134","front":"A postdoc's runs are mysteriously 3× slower than last month, only during business hours, and only for jobs that write per-step diagnostics. I/O shows each step appending 40 KB × 512 ranks to 512 separate files… on $HOME. Explain the full mechanism (why slow, why business hours, why it throttles OTHERS), and design the correct I/O architecture in three moves.","back":"Mechanism: $HOME is a small shared NFS-class server (metadata-weak, everyone's dotfiles and editors live there) — 512 ranks × per-step small appends = a metadata/IOPS storm (tolls, not bandwidth: 4.6's law on a filesystem), saturating the server. Business hours: contention with every logged-in human's interactive I/O — the postdoc's job and the site's PRODUCTIVITY throttle each other (this is how you get an email from admins, and why it's etiquette, not just performance). Architecture, three moves: (1) diagnostics to $SCRATCH (parallel FS built for exactly this) or better, node-local NVMe with end-of-job collation; (2) AGGREGATE: one file per node (or MPI-IO/HDF5 collective writes) instead of 512 file streams — the message-aggregation law (B5 P4) applied to storage tolls; (3) reduce frequency by design: per-step 40 KB diagnostics are usually a debugging habit — buffer in memory, flush every N steps (5.9's logging lesson, filesystem edition). Result: I/O time vanishes from the profile AND the admin email never arrives — Filesystem incidents are toll incidents: small-file metadata storms are 4.6's fixed-cost lesson wearing POSIX clothes, and the fixes are the same three levers as always — right tier, aggregation, frequency. The business-hours signature (contention with humans) is the tell that distinguishes shared-infrastructure problems from code problems; recognizing shared-fate signatures is the cluster-citizenship skill this lesson exists to install. Run-at-night (option 3) treats the symptom while remaining the problem.","url":"track-b/lesson-07.html","title":"B7 Clusters — SLURM, Modules, Apptainer — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-135","front":"A reviewer reruns your published simulation with your container and gets results differing at 1e-13 — and requests rejection for \"irreproducibility.\" Your runs used 64 ranks; theirs used 96. Write the response that saves the paper, and the one thing you wish past-you had done to make this a non-event.","back":"The response: different rank counts partition the global reductions differently → different summation ORDERS → last-bits differences amplified through κ steps — a documented property of finite-precision parallel arithmetic (A7 §2, 5.8), not an implementation defect; both results are equally valid evaluations. Then the quantitative core: our stated budget from κ·n·eps is 1e-12/step; the observed 1e-13 sits INSIDE the contract — and here is the twin-run table (8/64/96 ranks) demonstrating the spread is stable and budget-bounded, plus the rung-1 offer: at 64 ranks with the recorded seeds/env, our results reproduce BITWISE (please verify — that's the falsifiable claim). The wish: that the ORIGINAL methods section had contained the worked example's paragraph — rung named, budget derived, twin-runs cited — so the reviewer's 1e-13 would have read as CONFIRMATION of the contract instead of a red flag. Papers with the paragraph turn this exchange into one sentence; papers without it turn it into a revision cycle or worse — \"Obviously negligible\" (option 3) loses because it's an assertion where the field demands a DERIVATION — the whole point of budgets is replacing taste with κ-arithmetic; reviewers accept contracts and reject vibes. The rung-1 bitwise offer is the power move: it demonstrates you know EXACTLY which reproducibility you promised and can deliver it on demand. This exchange — nearly verbatim — happens across computational science weekly; the prepared version takes a paragraph, the unprepared version has taken careers.","url":"track-b/lesson-08.html","title":"B8 Precision & Reproducibility — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-136","front":"Migrating a fp64 ocean model to GPUs, a performance engineer proposes: fp32 fields (\"bandwidth-bound — B3 says 2×!\"), keep fp64 \"only where it matters,\" and -Ofast \"since we're optimizing anyway.\" Design the evaluation that answers this properly: the three experiments, their oracles, and the two components you already KNOW will need the fp64 spine before running anything.","back":"Known-before-running (from measured facts): (1) the LEDGERS/reductions — fp32 accumulation lost 96% of a 65k-sum's mass (A6) and naive-fp32 hit 5.2e-5 at 1e7 elements: conservation tracking dies in fp32 → fp64 (or Kahan-fp32) accumulators regardless of field precision; (2) anything downstream of large κ — the pressure/implicit solve (B2 §2: κ~1e6+ makes fp32 solutions noise) → fp64 solve or mixed-precision refinement. And -Ofast is struck IMMEDIATELY (it bundles fast-math: the executed Kahan deletion + reassociated ledgers — a numerics-flag veto, not a negotiation). The three experiments: (a) twin runs fp32-fields vs fp64 at three resolutions, oracled by B3's physics fixtures (conservation drift, spread-law, spectra) with budgets from the science team — does fp32 stay inside them and for how many simulated years?; (b) the V-floor check (B1): fine-grid Laplacians difference near-equal neighbors — measure where fp32's 7 digits start eating the stencil (resolution-dependent verdict, not global); (c) the long-horizon drift race — the error that decides climate-class runs is the TREND, only visible in extended twins. Verdict shape: likely fp32 fields + fp64 spine passes for engineering horizons and fails for climate horizons — precision is bought per-COMPONENT per-HORIZON, by experiment (A6's per-tensor-role logic, science edition) — The skeleton — two vetoes from measured facts, three experiments with physics oracles, per-component verdicts — is the professional template for every precision-migration debate (and they're constant now that fp64 GPU throughput carries a price premium). Option 1's absolutism forfeits real 2× wins that (a)-style experiments routinely validate for bounded horizons; option 3 skips the experiments that make the win TRUSTWORTHY. The -Ofast veto deserves its immediacy: it's the one proposal element with an executed counterexample in hand.","url":"track-b/lesson-08.html","title":"B8 Precision & Reproducibility — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-137","front":"A spectral-solver team reports: \"cuFFT is 40× slower than the benchmark claims.\" Their loop: per timestep, for each of 2,048 rows — cufftPlan1d, cufftExecC2C, cufftDestroy. Also, results are 8192× too large after a forward+inverse round trip (\"we patched it with a magic divide\"). Diagnose both symptoms, give the four-line fix, and name the two lessons they skipped.","back":"Symptom 1: plan-per-call — planning is the EXPENSIVE phase (algorithm selection + workspace: b9-fft's whole point), and they pay it 2,048× per step, plus 2,048 separate tiny launches (5.2's floor). Fix: ONE cufftPlanMany with batch=2048, created at startup, stream-set, executed per step — plan cost amortized to zero, one launch for all rows: expect ~the benchmark's speed (their 40× was ~plan-cost ÷ exec-cost, exactly). Symptom 2: cuFFT is UNNORMALIZED — forward+inverse multiplies by N=8192 by DESIGN; the \"magic divide\" is the correct 1/N placed superstitiously instead of contractually: the round-trip fixture (b9-parseval) would have named the convention on day one, and the scaling belongs in ONE documented place (their epilogue kernel — A11's fusion instinct), not a patch. Skipped lessons: 5.3's alloc-once (plans are allocations) and M2 L6's fixture-the-seam (conventions are seams) — Plan-per-call is cuFFT's version of cublasCreate-per-call (B2 gotcha 2) and cudaMalloc-in-loops (5.3) — the SAME law surfacing in every library, which is why the course drills laws over APIs. The unnormalized convention bites every first integration; the difference between a \"magic divide\" and a contract line is whether the NEXT person trusts it (3.6's comment discipline). Option 1's \"write custom kernels\" would burn a quarter rediscovering Cooley–Tukey at 0.3× cuFFT — the anti-B2 move.","url":"track-b/lesson-09.html","title":"B9 Thrust, CUB & cuFFT — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-138","front":"Your particle code uses thrust::sort_by_key every step to bucket 50M particles by cell (for B3-style neighbor interactions). Profiling shows sort = 61% of step time. A teammate says \"sort is O(N log N), it's optimal, nothing to do.\" Attack the premise three ways — the primitive swap, the algorithmic insight about YOUR data, and the frequency question — with expected magnitudes.","back":"(1) Primitive swap: for integer cell keys, GPU radix sort is O(N) not O(N log N) (thrust already radixes integer keys — but cub::DeviceRadixSort with bit-range restriction (only log₂(ncells) bits!) and double-buffering beats the generic path ~1.5–2×). (2) The data insight: particles MOVE SLOWLY — each step's ordering is 95%+ presorted, and full re-sorting ignores it: bucket-repair strategies (scan-based compaction of the few migrants — the worked example's DeviceSelect! — into their new cells) cost O(N + moved·log) ≈ near-memcpy vs full sort: 3–5× on the stage. (3) Frequency: does bucketing need EVERY step? If particles cross cells every ~10 steps on average, sort every k steps with a halo-margin on neighbor lists (B3's ghost-ring idea, temporal edition) — ÷k on the stage for a bounded staleness the physics tolerates (ask the science, B8's budget discipline). Stacked: 61% → ~10–15% of step time. 'Asymptotically optimal' is not 'situationally optimal' — the data's structure (slow movement) was the resource the generic call couldn't see — Three attack angles — better primitive configuration, exploit data structure, question the frequency — form the standard triage for ANY dominant library call (they're 5.10's loop specialized to primitives). The presortedness insight is the deep one: libraries optimize for adversarial inputs; your simulation's inputs are CORRELATED ACROSS TIME, and that temporal coherence is exploitable structure (the B-track's version of A8's prefix-caching). \"It's optimal\" ended the teammate's analysis exactly where the interesting engineering begins.","url":"track-b/lesson-09.html","title":"B9 Thrust, CUB & cuFFT — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-139","front":"A reviewer (or interviewer): \"Your strong-scaling efficiency collapses to 51% at 64 ranks — why should we accept a method that scales this poorly?\" Deliver the answer that turns the \"weakness\" into the report's strongest section.","back":"The 51% was PREDICTED at 50% before any run: strong scaling divides a fixed 4096² grid until each rank holds 512² — where the halo's surface/volume ratio and per-message latency make comm ≈ compute (the two-lane arithmetic in §3, committed to the repo before the study — the git log shows it). This isn't the method scaling poorly; it's the PROBLEM SIZE exhausting its parallelism, on schedule — the same physics that gives 97% WEAK scaling at identical rank counts (the curve directly above it, same hardware, same code). The report's guidance follows: for THIS grid, 16 ranks is the efficiency knee (arithmetic provided); for more ranks, grow the problem — and the model that called the cliff within one point is now a validated capacity-planning tool, which is worth more than the missing 49%. Accepting arithmetic's verdict and PREDICTING it is the difference between a scaling study and a scaling advertisement — Option 4 is the field's quiet scandal (unlabeled or omitted strong-scaling curves) and reviewers increasingly hunt for it; option 1 concedes a defect that doesn't exist. The winning move — prediction committed first, cliff arriving on schedule, knee arithmetic offered as guidance — converts Amdahl's law from embarrassment into demonstrated mastery. This exact exchange decides HPC interviews: the candidate who NEEDS 90% everywhere doesn't understand scaling; the one who predicts the 50% does.","url":"track-b/lesson-10.html","title":"B10 Capstone — The Scaling Study — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-140","front":"\"Your conservation ledger drifts 2e-16 per step. Over my planned 10⁹-step production run, that's potentially 2e-7 — real drift. Is your solver fit for my purpose?\" — the questioner runs century-scale climate integrations. Answer with the ladder, the mechanism, and the honest escalation path.","back":"The question is exactly right to ask, and the answer has three parts. Mechanism: the 2e-16/step is fp64 pairwise-summation wobble (B8's measured rung), and its ACCUMULATION law matters — random-sign rounding walks as ~√N·eps ≈ 3e-11 over 10⁹ steps, not linearly to 2e-7… but only if the ledger's errors stay unbiased: systematic flux-bookkeeping bias accumulates LINEARLY, which is why the fixture distinguishes them (twin runs + sign statistics on the drift — B8 Ex.2's machinery). Fitness: for 10⁹ steps I'd escalate the ledger to compensated (Kahan) accumulation — measured O(eps) flat, deleting the growth question entirely for 4 flops/element on ONE reduction (B8's ladder: the escalation is a config line, and -ffast-math is already banned by the contract so it SURVIVES compilation). And the honest boundary: at your horizon, the ledger's arithmetic stops being the story — timestep truncation bias (B1's O(h) vs O(h⁴) per-step error, systematically signed!) dominates any summation wobble by orders: the fitness conversation belongs at the INTEGRATOR's order and the energy-drift character (B1's worked example), which my report's §5 prices but your production spec must choose. Fit for purpose = matched error BUDGETS at your horizon — here's the arithmetic; you own the horizon — The three-part shape — mechanism with the √N-vs-linear distinction, the cheap escalation (Kahan) already de-risked by the flag contract, and the honest redirect to the DOMINANT error term at that horizon — is what numerics maturity sounds like. Options 1 and 3 both dodge the budget arithmetic in opposite directions. Note the trap defused: the questioner's linear extrapolation (2e-7) was the WRONG model for unbiased rounding but the RIGHT paranoia for biased bookkeeping — honoring both halves is the credibility move.","url":"track-b/lesson-10.html","title":"B10 Capstone — The Scaling Study — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-141","front":"An artist reports that a stretched crate (scaled 3× along one axis) looks \"weirdly lit\" — highlights point the wrong way — while unstretched crates look fine. A programmer says \"lighting is lighting, the model matrix is applied to everything correctly.\" Diagnose it.","back":"The normals are being transformed by the model matrix M instead of its inverse-transpose (M⁻¹)ᵀ. For pure rotations/translations M⁻¹ᵀ = M so unstretched objects look fine, which is why the bug hid — but a non-uniform scale makes M·normal no longer perpendicular to the surface (executed: dot = 3, not 0), so the shading normal points wrong and highlights drift. Fix: build a separate normal matrix = (M⁻¹)ᵀ and use it for normals in the shader — Option 1 blames the asset, but the same mesh lights correctly when unstretched — so the data is fine and the transform is the culprit. Option 3 invents a hardware limit that doesn't exist. Option 4 confuses two unrelated bugs (sRGB is C5). The correct answer names the exact mechanism (normals need (M⁻¹)ᵀ) and, crucially, explains why it only appears under non-uniform scale — the \"it works on some objects\" clue is the fingerprint of the inverse-transpose bug, because M⁻¹ᵀ = M for rotations. This is the worked example as a field diagnosis.","url":"track-c/lesson-01.html","title":"C1 Graphics Math — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-142","front":"A junior engineer stores all rotations as Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll) and animates a camera by lerping the three angles between keyframes. Near straight-up shots the camera \"snaps\" and spins oddly. What's happening and what's the fix?","back":"Gimbal lock: near pitch = 90° (looking straight up) two of the three rotation axes align, collapsing a degree of freedom, so small changes produce large unexpected spins — and lerping Euler triples takes a non-shortest, wobbling path through orientation space. Fix: store rotations as quaternions and interpolate with slerp, which has no gimbal lock and follows the shortest constant-speed arc. Convert to a matrix only to hand the GPU the transform — Option 1 misattributes a structural problem to precision — the snap happens even in exact arithmetic. Options 3/4 are unrelated or defeatist. The correct answer identifies gimbal lock (the aligned-axis degeneracy at 90°) plus the bad interpolation path, and gives the standard fix (quaternions + slerp). The tell is \"near straight-up\" — the pitch=90° singularity is exactly where Euler angles fail, which is why every camera and skeletal-animation system stores orientation as quaternions and only converts to a matrix at the last moment.","url":"track-c/lesson-01.html","title":"C1 Graphics Math — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-143","front":"An artist increases a distant forest's tessellation 8× for \"better detail.\" Framerate craters even though the forest looks identical at that distance. A programmer blames \"vertex shader load.\" Using the pipeline-as-hardware model, what's the more likely dominant cost?","back":"The dominant cost is almost certainly fragment-side, from the 2×2 quad rule: at 8× tessellation the triangles are now a few pixels each, so nearly every shaded quad is a boundary quad running helper lanes whose output is discarded — up to 75% wasted fragment work (5.6 divergence). The vertices also cost more, but the quad-overdraw explosion on tiny triangles is the framerate killer, and it produces zero visible benefit at that distance. Fix: LOD — fewer triangles for distant objects. Verify with RenderDoc's quad-overdraw view (C10) — Option 1 is half-right but misses the bigger cost — vertex work scales ~8× linearly, but the fragment helper-lane waste scales super-linearly as triangles cross below the quad size, which is why the cliff is so steep. Option 4 misses that the cost here buys NO visible detail (the forest is far away). The correct answer applies the quad rule: tiny triangles turn the fragment stage into mostly-discarded helper-lane work, the exact mechanism behind the over-tessellation cliff, and names the tool (quad overdraw) and fix (LOD). Seeing the fragment stage as SIMT hardware with a fixed 2×2 quantum is what makes this predictable rather than mysterious.","url":"track-c/lesson-02.html","title":"C2 The Rasterization Pipeline — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-144","front":"A large ground plane textures fine up close but the texture \"swims\" and warps toward the horizon; separately, distant fence posts flicker against the sky. Are these the same bug?","back":"No — two different precision problems. The swimming texture is missing perspective-correct interpolation: UVs interpolated affinely (without the 1/w correction, C1) warp on large angled triangles. The flickering fence posts are z-fighting: standard depth precision clusters near the camera (worsened by fp32, M1 L8), so distant surfaces at similar depths get the same quantized z and fight. Fixes are different: perspective-correct varyings for the first (usually default), reverse-Z depth for the second. Same region of the screen, unrelated causes — It's tempting to lump \"distance artifacts\" together, but they're distinct pipeline-precision issues. Option 3's far-plane change actually makes z-fighting WORSE (a larger depth range spreads the same bits thinner). Option 4 externalizes both to hardware. The correct answer separates them: affine-interpolation swim (a 1/w problem in interpolation) versus z-buffer precision clustering (a depth-encoding problem fixed by reverse-Z). Both trace to C1's perspective divide — one in how attributes interpolate, one in how depth quantizes — which is why understanding the divide pays off across the whole pipeline.","url":"track-c/lesson-02.html","title":"C2 The Rasterization Pipeline — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-145","front":"A CUDA engineer new to graphics says \"Vulkan looks like a thousand new concepts — I don't have time to learn all this.\" What's the reframing that makes it tractable, and what's the ONE thing that's actually new?","back":"Vulkan's objects map almost one-to-one onto the Module 5 CUDA model: VkQueue is a stream, VkCommandBuffer is recorded launches, VkDeviceMemory is the two host/device worlds, VkPipeline is compiled state, semaphores/fences are events. So the concepts aren't new — it's the same mental model with the driver's automatic decisions exposed as explicit objects. The one genuinely new skill is ownership: doing the memory and synchronization bookkeeping yourself instead of trusting the driver, which is why a forgotten barrier becomes a real race and validation layers exist — Options 3 and 4 dodge rather than reframe (and are factually off — Vulkan is the modern desktop and cross-platform standard). The correct answer is the worked example as an onboarding strategy: map the scary object names onto the CUDA concepts the engineer already owns, which collapses \"a thousand concepts\" into \"five familiar ideas plus one new discipline (ownership).\" This is the fastest path for a compute person into graphics — the physics transfers; only the bookkeeping responsibility is new.","url":"track-c/lesson-03.html","title":"C3 First Triangle: OpenGL to Vulkan — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-146","front":"A tutorial from 2018 has the reader write ~200 lines of VkRenderPass and VkFramebuffer setup before the first triangle. A 2026 tutorial skips almost all of it. What changed, and is the new way \"less correct\"?","back":"Dynamic rendering (a Vulkan 1.3 feature, core in 1.4) replaced the VkRenderPass/VkFramebuffer ceremony with inline vkCmdBeginRendering/EndRendering. It's not less correct — it's the same explicit control over attachments and load/store operations without the separate up-front objects, which were mostly boilerplate for the common single-pass case (the renderpass design still helps mobile tilers, so it remains available). The 2026 tutorial removes hundreds of lines of the least-illuminating ceremony while keeping what matters explicit — Option 1 assumes brevity means bugs, but dynamic rendering is a spec feature, not a shortcut. Option 4 overstates it — renderpasses still exist and matter for tiler GPUs. The correct answer names the actual change (dynamic rendering, core in 1.4) and correctly frames it: the ceremony that was pure tax for the desktop single-pass case got a first-class replacement, while the explicitness that earns its keep (attachments, load/store ops) stays. This is the API maturing — a good signal to prefer current tutorials, and a reminder to verify against the version you're targeting.","url":"track-c/lesson-03.html","title":"C3 First Triangle: OpenGL to Vulkan — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-147","front":"A team shares a C++ lighting struct with a UBO. Everything works until an artist adds a second vec3 field, after which materials render with wrong roughness and emissive values — but only for materials touched after the new field. A programmer suspects a shader math bug. Where should they actually look?","back":"The std140 memory layout. Adding a vec3 changed the byte offsets of every field after it, because std140 aligns vec3 to 16 bytes and the new vec3 pushed subsequent fields to offsets the C++ side doesn't match — so the shader reads roughness/emissive from the wrong bytes (garbage). The math is fine; the data is misaligned. Fix: recompute the std140 offsets, pad the C++ struct to agree (a float after each vec3, or vec4s), or generate both from one definition, and verify with a std140 calculator — The symptom screams \"shading bug\" (wrong roughness/emissive), which is exactly why this is misdiagnosed and chased in the shader math. But the tell is temporal: it broke when a vec3 was ADDED, and only affects fields after it — the fingerprint of an alignment shift, not a math error. Option 4 blames the driver for correct (if surprising) std140 behavior. The correct answer is the worked example as a field diagnosis: a vec3 changed the offsets, the shader now reads the wrong bytes, and the fix is to make the C++ layout match std140 and verify it. \"Wrong values after adding a field\" is layout until proven otherwise.","url":"track-c/lesson-04.html","title":"C4 GLSL Shaders Deep Dive — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-148","front":"You need to pass a per-object model matrix (64 bytes) that changes every draw, and a scene's 200 light structs (large, read in a loop) that change per frame. Which mechanisms fit each, and why?","back":"Push constants don't fit either well at those sizes (typically ~128 B total), and 200 lights certainly won't. The per-object matrix that changes every draw is a good fit for push constants if it fits the limit, or a small dynamic UBO otherwise — it's small and per-draw. The 200 lights are large and per-frame, so a storage buffer (SSBO) indexed in a loop is right — SSBOs hold big variable-size arrays. This is 4.5's hierarchy: small+hot (the matrix) near the shader, big+cold (the light array) in a buffer you index — Option 1 ignores the push-constant size limit (~128 B) — 200 light structs are orders of magnitude too big. Option 3 works but wastes the fast path for the tiny per-draw matrix and treats a per-draw value like bulk data. Option 4's texture-for-lights is an old trick from before SSBOs existed and is worse ergonomically now. The correct answer applies the size/frequency match: per-draw + tiny → push constants (or small dynamic UBO); per-frame + large array → SSBO. It's literally 4.5's memory-hierarchy reasoning — the mechanism should mirror how big and how often the data is.","url":"track-c/lesson-04.html","title":"C4 GLSL Shaders Deep Dive — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-149","front":"A game's art looks \"muddy\" — midtones too dark, transparent edges have dark fringes, gradients band. An artist keeps brightening textures to compensate, which helps some assets and breaks others. What's the root cause and the real fix?","back":"The renderer is doing lighting and blending on gamma-encoded (sRGB) values instead of linear light. Since sRGB 0.5 is only ~0.22 in linear (executed), blending and lighting on those values comes out too dark and produces dark transparency fringes and banded gradients. Brightening textures is a per-asset hack that can't fix a pipeline-wide encoding error. The real fix is architectural: mark color textures sRGB (hardware decodes to linear on read), do ALL lighting/blending in linear space, and write to an sRGB target (hardware encodes for display) — get the color space right once and the whole class of bugs disappears — The artist's per-asset brightening that \"helps some, breaks others\" is the fingerprint of a systemic gamma error, not an asset problem (options 2/3/4 all treat symptoms per-asset). Option 1 externalizes it to hardware. The correct answer identifies the root cause — computing on sRGB values that should be linear — with the executed 0.22 number, and gives the architectural fix (decode on read, linear in the middle, encode on write). This is M1 L8's \"a number needs an interpretation\" as a whole-pipeline discipline: the muddy look is a units error, and you fix units once, not per-asset.","url":"track-c/lesson-05.html","title":"C5 Textures, Sampling, Mipmaps — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-150","front":"A textured road stretching to the horizon shimmers and aliases in the distance despite bilinear filtering being on. What's missing, and why doesn't bilinear alone fix it?","back":"Mipmaps (and ideally anisotropic filtering) are missing. Bilinear filtering blends 4 texels around ONE sample point, which fixes magnification (texture bigger than screen) but not MINIFICATION — in the distance many texels map to one pixel, and sampling 4 of thousands still aliases. Mipmaps fix it by prefiltering the texture to lower resolutions and picking the level with ~1 texel per pixel (via the quad's derivatives, C2). The road at a grazing angle is compressed much more in one direction, so anisotropic filtering — sampling along that direction — is what makes it crisp rather than blurry — Option 1 (higher res) makes it worse — more texels per pixel means more aliasing without mips. Option 3 (nearest) removes filtering entirely, aliasing harder. The correct answer distinguishes magnification (bilinear's job) from minification (mipmaps' job): bilinear samples 4 texels at one point, which can't represent the thousands of texels a distant pixel covers, so it shimmers. Mipmaps prefilter and the quad-derivatives pick the level; anisotropic handles the grazing-angle compression of a receding road. Knowing that bilinear fixes magnification but mipmaps fix minification is the core sampling insight.","url":"track-c/lesson-05.html","title":"C5 Textures, Sampling, Mipmaps — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-151","front":"A team's older Phong-based game had materials that \"looked amazing in the showcase level but wrong everywhere else,\" requiring per-level material tweaks. They switch to PBR and the re-tuning stops. What property of PBR made the difference?","back":"Energy conservation. Phong's diffuse and specular are ad-hoc terms with no physical bound, so a material tuned to look right under one lighting setup reflects the wrong amount under another — hence per-level tweaking. PBR's BRDF is normalized so total reflected light never exceeds incoming, which makes a material authored once (base color, metallic, roughness) look correct under any lighting — sunlight, interior, dusk — with no re-tuning. The scene-invariance comes directly from the physical constraint, not from more terms or bigger textures — Option 1 confuses lighting model with texture resolution (unrelated). Option 3 is false — PBR has a sophisticated specular (the microfacet BRDF), not none. Option 4 misses the mechanism. The correct answer identifies energy conservation as the property: Phong's unbounded ad-hoc terms must be re-tuned per lighting, while PBR's normalized, energy-conserving BRDF is scene-invariant by construction. This is the entire industry reason for the switch — author physically once, trust everywhere — and it's why the worked example verifies conservation by integration rather than taking \"physically based\" on faith.","url":"track-c/lesson-06.html","title":"C6 Lighting: Phong to PBR — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-152","front":"In a glTF PBR material, changing the single \"metallic\" parameter from 0 to 1 dramatically changes appearance — the surface loses its diffuse color and its reflections take on the base color. What's happening physically, and why is it one parameter?","back":"Metallic switches F0 (reflectivity at normal incidence) and the diffuse contribution. Dielectrics (metallic=0) reflect ~4% (F0=0.04) and have a diffuse albedo color; metals (metallic=1) have NO diffuse and reflect their base color as the specular tint (F0 = albedo). So flipping metallic swaps F0 from 0.04 to the base color and removes diffuse — that one physical distinction (metals conduct, so they absorb transmitted light and only reflect, tinting the reflection) is captured by a single parameter, which is exactly why glTF's model is just base color + metallic + roughness — Option 1 confuses metalness with roughness (roughness controls highlight tightness; metalness controls the diffuse/F0 physics). Option 4 misunderstands the model — metallic SHOULD remove diffuse, that's the physics. The correct answer explains the real mechanism: metals have no diffuse (they absorb transmitted light) and reflect their albedo as tinted specular (F0=albedo), while dielectrics reflect a flat ~4% and keep a diffuse color. Compressing that entire distinction into one metallic parameter is the elegance of the metalness workflow and why glTF materials are so compact — one switch between two physical regimes.","url":"track-c/lesson-06.html","title":"C6 Lighting: Phong to PBR — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-153","front":"A graphics programmer says \"I don't know compute shaders, I only do the rendering pipeline — I'd have to learn a whole new model to add a blur.\" A colleague who took the core course disagrees. Why?","back":"A GLSL compute shader IS the CUDA/GPU model the core taught, renamed: local_size is blockDim, gl_GlobalInvocationID is the global index, shared is __shared__, barrier() is __syncthreads(), subgroups are warps. So anyone who learned the core reduction, tiling, and occupancy already knows compute shaders — they'd write the blur with the exact tools (shared-memory tiling, separability) from Module 5. It's not a new model; it's the same model with graphics-API spelling, which is the whole point of the reunion — Option 3 is partly true (small blurs can be fragment-shader passes) but misses the point and is wrong for large separable blurs with intermediates. Option 4 is false (compute does post-processing constantly). The correct answer is the reunion thesis: compute shaders are the Module 5 model with renamed keywords, so the core course already taught them. A programmer who knows the 5.8 reduction and 5.5 tiling can write a compute blur immediately — the \"whole new model\" fear is exactly the misconception this lesson dissolves. Graphics and compute converge on the same hardware primitives.","url":"track-c/lesson-07.html","title":"C7 Compute Shaders — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-154","front":"A team implements a 21×21 Gaussian bloom as a single fragment-shader pass reading the full 21×21 neighborhood per pixel, and it's too slow. Before writing any GPU code, what's the algorithmic fix and how much does it save?","back":"Make it separable: a Gaussian factors as G(x)·G(y), so replace the one 21×21 pass (441 taps/pixel) with two 1D passes — horizontal then vertical, 21+21 = 42 taps/pixel — a ~10.5× reduction in taps, done in two compute (or fragment) passes with an intermediate buffer. The algorithm changes from O(N²) to O(N) in the radius for free, from a mathematical property, before any low-level optimization. That's the difference between the bloom fitting the frame budget or not — Option 1 sacrifices the visual (a smaller blur is a different look) to work around an algorithmic inefficiency. Options 3/4 are nonsensical for a per-pixel image filter. The correct answer is the separability win: the biggest speedups come from algorithm, not micro-optimization, and a Gaussian's separability turns 441 taps into 42 (10.5×) with no quality loss. This is the worked example — reach for the mathematical property (separable kernel) before touching the GPU code, exactly the compute-first thinking the core built.","url":"track-c/lesson-07.html","title":"C7 Compute Shaders — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-155","front":"A compute post-process reads an image the render pass produced, and the result flickers between correct and corrupt — and it's fine on the dev machine but corrupt on a tester's different GPU. What's the bug class, and why the machine-dependence?","back":"A missing pipeline barrier between the render pass's writes and the compute read — a data race (5.4). The compute pass sometimes starts before the color writes are visible and before the image is transitioned to a shader-read layout. It's intermittent and machine-dependent because races depend on GPU scheduling, which varies by hardware and frame — so it can pass on the dev GPU and fail on another (a test certifies the machine it ran on, D2). Fix: add the barrier (COLOR_WRITE → SHADER_READ + layout transition), and turn on synchronization validation, which flags the missing barrier deterministically at dev time — Option 1 blames the driver for the app's missing synchronization (a common wrong reflex). Options 3/4 don't explain the intermittency or machine-dependence. The correct answer identifies the bug class (missing barrier = data race, 5.4) and explains the fingerprint: races are timing-dependent, so they vary by GPU and frame, which is why \"works on my machine\" is worthless for synchronization — the same lesson as D2's wavefront bug (a test certifies only the machine it ran on). The fix is the explicit barrier plus synchronization validation to make the race a deterministic dev-time error. Intermittent + machine-dependent = race, until proven otherwise.","url":"track-c/lesson-08.html","title":"C8 Vulkan Sync — Command Buffers & Barriers — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-156","front":"A newcomer is overwhelmed by Vulkan's synchronization — semaphores, fences, barriers, timeline semaphores, layouts. What's the reframing that makes it learnable, and which core lesson is it?","back":"It's 5.9's async/events model with new names: a queue is a stream, a semaphore is a GPU→GPU event, a fence is a GPU→CPU event, a pipeline barrier orders work and makes writes visible within a queue, and a timeline semaphore is a counter event. The underlying questions — order this after that, make writes visible before reads, tell the CPU when the GPU is done, keep frames pipelined without hazards — are exactly 5.9's. Write the translation table down and the scary chapter becomes 5.9 with a dictionary; the only genuinely new bit is image layout transitions riding along on barriers — Options 3/4 avoid understanding rather than build it (and copied sync breaks the moment your pipeline differs). The correct answer is the lesson's thesis: Vulkan sync is 5.9's events-and-streams model renamed, so a person who learned the core async lesson already owns the concepts — queue/stream, semaphore/fence as events, barrier as hazard-ordering-plus-visibility. The translation table converts overwhelm into recognition, with image layouts as the one new wrinkle. This is the same move as C3 (Vulkan objects = Module 5 concepts): the physics transfers; only the vocabulary and the ownership burden are new.","url":"track-c/lesson-08.html","title":"C8 Vulkan Sync — Command Buffers & Barriers — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-157","front":"A student implements a ray tracer that tests every ray against every triangle and concludes \"ray tracing is fundamentally too slow for real time.\" What's the flaw, and what's the actual complexity with the right data structure?","back":"The flaw is the brute-force O(rays × triangles) approach. A bounding-volume hierarchy (BVH) turns each ray's triangle search into ~O(log N) by testing nested bounding boxes and skipping whole subtrees the ray misses — for a 1M-triangle scene that's ~20 box tests instead of 1M triangle tests, roughly 50,000× fewer (executed-style estimate). RT cores then do that traversal in fixed-function silicon. So the complexity is ~O(rays × log triangles), not O(rays × triangles) — the BVH is exactly M1's log-lesson turning linear search into logarithmic, and it's what makes real-time RT feasible — Options 3/5 sacrifice quality/scene to dodge the algorithmic problem; option 4 is backwards (GPUs with RT cores are the target). The correct answer identifies the missing data structure — the BVH — and the complexity it buys: O(log N) per ray instead of O(N), a ~50,000× win on a large scene, plus fixed-function RT cores. This is M1's logarithm lesson in its most literal application: a tree turns linear search into logarithmic, and that single structure is the difference between \"impossible\" and \"shipping in games.\" Brute-force complexity is the flaw; the BVH is the fix.","url":"track-c/lesson-09.html","title":"C9 Ray Tracing — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-158","front":"A team ray-traces reflections at 1 sample per pixel and the result is unusably grainy. A programmer says \"we need 1000 samples per pixel like offline renderers, so real-time RT is a lie.\" What's the real solution?","back":"Temporal accumulation (denoising): blend this frame's noisy 1-spp sample with reprojected samples from previous frames, so each pixel accumulates many effective samples over several frames and converges to a clean image — nearly free by reusing past work, no 1000-spp needed. The cost is ghosting when the camera or objects move fast (reprojection breaks down), so correctness becomes 'measurably plausible' rather than exact. Combined with a hybrid pipeline (ray-trace only reflections, rasterize the rest), this is how real-time RT actually ships — Option 3's 16 spp is still far too expensive for a real-time budget (which affords ~1). Option 4 (a dumb blur) destroys detail and doesn't converge. The correct answer is the denoising bargain: temporal accumulation reuses past frames to reach many effective samples from 1 spp, cheaply, at a ghosting cost under motion — which is why real-time RT correctness is 'measurably plausible' (PSNR), not exact. The programmer's error is assuming real-time must match offline's brute-force sample count; the real innovation is amortizing samples over time, plus spending rays only where they matter (hybrid). This is the track's thesis — the eye is the customer, so a denoised approximation is the right answer.","url":"track-c/lesson-09.html","title":"C9 Ray Tracing — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-159","front":"A frame misses 60 fps and the team's most experienced graphics programmer is confident the new volumetric fog is the cause and starts optimizing its shader. What should happen first, and why is the confidence a liability?","back":"Capture the frame in RenderDoc and sort passes by GPU time FIRST, before touching any shader. Even experienced intuition is a guess, and the bottleneck is frequently a pass nobody suspects (overdraw in transparency, a pile of tiny draws, a bandwidth-blown G-buffer). Optimizing the fog before confirming it's the top pass risks spending days shaving milliseconds off a pass that isn't the bottleneck while the real culprit sits untouched — the frame still misses 60 and the effort is wasted. The confidence is a liability precisely because it skips the capture that would validate or refute it (5.10's doctrine: measure, don't guess) — Option 1 elevates intuition over measurement, the exact error 5.10 forbids — even experts guess wrong about bottlenecks often. Option 4 masks the problem without understanding it. Option 3 outsources judgment. The correct answer is the runbook's first rule: capture and sort before optimizing, because the top pass is your whole problem and it's rarely the one you'd guess. Experience makes better guesses, but a guess about a bottleneck is still a guess, and the capture is cheap insurance against a wasted day — the worked example (lighting assumed, transparency actual) is this quiz made concrete. Confidence that skips measurement is how senior engineers waste days too.","url":"track-c/lesson-10.html","title":"C10 Frame Profiling — RenderDoc & Nsight — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-160","front":"A RenderDoc capture shows the top pass is the deferred lighting pass, and Nsight reports it's memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound. What core lesson does this map to, and what's the fix family?","back":"Bandwidth-bound means the pass is moving too many bytes, not doing too much math — it's 4.6's bus economics. For deferred lighting that usually means a fat G-buffer (too many/too-large render targets read per pixel). The fix family is reducing bytes: pack the G-buffer (fewer, smaller targets — e.g. encode normals in 2 channels, pack material params), use lower-precision formats where acceptable, or compress. Optimizing the lighting MATH (option 1) won't help a bandwidth-bound pass — you'd be speeding up the thing that isn't the bottleneck (the roofline verdict, 4.7) — Option 1 is the classic mistake: optimizing compute on a bandwidth-bound pass, which the roofline (4.7) says won't help — you're not compute-bound. Options 3/4 make it worse. The correct answer reads the counter correctly: bandwidth-bound = 4.6's bytes-crossing-the-bus problem, and for deferred rendering the usual cause is a fat G-buffer, so the fix is fewer/smaller/packed targets. This is why you confirm with counters (step 4): \"the pass is slow\" isn't actionable, but \"the pass is bandwidth-bound\" points directly at the byte budget, not the math. The counter tells you which knob to turn.","url":"track-c/lesson-10.html","title":"C10 Frame Profiling — RenderDoc & Nsight — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-161","front":"Two candidates submit a Vulkan renderer capstone. Candidate A's \"looks great\" in a screenshot. Candidate B's comes with a PSNR test that runs in CI, a per-pass budget table measured in RenderDoc, and a machine file. Why is B's substantially stronger even if the renders look identical?","back":"B demonstrates the engineering judgment that separates a hobby project from a shippable one: the PSNR-in-CI test enforces correctness as a build gate (a refactor that breaks a material fails the build, not the demo), the measured per-pass budget proves B can profile and account for a frame's time rather than hope it's fast, and the machine file makes the results reproducible by a stranger. A screenshot proves it rendered once on one machine; B's trust stack proves B can keep it correct, fast, and reproducible — which is what shipping actually requires. The renderer is the setup; the report is the deliverable — Options 1 and 4 mistake the artifact (a render) for the skill (shipping a render reliably). Option 3 assumes technique when the difference is process. The correct answer is the capstone's thesis, shared with 5.11 and D9: the report — enforced correctness, measured performance, reproducibility — is the portfolio piece, because it demonstrates the judgment that a screenshot can't. Anyone can produce a nice image once; proving you can keep it correct under refactors (PSNR in CI), fit it in a budget you measured (RenderDoc table), and let a stranger reproduce it (machine file) is the rare skill an interviewer is buying. B shows they can be trusted with a frame.","url":"track-c/lesson-11.html","title":"C11 Capstone — A Small Renderer with a Ray-Traced Effect — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-162","front":"For the capstone's correctness test, a teammate proposes checking that the rendered frame matches the golden image bit-for-bit. Why is that the wrong test, and what's right?","back":"Bitwise equality will fail a correct renderer: ray tracing and denoising reorder and approximate (C9), floating-point reduces in different orders (M1 L8), and even driver/GPU differences change the last bits — so a bitwise test rejects images that are perceptually identical and correct. The right test is a perceptual budget: PSNR (or SSIM) against the golden image above a threshold (e.g. 40 dB), which passes perceptually-identical frames and fails ones that actually look wrong (a broken material, a darkened scene). It's the fixture doctrine (M2 L6) adapted to eyeballs — bounded and enforceable, not bitwise, because the consumer is a human — Option 1 is the trap — bitwise sounds rigorous but fails correct renders because RT, denoising, and floating-point all legitimately vary the exact bits (the same lesson as every reduction in the course: budget, not bitwise). Option 3 tests nothing about correctness; option 4 tests almost nothing. The correct answer applies M1 L8 and C9 to the test itself: the image varies at the bit level for legitimate reasons, so correctness is a perceptual threshold (PSNR/SSIM), which passes what looks right and fails what doesn't. This is the track's thesis in the test harness — the eye is the consumer, so \"measurably plausible\" is the correctness standard.","url":"track-c/lesson-11.html","title":"C11 Capstone — A Small Renderer with a Ray-Traced Effect — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-163","front":"Your team is \"a CUDA shop.\" A cloud vendor offers AMD MI300X capacity at roughly half the price of the H100s you can't get enough of. The lead engineer says \"porting is a full rewrite — not worth it.\" You've read this lesson. What's the accurate rebuttal?","back":"It's not a rewrite — HIP is CUDA with the host API identifiers renamed and the kernel body (index math, launch geometry, shared memory) unchanged; the real, bounded work is auditing every hard-coded warp-size-32 assumption for wavefront 64 and re-tuning the two or three hottest kernels. Call it a week per major kernel path, not a quarter — and the alternative, staying single-vendor during a supply crunch at 2× the price, is the larger risk — The seductive wrong answer is \"full rewrite\" because it's the safe-sounding pessimism, but it contradicts the translation table and D2's hipify reality (the diff is renames, the device logic is byte-identical). Option 4 over-corrects: WebGPU trades away the control and the vendor libraries you need for a training workload (the 90/10 tax at its worst). The correct answer names the actual cost — the wavefront-64 audit plus hot-kernel re-tuning — which is exactly the 90/10 rule applied: 90% of the port is free renames, the 10% that isn't is specific and findable.","url":"track-d/lesson-01.html","title":"D1 The Portability Problem & the 2026 Landscape — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-164","front":"A colleague concludes: \"El Capitan is an AMD machine, so all the CUDA experience on our team is worthless for HPC now.\" Where is the thinking wrong?","back":"The CUDA experience IS the asset — El Capitan's MI300A is programmed with HIP and Kokkos, both of which map 1:1 onto what the team already knows: warps become wavefronts, __shared__ becomes LDS, __syncthreads becomes a group barrier. What transfers is the hard-won part — occupancy intuition, the memory hierarchy, coalescing, reduction patterns — none of which is NVIDIA-specific. The team ports vocabulary, not concepts — Option 2 is a factual trap — El Capitan is genuinely AMD (verified TOP500, June 2026), so denying the premise is wrong. Option 1 mistakes different syntax for different concepts, the exact error this lesson dismantles. The correct answer is the track's thesis stated defensively: the concepts are the moat and they're vendor-neutral; someone fluent in CUDA's ideas becomes productive in HIP/Kokkos in days because the translation table is short and the arithmetic (the worked example) doesn't move.","url":"track-d/lesson-01.html","title":"D1 The Portability Problem & the 2026 Landscape — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-165","front":"Wait — first the setup. A team hipifies a working CUDA library, all fixtures pass on their NVIDIA CI, they deploy to an MI300 cluster, and a downstream model's accuracy quietly degrades. No crashes, no errors. What's the first thing you check, and why does the CI being green not clear the port?","back":"Grep every kernel for hard-coded 32 / 16 / 0xffffffff — warp-width assumptions. The CI was green because it ran on NVIDIA (warpSize 32), where those literals are correct; the bug only manifests on AMD's 64-lane wavefront, where a baked-in 32-lane reduction or ballot silently processes half the lanes. Green CI on the wrong architecture proves nothing about the target; you need the fixtures to run on the AMD hardware — Option 4 is the exact false confidence this lesson exists to destroy — hipify guarantees the code compiles, not that architecture-specific constants are right. Option 1 chases the wrong failure mode: this bug has no out-of-bounds access, so the sanitizer (5.4) stays silent — it's a logic error, not a memory error. The correct answer names the mechanism (warp-width literals) and the reason CI lied (it ran on 32-lane hardware). The deep lesson: a fixture only tests the machine it runs on, so cross-vendor ports demand cross-vendor CI — the wavefront bug is invisible until the fixture executes where warpSize is 64.","url":"track-d/lesson-02.html","title":"D2 ROCm & HIP — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-166","front":"A kernel is memory-bound on your Colab T4 (roofline says so, 4.7). You port it to an MI300 with HBM3E, expecting the same verdict and tuning. Your teammate says \"it's the same kernel, so it's still memory-bound — reuse the T4 tuning.\" Right or wrong?","back":"Wrong — arithmetic intensity is a property of the kernel, but the ROOFLINE it sits on is a property of the machine. MI300's HBM3E bandwidth and compute peak differ from the T4's, so the ridge point moves; a kernel that was memory-bound on the T4 can become compute-bound on the MI300 (or stay memory-bound but with a totally different achievable ceiling). The tuning that helped on the T4 (say, coalescing effort) may be irrelevant if the kernel is now compute-bound. Re-profile on the target — The subtle correct point (4.7 wearing a cross-vendor hat): arithmetic intensity (FLOPs per byte) is intrinsic to the algorithm and ports; the roofline's slopes and ridge are the hardware's, and they don't. Option 3's \"always faster\" is the lazy over-claim D8 dismantles — faster at what, and per dollar/watt? The metric that survives porting is % of the target's OWN roofline, which is exactly why you re-profile rather than assume. Reusing a verdict across machines is reusing an answer to a question you never re-asked.","url":"track-d/lesson-02.html","title":"D2 ROCm & HIP — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-167","front":"A developer ports a CUDA pipeline to SYCL using buffers/accessors, but wraps every submit in q.wait() \"to be safe, like cudaDeviceSynchronize after each kernel.\" The result is correct but no faster than serial. What did they do, and what's the fix?","back":"They defeated the runtime's whole value proposition: by calling q.wait() after every kernel they forced a global barrier between submissions, so the scheduler can never overlap independent work (the K1/K3 concurrency) it would have found from the accessors. The fix is to NOT wait — submit all the command groups and let the inferred DAG serialize only the true dependencies, waiting once at the end (or on the buffer's host accessor). The accessors already encode the safety; the manual waits are redundant and destroy the parallelism — This is the single-source model's signature footgun: importing CUDA's explicit-sync habit (5.3/5.9) into a system that infers synchronization makes every kernel a barrier. Option 4 misdiagnoses — USM can overlap too; the problem is the waits, not the memory model. The correct answer names the mechanism (global barrier per submit kills inferred overlap) and the fix (trust the accessors, wait once). The deep point: in SYCL, correctness comes from declaring data intent, so adding manual synchronization on top doesn't add safety — it only subtracts performance.","url":"track-d/lesson-03.html","title":"D3 SYCL & oneAPI — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-168","front":"Your team needs a reduction over 100M floats, portable across NVIDIA and AMD. One engineer wants to hand-write a shuffle-tree reduction \"for control\"; another wants sycl::reduction . Given D2, which is the defensible default and why?","back":"Use sycl::reduction as the default. A hand-written shuffle tree bakes in exactly the warp-width literal that silently breaks on AMD's 64-lane wavefront (D2's bug), so \"for control\" really means \"for the privilege of re-introducing the 32-vs-64 landmine on every backend.\" The library reduction is written by people who handle the per-architecture SIMD width and race-freedom for you (5.11) — it's correct-by-construction across vendors. Reach for the hand-written version only if a profiler proves the library is a bottleneck AND you're willing to own the cross-vendor tuning — Option 3 is the D2 bug stated as a (bad) plan. Option 5 defeats the entire point of single-source — two codebases is what portability was supposed to eliminate. The correct answer connects two lessons: SYCL's library reduction is the library-first doctrine (5.11) doing exactly what it's for — encapsulating the wavefront-64 tuning (D2) so your one source is correct everywhere. \"For control\" is a real reason sometimes, but the burden of proof is on the hand-written path, because control here means owning the landmine.","url":"track-d/lesson-03.html","title":"D3 SYCL & oneAPI — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-169","front":"A junior engineer says \"OpenCL is dead — it lost to CUDA, so there's no reason to ever learn or choose it in 2026.\" Where is this wrong, and what's the precise scope of OpenCL's continued relevance?","back":"It conflates \"lost the datacenter\" with \"dead.\" OpenCL still owns the device long tail — mobile GPUs, DSPs, embedded SoCs, and FPGAs, where it's often the only open standard the vendor ships — so if your target is a phone GPU or an FPGA, OpenCL is the choice, not a legacy one. And via mandatory SPIR-V ingestion (OpenCL 3.1) it became a backend under SYCL and chipStar, so it's load-bearing infrastructure even for people who never write it directly. Its scope narrowed to breadth and plumbing; it didn't vanish — Options 3 and 4 are false claims (CUDA is thriving; OpenCL isn't faster on NVIDIA). The correct answer draws the real boundary: OpenCL lost the high-margin center (datacenter, desktop) but holds the breadth (embedded/FPGA) and gained a second life as SPIR-V infrastructure. \"Dead\" is a category error — the useful question is never \"is X dead\" but \"where is X still the right tool,\" and for OpenCL the answer is specific: the widest hardware reach, plus the plumbing under SYCL.","url":"track-d/lesson-04.html","title":"D4 OpenCL Today — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-170","front":"OpenCL was an open, cross-vendor standard released before CUDA matured, yet CUDA won. A colleague concludes \"open standards always lose to proprietary ones.\" What's the more accurate lesson?","back":"The accurate lesson is narrower and more useful: the winner was the better PLATFORM, not the more proprietary one. CUDA won because it shipped libraries (cuBLAS/cuDNN), profilers (Nsight), and coherent docs while OpenCL had a spec but fragmented, late, buggy vendor implementations and no library ecosystem. Openness didn't doom OpenCL; the missing platform did. Plenty of open standards win (SPIR-V, Vulkan, the web) when they arrive with tooling and reliability — so the takeaway is 'invest in the ecosystem,' not 'proprietary wins' — Options 1 and 3 overgeneralize into a cynical and false rule (WebGPU, Vulkan, Linux, and SPIR-V are open winners). Option 4 is glib. The correct answer isolates the actual causal variable — ecosystem completeness — which is both more accurate and more actionable: it tells a team building any platform that the spec is the cheap part and the libraries/tools/docs/reliability are what determine adoption. This is 5.11's library-first doctrine viewed from the vendor's side: the libraries are why developers show up.","url":"track-d/lesson-04.html","title":"D4 OpenCL Today — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-171","front":"A developer writes a fast WGSL reduction using subgroupAdd (the warp-shuffle equivalent), tests it in Chrome where it flies, and ships. Bug reports flood in from Firefox and Safari users: the feature silently does nothing or the pipeline fails to create. What went wrong and what is the correct pattern?","back":"Subgroups are an optional extension (shipped in Chrome 134, not universal), so the developer used a non-baseline feature without detecting it. The pipeline that requires subgroups cannot be created where the feature is absent. The correct pattern is to feature-detect — check adapter.features.has('subgroups') and requestDevice with it in requiredFeatures only if present — and provide a baseline fallback using var<workgroup> + workgroupBarrier (guaranteed everywhere). Code to the baseline; treat subgroups as a fast path, not a given — Option 1 is factually wrong (all three ship WebGPU as of 2026). Option 4 misunderstands WGSL — it is compiled at runtime by each browser's engine, not per-browser by you. The correct answer names the mechanism (an optional extension used unconditionally) and the fix (feature-detect + baseline fallback), which is the browser edition of D4's \"SVM is optional, query it\" and D1's 90/10 tax: the portable core is free, the fast-path feature costs a detect-and-fallback branch. The general law: at browser scale, \"works in my Chrome\" certifies exactly one engine.","url":"track-d/lesson-05.html","title":"D5 WebGPU & WGSL — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-172","front":"Your WebGPU reduction returns 524594.83 while the CPU fp64 sum returns 524594.83 23 — they differ in the 7th digit. A teammate flags it as a GPU bug. Is it?","back":"No — it is correct within the fp32 budget. The GPU reduces in a tree order (partials, then host sum) while the CPU sums serially in fp64, and fp32 addition is non-associative (M1 L8), so a disagreement of a few parts in 10^6 on a million-element sum is expected and right. The fixture must use an error budget (roughly N*1e-3 here), not bitwise equality; demanding equality would fail a correct reduction. The all-ones case (exact integer sum) confirms the algorithm — only the floating-point rounding differs — Option 4 is a factual trap: WebGPU's baseline is fp32, not fp64 (f16 is an extension, fp64 generally unavailable). Options 1/3 apply a bitwise-equality standard that M1 L8 spent a whole lesson dismantling — floating-point sums depend on order, and parallel reductions reorder by design. The correct answer is the reduction's correctness policy from 5.8 and 5.11, cross-vendor: budget-based fixtures, with the exact-integer case as the algorithm check. This is the same \"do not expect bitwise across execution orders\" lesson that governs every backend in this track.","url":"track-d/lesson-05.html","title":"D5 WebGPU & WGSL — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-173","front":"A developer new to Kokkos writes View<double**, LayoutRight> everywhere because \"row-major is what I know from C.\" The code is correct on the CPU but 4-8x slower than expected on the GPU. What's the mechanism, and what should they have written?","back":"By hard-coding LayoutRight they forced row-major on the GPU too, so adjacent GPU threads accessing adjacent i now stride by the row length M — uncoalesced access paying the sector tax (5.7), which is the 4-8x. The fix is to NOT specify a layout: write View<double**> and let Kokkos default to LayoutLeft on the GPU build (coalesced) and LayoutRight on the CPU build (cache-friendly). Hard-coding a layout throws away the compile-time switch that is the whole point of a View — Option 1 over-corrects into the opposite hard-code, which would then be slow on the CPU — the point is that neither fixed layout is right for both machines. Option 4 misdiagnoses a memory-access problem as an occupancy one (adding threads doesn't fix uncoalesced access, 5.7). The correct answer names the mechanism (hard-coded row-major = strided/uncoalesced on GPU) and the fix (let the View default so the layout tracks the backend). The meta-lesson: specifying a layout is opting OUT of performance portability — you do it only when you have a specific reason, and then you own the per-machine consequence.","url":"track-d/lesson-06.html","title":"D6 Kokkos & RAJA — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-174","front":"A Kokkos kernel with ExecutionSpace = Cuda tries to read a View allocated in HostSpace . On a hand-written CUDA port, the analogous bug (dereferencing a host pointer on the device) was a runtime crash you found with the sanitizer (5.4). What happens in Kokkos, and why is that better?","back":"It's a COMPILE error: because execution and memory spaces are template parameters, the type system knows a Cuda-space kernel can't legally access HostSpace data, and rejects it at build time. That's strictly better than 5.4's runtime discovery — the bug can't ship, can't reach production, and needs no sanitizer run to find. It's 5.3's host/device ownership discipline enforced by the compiler instead of by your vigilance. The fix is an explicit deep_copy from host to device space (the copy you'd have written in CUDA, now type-checked) — Options 3 and 4 describe a convenience Kokkos deliberately doesn't provide — silent host-device copies would hide the 4.6 bus cost that you need to see and control, so Kokkos makes you write deep_copy explicitly. Option 1 misses the type-level guarantee. The correct answer is the payoff of making 5.3's two worlds into types: a whole class of runtime memory-space bugs (3.5, 5.4) becomes a compile error. Moving a bug from runtime to compile time is one of the highest-leverage things a type system does — it converts \"found in production at 3am\" into \"found by the build.\"","url":"track-d/lesson-06.html","title":"D6 Kokkos & RAJA — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-175","front":"A team benchmarks a Triton matmul against cuBLAS on NVIDIA and finds Triton ~2x slower at large sizes. They conclude \"Triton is a failure for portability.\" What's the flawed assumption, and what's the correct role for Triton here?","back":"The flawed assumption is that portable custom kernels should beat hand-tuned vendor libraries at their own game. A production matmul is exactly the tensor-core peak operation where cuBLAS/rocBLAS encode assembly-level scheduling Triton doesn't match — that's the honest last-10% limit, not a portability failure. Triton's role is the OTHER 90%: fused, custom, and non-standard kernels (fused epilogues, attention variants, novel ops — A5) that no library ships, made portable across vendors with zero source changes. Comparing Triton to cuBLAS on matmul tests it where it's designed to lose; test it on a fused kernel cuBLAS can't express — Options 3 and 4 are nonsense (Triton targets both vendors; you can't recompile cuBLAS through Triton). The correct answer draws D1's 90/10 line precisely: matmul-at-peak is the 10% vendor libraries own, so benchmarking Triton there is choosing its worst case. Triton wins where there's no library to call — fused and custom kernels — which is most of a real model's long tail (Track A's fusion story). The general lesson: judge a tool against its purpose, not against a specialist at the specialist's task; \"portable custom kernels\" and \"peak vendor matmul\" are different jobs.","url":"track-d/lesson-07.html","title":"D7 Triton & MLIR — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-176","front":"Moving a hand-written CUDA reduction to AMD took a wavefront-64 audit (D2). Moving a Triton reduction to AMD took zero source changes. A colleague says \"so Triton has no wavefront-64 problem — the bug doesn't exist there.\" Precisely right or subtly wrong?","back":"Subtly wrong in its reasoning, right in its outcome. The wavefront-64 issue absolutely exists at the hardware level on AMD — but the Triton COMPILER handles the SIMD-width difference when it lowers TTGIR to AMDGCN, so YOUR source never encodes a literal 32 to get wrong. The bug didn't vanish; the compiler owns it instead of you. That's the whole value of the IR rung — it absorbs the per-architecture details (warp width, tuning) that a hand port makes you audit. But you should still re-validate the reduction numerically on AMD, because a compiler can have its own bugs, and a fixture certifies the machine it runs on (D2) — Option 4 is a factual error (AMD wavefronts are 64). Option 3 is wrong (Triton targets AMD via ROCm 6.2+). The subtle correctness the answer captures: \"no source-level bug\" is not \"no hardware difference\" — the compiler assumed responsibility for the 32-vs-64 handling, which is exactly why the IR rung is powerful. But it also means you've traded auditing your own literals for trusting the compiler, so the D2 discipline (re-run fixtures on the actual AMD hardware) still applies — now guarding against compiler bugs instead of your own. Ownership moved; it didn't disappear.","url":"track-d/lesson-07.html","title":"D7 Triton & MLIR — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-177","front":"A vendor's slide shows their GPU is \"2.3x faster\" than a competitor on a training benchmark. You get to ask three questions before believing it. Which three most efficiently expose whether the number is honest?","back":"(1) Same problem size and warmed clocks on both? (exposes mismatched-sizes and no-warmup sins). (2) What's the % of each GPU's own roofline? (exposes whether it's the chip or a badly-tuned competitor port — the home-turf sin — and gives a fair engineering number). (3) What's the images-or-tokens per dollar and per watt? (exposes the wall-time-only sin and reveals the decision-relevant number). Those three cover four of the five sins and convert \"2.3x faster\" into a claim you can actually evaluate or dismiss — Option 4's details matter for reproduction but don't directly test the claim's honesty. Option 3's questions are relevant but narrower (precision/scale/batch are sub-cases of \"same problem size\"). Option 1 asks about specs, not methodology. The correct answer targets the sins directly: mismatched sizes and cold clocks (sin 1, 3), home-turf tuning via roofline fraction (sin 5, 4), and the missing cost/watt denominator (sin 2). Three questions, four sins, and you've converted a marketing number into either a defensible claim or an exposed one — which is the whole skill of reading a benchmark.","url":"track-d/lesson-08.html","title":"D8 Honest Cross-Vendor Benchmarking — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-178","front":"Your team ports a kernel to a competitor's GPU, measures it 40% slower in wall time, and the lead drafts an email: \"Vendor B's hardware is 40% slower for our workload.\" Before sending, what single measurement could overturn this conclusion, and why?","back":"The % of each GPU's own roofline. If your kernel hits 85% of GPU A's ceiling but only 45% of GPU B's, the 40% wall-time gap is mostly YOUR under-tuned port on B (the home-turf sin, D2), not B's hardware — a re-tune could close much of it. Wall time conflates 'the chip is slower' with 'our code is worse on this chip'; the roofline fraction separates them. Sending 'B is 40% slower' without that measurement risks a false hardware conclusion drawn from your own porting effort — Option 4 conflates the economics question (valid but separate) with the hardware-speed claim being made. Option 3's clock speed doesn't explain utilization gaps (a kernel can be slow on a fast-clocked chip if it's uncoalesced). Option 1 is the exact error the lesson forbids — wall time smears chip-speed and code-quality together. The correct answer is the roofline fraction (4.7): it's the one measurement that says whether the 40% is the hardware or your port. This is D2's \"a slow first port measures your porting, not their hardware\" as a benchmarking discipline — never publish a hardware verdict earned by an un-tuned kernel.","url":"track-d/lesson-08.html","title":"D8 Honest Cross-Vendor Benchmarking — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-179","front":"You've built the four-backend reduction and all four pass your fixture. You ran the fixture on NVIDIA (CUDA), your laptop CPU (SYCL/Kokkos), and in Chrome (WGSL), but the HIP build you only compiled — no AMD hardware handy — and it built cleanly. Is your correctness claim complete?","back":"No — the HIP backend's fixture has not EXECUTED on a 64-lane wavefront, which is exactly where the reduction's most likely bug lives (a hard-coded warp-size-32 shuffle tree returns the sum of only the first 32 of 64 lanes — D2, and it compiles clean). A clean build certifies syntax, not the wavefront-width correctness. The claim is complete only when the fixture runs on actual AMD hardware (or a cloud AMD runner); until then HIP is unverified, and 'the other three pass' says nothing about the 64-lane path — Option 4 is the exact false confidence D2 exists to kill — HIP shares CUDA's SOURCE, but AMD's wavefront is 64, so the runtime behavior of a warp-width-dependent kernel differs. Option 1 generalizes from the wrong backends (the CPU and browser reductions don't have a 64-lane wavefront to test). The correct answer is the capstone's central trust rule: the fixture certifies the machine it ran on, so a backend you only compiled is unverified — and the reduction is precisely the kernel where the wavefront bug hides. Complete the claim by executing HIP's fixture on AMD, or label it \"built, not verified\" honestly in the report.","url":"track-d/lesson-09.html","title":"D9 Capstone — One Algorithm, Four Backends — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"},{"id":"q-180","front":"An interviewer looks at your capstone and asks: \"You wrote the same reduction four times — isn't that just busywork? What am I supposed to learn about you from this?\" What's the strong answer?","back":"\"The four reductions are the setup; the deliverable is the report. It shows I can take one algorithm across four vendor ecosystems, hold all four to a single shared fixture that I ran on each backend's actual hardware, catch the cross-vendor bug that compiles clean (the wavefront-64 reduction trap), report performance as each device's own-roofline fraction instead of a misleading wall-time race, and account honestly for what the last 10% cost on each rung. That's judgment across vendors — knowing which backend to reach for under which constraint — which is the skill that's rare and doesn't come from writing any single kernel\" — Options 1, 3, and 5 sell the low-value interpretation (syntax knowledge, mechanical porting) that the interviewer's question is baiting. Option 4 misframes it as a hardware benchmark (and a naive one — D8 forbids \"which vendor is fastest\"). The strong answer reframes the artifact: the kernels are scaffolding; the report — shared fixtures executed per backend, the caught wavefront bug, own-roofline performance, honest 90/10 accounting, and the judgment of which rung fits which constraint — is the point. That reframe (from \"wrote it four times\" to \"demonstrated cross-vendor judgment with evidence\") is itself the hire signal.","url":"track-d/lesson-09.html","title":"D9 Capstone — One Algorithm, Four Backends — GPU Mastery","tag":"quiz"}];
